
window.DOT_I18N={"en":{"brand":"Brand","copyLink":"Copy link","copied":"Copied","heroTitle":"Connecting the Dots","heroBody":"Why does a Caribbean island have a name associated with artificial intelligence? Who can give a city its own place on the Internet? Discover the stories, money and identities behind .com, .ai and other Internet names.","tabOverview":"Overview","tabArchaeology":"History","tabHow":"How it works","tabCities":"Cities & territories","tabContention":"Contention","tabStrange":"Strange Internet","tabReveal":"Round 2026","tabPress":"Data & sources","tabSources":"Methodology","applicationsRound":"Applications by round","applicationsRoundSub":"Submitted applications by round. In 2026, 1,663 were submitted; 1,615 applications are active in the Reveal Day snapshot.","icann2012":"2012: what ICANN received","icann2012Sub":"These are separate official classifications, not slices of one pie.","overviewCallout":"Who gets a place in the root, who gets to decide, what is a dot worth, and who belongs after it?","overviewFoot":"Official ICANN and IANA fields remain separate from editorial interpretation. The archive follows evidence across time without turning outcomes into inevitabilities.","archTitle":"The archaeology of the dot","archSub":"Follow what was imagined, what was formally applied for, what reached the root, and how names later moved, changed operator, were retired or became disputed.","timeCapsule":"2000 already looks like a time capsule","timeCapsuleSub":"A curated selection from the proof-of-concept round.","string":"String","theme":"Theme","status":"Status","why":"Why it matters","scopeGeneral":"General principles","scopeGeneralSub":"How the DNS, TLD roles and the new-gTLD process work, independently of .lugano.","scopeLugano":".lugano · applied case","scopeLuganoSub":"What those general rules mean for Lugano’s 2026 application.","luganoAppliedTitle":"From the general rule to .lugano","luganoAppliedBody":"The .lugano application follows the same process described above. Until evaluation, contracting and delegation are completed, .lugano remains an application and is not an active TLD.","luganoExampleLabel":"Hypothetical example","luganoExampleBody":"If .lugano is delegated, the same DNS structure could produce addresses such as servizi.lugano or www.servizi.lugano. This illustrates the hierarchy; it is not a live service.","luganoLanguageGuide":"Speaking precisely about .lugano","luganoLanguageGuideSub":"Two distinctions that matter while the application is still in process.","howTitle":"How Internet domains actually work","howSub":"The essential concepts for navigating Internet domains, DNS and Internet governance.","anatomyTitle":"Start with one address","anatomyBody":"In <b>www.example.com</b>, <b>.com</b> is the top-level domain (TLD). “example” is the second-level label; the name registered under .com is <b>example.com</b>. “www” is an additional label identifying a subdomain.","rootLabel":"Root","rootBody":"The invisible top of the DNS hierarchy.","tldLabel":"Top-level domain","tldBody":"The final part: .ch, .com, .org.","secondLabel":"Second-level label","secondBody":"Here, “example” is the label immediately to the left of .com. Together they form the domain name example.com.","hostLabel":"Optional label","hostBody":"An optional label added in front of the registered name. In www.example.com, “www” can identify a specific site or service; it is not part of the TLD.","whoTitle":"Who does what?","whoSub":"The Internet has no single “owner”. It works through distinct roles, contracts and technical coordination.","roleIcann":"ICANN","roleIcannBody":"Coordinates policies and contracts for generic top-level domains through a global multistakeholder model.","roleIana":"IANA / PTI","roleIanaBody":"Acts as Root Zone Manager: PTI receives and validates TLD change requests and manages the authoritative delegation data for the DNS root.","roleRegistry":"Registry operator","roleRegistryBody":"Operates a TLD and its authoritative registry database. gTLD operators work under ICANN registry agreements; ccTLD governance arrangements differ.","roleRsp":"Registry Service Provider","roleRspBody":"Provides critical technical registry services such as DNS and registration infrastructure for the registry operator.","roleRegistrar":"Registrar","roleRegistrarBody":"For gTLDs, an ICANN-accredited registrar provides the direct registration channel to registrants. ccTLDs use their own registry and registrar models.","roleRegistrant":"Registrant","roleRegistrantBody":"The person or organization that registers and uses a domain name under the TLD.","birthTitle":"How a new TLD is born","birthSub":"The 2026 process is not “apply and receive”. It is a staged public process.","step1":"Application","step1b":"Applicant files the application and pays the required fee.","step2":"Reveal","step2b":"ICANN publishes public application data and identifies identical-string contention.","step3":"Input & objections","step3b":"Community input, formal objections and relevant government advice can enter the process.","step4":"Evaluation","step4b":"String, applicant, operational, financial and technical components are evaluated.","step5":"Contention","step5b":"If multiple applicants seek the same or confusingly similar string, the contention must be resolved.","step6":"Contract","step6b":"A successful applicant signs the Base Registry Agreement with ICANN.","step7":"Delegation","step7b":"After onboarding and technical checks, IANA/PTI processes the delegation request and the Root Zone Maintainer implements the validated change in the DNS root.","step8":"Launch","step8b":"Only then can the registry move toward actually using and allocating names under the TLD.","controlTitle":"For .lugano: what the registry could govern, and what it would not mean","canTitle":"What the registry could govern","can1":"Eligibility and allocation policies for names under .lugano, within ICANN rules and applicable law.","can2":"Reserved names, launch phases and local governance choices.","can3":"Registration-policy and security requirements for names operating under .lugano.","cannotTitle":"What it would not mean","cannot1":"Lugano does not “own the Internet” or control .ch, .com or other TLDs.","cannot2":"An application is not an assignment: .lugano is not active until the process, contract and delegation are complete.","cannot3":"ICANN does not operate the City’s websites; it coordinates the global naming system and the gTLD contractual framework.","sourceGuide":"Official explainer","cityTitle":"From place name to public infrastructure","citySub":"The city-TLD idea predates the 2012 round by more than a decade.","geoLayer":"2012 geographic layer","geoApps":"applications self-identified as geographic","geoDelegated":"geographic gTLDs delegated from the 2012 round in ICANN statistics","secondGen":"A second generation of city TLDs?","secondGenBody":"The first wave was largely about identity, locality and registration. A 2026 city namespace could also be designed around trusted public services, verifiable identity, machine-readable governance and interaction with AI agents. That is an editorial hypothesis, not an ICANN classification.","contentionTitle":"What everyone wanted in 2012","contentionSub":"Contention is a surprisingly good snapshot of the app, platform and e-commerce era.","strangeTitle":"The strange Internet","strangeSub":"Not just odd names. These are small fossils of what each era thought deserved its own piece of the Internet.","searchPlaceholder":"Search .wtf, mobile, identity, trust…","allRounds":"All programs / rounds","crossRound":"Cross-round","allCategories":"All categories","reset":"Reset","searchHint":"Search a TLD, a social theme or an idea.","results":"results","noResults":"No matches. Even the Internet occasionally runs out of weirdness.","source":"Source","sourcesN":"Sources","whatItWas":"What it was","whatItSays":"What it says about the era","strangeNext":"<b>How to read this section:</b> an application or delegation is a FACT; counts derived from the published corpus are marked DERIVED; the cultural or technological meaning is a READING. A 2026 application is not yet a TLD.","official2026":"2026 official state","paidApps":"paid applications proceeding","revealBody":"ICANN published the 2026 Round applications on 7 October 2026, including primary strings, applicable replacement and variant strings, public application data and preliminary identical-string contention sets.","preRevealIntel":"Reveal Day data","secondaryNote":"Secondary source retained for historical context. Current 2026 application facts use ICANN’s Reveal Day publication and APS records.","importPlan":"Post-Reveal data ingest","pressTitle":"Data, facts & reusable resources","pressSub":"Verified facts, definitions and reusable resources for understanding the data without overstating what it means.","safeFacts":"Five key facts","copyFact":"Copy","languageGuide":"Precise words, clear concepts","languageGuideSub":"The essential concepts for navigating domains, DNS and Internet governance.","sayThis":"More precise","notThis":"Not the same thing","safe1":"The City of Lugano has applied for the new top-level domain .lugano.","avoid1":"Lugano has obtained / owns the .lugano domain.","safe2":"A top-level domain is the final part of an Internet address, such as .ch or .com.","avoid2":"A TLD is simply a new website address.","safe3":"ICANN coordinates the global domain-name system and the new-gTLD process.","avoid3":"ICANN is the government or owner of the Internet.","safe4":"If successful, .lugano could become a governed namespace under rules defined by its registry operator within the ICANN framework.","avoid4":"Anyone will automatically be able to register any .lugano name.","downloadsTitle":"Download the working data","downloadsSub":"Download the underlying datasets for verification, reuse or further analysis.","downloadStrange":"Strange Internet dataset","downloadRounds":"Round summary","downloadContention":"Contention · 2012 + 2026","downloadGlossary":"DNS & governance glossary","downloadFacts":"Key facts","downloadPack":"Download the current complete site data pack (v{version})","anglesTitle":"Questions raised by the data","angle1":"How the language of TLD applications mirrors the society of each era.","angle2":"From the delegated .berlin domain to the .lugano application: cities and the DNS root.","angle3":"What disputed strings reveal about trademarks, geography, communities, governments and ICANN accountability.","angle4":"What the 2026 contention sets reveal about AI, agents, finance, brands and identity.","angle5":"Can a city TLD evolve from digital branding into trust infrastructure?","sourcesTitle":"Sources & methodology","sourcesSub":"Primary ICANN and IANA sources are preferred. Secondary or archival mirrors are explicitly labelled, and governance cases link back to the underlying decisions and records.","guardrails":"Methodological guardrails","primary":"Primary","secondary":"Secondary","footerRight":"Static, local-first and ready for public hosting.","applications":"applications","applicants":"applicants","geographic":"Geographic","community":"Community","idn":"IDN","round2000":"Proof of concept","round2004":"Sponsored TLD round","round2012":"Mass expansion","round2026":"2026 round","tl1984Title":"Before .com, there was .arpa","tl1984Body":"RFC 920 described .arpa as the initial, transitional top-level domain for the ARPA-Internet while the DNS was being introduced. It was supposed to disappear; instead it survived and today serves Internet infrastructure.","tl1985Title":"The first .com: symbolics.com","tl1985Body":"In January 1985, .com, .edu, .gov, .mil, .net and .org joined .arpa in the root. On 15 March, symbolics.com became the first registered .com domain.","tl1999Title":"The city idea appears","tl1999Body":"The .berlin concept is traced back to 1999.","tl2000Title":"Proof of concept","tl2000Body":"47 applications. Even .nyc appears, then withdraws.","tl2004Title":"Sponsored communities","tl2004Body":"10 applications for 9 strings: .asia, .cat, .jobs, .mail, .mobi, .post, .tel, .travel and .xxx.","tl2012Title":"The explosion","tl2012Body":"1,930 applications for 1,409 distinct strings, including 66 geographic applications.","tl2014Title":"Cities go live","tl2014Body":".berlin opens to the public; .nyc and .paris are delegated.","tl2026Title":"The next layer","tl2026Body":"1,615 applications are active at Reveal Day. ICANN has now published the applying entities and strings, including the official .lugano record.","city":"City","children":"Children","health":"Health","labour":"Labour","publicInterest":"Public interest","techFossil":"Tech fossil","geography":"Geography","culture":"Culture","identity":"Identity","publicInfrastructure":"Public infrastructure","oddity":"Oddity","socialMirror":"Social mirror","optimism":"Optimism","trust":"Trust","adult":"Adult","personalBrand":"Personal brand","statusCulture":"Status culture","intimacy":"Intimacy","politics":"Politics","faith":"Faith","lifestyle":"Lifestyle","protest":"Protest","withdrawn":"Withdrawn","returned":"Returned","evaluated":"Evaluated","selected":"Selected","proposed":"Proposed","delegated":"Delegated","notDelegated":"Not delegated","longitudinal":"Proposed → delegated","cNyc":"A city-TLD application already existed in 2000.","cKids":"Several applicants wanted a dedicated namespace for children.","cHealth":"The World Health Organization proposed a dedicated health namespace.","cUnion":"The international trade-union movement applied for .union.","cWap":"A pure fossil from the WAP/mobile-web era.","cGeo":"A geographic namespace concept well before the 2012 city wave.","cMuseum":"One of the seven selected proof-of-concept TLDs.","cName":"Personal identity imagined directly in the namespace.","cPost":"Public-service infrastructure; the postal sector returned with .post in 2004.","sKids":"Multiple applicants proposed .kids in 2000; one application also paired it with .xxx.","iKids":"The early Web was already asking whether children needed a distinct, governed space rather than merely filtered content.","sHealth":"WHO proposed a dedicated .health namespace.","iHealth":"Long before today’s arguments about online misinformation, public-health actors were already thinking about trust at the naming layer.","sUnion":"The International Confederation of Free Trade Unions applied for .union.","iUnion":"Industrial-era collective institutions were actively looking for a native place in the new digital public sphere.","sNyc":"A .nyc application existed in 2000 and was later withdrawn.","iNyc":"The desire for city identity online appeared before ICANN had a mature framework for city TLDs.","sSucks":"Name.Space proposed .sucks in 2000; a .sucks gTLD was eventually delegated in 2015.","iSucks":"A provocative idea moved from fringe proposal to production infrastructure. The boundary of what “belongs” in the root expanded dramatically.","sWar":".war appeared in the huge Name.Space bundle of proposed strings.","iWar":"The proposal treated the DNS almost as a taxonomy of human life, including its darkest categories.","sSoup":"Yes: .soup was proposed.","iSoup":"The early expansion debate sometimes assumed that almost any semantic category could deserve a top-level namespace.","sMagic":".magic was proposed in 2000.","iMagic":"Early Internet naming still carried a distinctly utopian belief that broad concepts could become self-contained digital places.","sMobi":".mobi was proposed in the 2004 sponsored round and later delegated.","iMobi":"Before responsive design and smartphones normalized one Web, mobile looked different enough to deserve its own namespace.","sMail":".mail was proposed as a sponsored namespace but was not delegated.","iMail":"Spam and trust were already serious enough problems that some actors tried to solve them at the naming layer.","sCat":".cat was created for the Catalan linguistic and cultural community.","iCat":"A TLD did not need to map neatly onto a sovereign state: culture and language could also become infrastructure.","sPost":".post came from the Universal Postal Union.","iPost":"One of the oldest global communications infrastructures tried to map itself into the architecture of the Internet.","sXxx":".xxx became one of the most contentious sponsored-TLD stories.","iXxx":"Naming decisions are not merely technical; they can become proxies for wider political, cultural and regulatory conflicts.","sGuru":".guru was delegated from the 2012 round.","iGuru":"The 2010s turned expertise itself into a marketable online identity.","sNinja":".ninja was delegated from the 2012 round.","iNinja":"The playful job-title vocabulary of startup and developer culture reached the DNS root.","sRich":".rich was delegated from the 2012 round.","iRich":"Status and exclusivity became explicit identity categories that could be bought and signalled online.","sDating":".dating was delegated from the 2012 round.","iDating":"As platforms specialized, intimate life also became a commercial vertical in the namespace.","sGop":".gop was delegated to a US political organization.","iGop":"Political identity itself became a branded namespace, showing how far the 2012 round pushed specialization.","sLgbt":".lgbt was delegated from the 2012 round.","iLgbt":"Community and identity could be represented directly at the top level of the naming system.","sBible":".bible was delegated from the 2012 round.","iBible":"Faith communities joined brands, places and industries in claiming explicit space in the root.","sBeer":".beer was delegated from the 2012 round.","iBeer":"Products were no longer only things sold online; they became lifestyle categories with their own namespaces.","sPizza":".pizza was delegated from the 2012 round.","iPizza":"Everyday consumption became part of the DNS vocabulary, from sectors and brands down to food culture.","sWtf":".wtf was delegated from the 2012 round.","iWtf":"Internet slang itself became infrastructure, perhaps the most concise proof that the namespace had become culturally mainstream.","sFail":".fail was delegated from the 2012 round.","iFail":"Even failure became a viable semantic category in the DNS root. The namespace had moved far beyond institutional labels.","pf2026":"Applications active in ICANN’s official 2026 Reveal Day snapshot.","pf2012apps":"Applications received in the 2012 new-gTLD round.","pfGeo":"2012 applications identified as geographic in ICANN statistics.","pfYears":"Years between the 2012 round and the new 2026 application round.","pfBerlin":".berlin became the first city TLD to open registrations to the general public in 2014.","plan1":"Capture the official APS dataset.","plan2":"Normalize primary, replacement and variant strings.","plan3":"Build contention sets and applicant-level counts.","plan4":"Classify geography/cities, brands, communities, AI/agent, identity/trust, finance/crypto and other editorial themes.","plan5":"Compare with 2000, 2004 and 2012.","plan6":"Generate LinkedIn-ready facts and downloadable data exports.","guard1":"“Geographic” is an ICANN application classification; “city TLD” is a narrower analytical classification used in this publication.","guard2":"Application counts, unique-string counts, registry portfolios, RSP-managed TLDs and delegated-TLD counts are different metrics and are never blended.","guard3":"The 2026 application layer is post-Reveal. Official aggregate metrics and preliminary contention data are preserved locally. The Explorer integrates a locally frozen secondary snapshot of the Reveal Day primary-string inventory; a complete local APS corpus at individual-application level is not claimed. Strings and contention sets may still change during the Replacement Period before String Confirmation Day.","guard4":"“Zeitgeist” themes and the five-layer power model are analytical classifications and remain separate from official ICANN fields.","tabBeyond":"TLDs as infrastructure","tabGeo":"DNS geography","tabLugano":".lugano","beyondTitle":"When a TLD becomes infrastructure","beyondSub":"Some namespaces went far beyond naming. They added eligibility, verification, security rules, public-interest governance or shared digital services.","maturityTitle":"From a name to a trust layer","maturitySub":"The interesting shift is not the number of domains. It is how much meaning and verification the namespace can carry.","m1":"Name","m1b":"A memorable address.","m2":"Qualified community","m2b":"Rules define who belongs.","m3":"Verified identity","m3b":"Eligibility is checked.","m4":"Security baseline","m4b":"Technical requirements become part of the namespace.","m5":"Shared services","m5b":"The TLD becomes a platform for common services.","stageShared":"Shared services","stageCivic":"Civic infrastructure","stageVerified":"Verified trust","stagePublicId":"Verified public identity","stageQualification":"External qualification","stageCommitment":"Profile + commitment","stageQualified":"Qualified namespace","casePostTitle":"A TLD with a service layer","casePostBody":"The UPU vets registrants, mandates a cybersecurity framework and operates trust.post, a Shared Services Platform for the postal and logistics sector.","caseCatTitle":"A domain that funds a digital commons","caseCatBody":"The .cat registry reinvests its revenues in digital rights, tools and projects for the Catalan-speaking community, describing the wider mission as civic infrastructure.","caseBankTitle":"Security rules encoded into membership","caseBankBody":"Resolving .bank and .insurance domains must implement requirements such as DNSSEC, TLS and email authentication. The suffix is designed to signal a higher-trust environment.","caseGovTitle":"The suffix itself is an institutional credential","caseGovBody":"Only verified U.S. government organizations can register .gov. CISA verifies eligibility and the identity of requesters before approval.","casePharmacyTitle":"A domain tied to accreditation","casePharmacyBody":"A registrant must first demonstrate compliance with NABP standards and receive accreditation. The domain is connected to an external qualification process.","caseEcoTitle":"Activation requires a profile and a pledge","caseEcoBody":"A .eco registration remains on hold until the registrant affirms eligibility, completes an Eco Profile and makes a public environmental commitment.","caseSwissTitle":"A namespace reserved for a defined community","caseSwissBody":".swiss is restricted to the Swiss community and applies eligibility rules linking the registrant and the requested name to Switzerland.","sourceCase":"Primary source","geoTitle":"The geography of power in the DNS","geoSub":"Rules, registry contracts, technical infrastructure and applicant demand have different geographies. Explore each layer separately to understand where decisions and capabilities concentrate.","geoPlaces":"Places represented","geoGovernance":"Coordination & root","geoRsp":"Evaluated RSP supply · 2026","geoPlacesNote":"Curated examples of city and territorial TLDs. This layer shows what a namespace represents, not a complete census and not where its technical backend is located.","geoGovernanceNote":"Selected organizational locations for ICANN, PTI/IANA and the Root Zone Maintainer. ICANN policy is a global multistakeholder process; PTI validates root changes and Verisign implements them. Headquarters are not a map of where root service or Internet traffic physically runs.","geoRspNote":"Evaluated RSP organizations by ICANN region as of 31 August 2026: Europe 16, Asia-Pacific 7, North America 6, Africa 0, Latin America & Caribbean 0. This is the geography of pre-evaluated technical supply. Applicants’ selected RSPs are not publicly posted in the 2026 application data (Question 176), so this is not applicant market share.","registryGeoTitle":"Where do TLDs “reside”?","registryGeoBody":"The useful answer has three layers: the place a TLD represents, the legal home of its registry operator, and the distributed technical backend. IANA records the TLD manager for every delegation. A full manager-country aggregation will be added with the 2026 dataset rather than inferred from brand names or server locations.","powerTitle":"Where is DNS power concentrated?","powerBody":"Policy, root-zone management, maintenance, registry operations and technical backends are different roles. Mapping them separately reveals concentration without pretending that one organization “owns the Internet”.","rootStat1":"12 independent root-server operators","rootStat2":"13 logical root-server identities (A–M)","rootStat3":"2,045 operational root-server instances as of 12 Sep 2026","rspStat":"29 evaluated RSP organizations in August 2026","mapHint":"Select a layer to explore the map. Hover or tap a marker for context.","nodeLA":"ICANN headquarters and Public Technical Identifiers (IANA functions).","nodeReston":"Verisign headquarters; Verisign performs Root Zone Maintainer services.","nodeRegional":"ICANN regional office.","nodeEngagement":"ICANN engagement center.","luganoTitle":".lugano — from a name to a trust layer","luganoSub":"A vision for what the namespace could become if the application succeeds. This is direction, not an announcement of services already implemented.","visionBadge":"VISION · NOT YET IMPLEMENTED","luganoLead":"The ambition is not simply to put Lugano after a dot. It is to create a public-interest namespace in which trust can become visible, governable and eventually machine-readable.","lugPillar1":"Public stewardship","lugPillar1b":"Public responsibilities, access criteria and review procedures should be identifiable in the published rules.","lugPillar2":"Qualified participation","lugPillar2b":"Different classes of users or services could carry explicit eligibility and accountability rules.","lugPillar3":"Security & interoperability","lugPillar3b":"Minimum technical requirements and open interfaces can make trust operational rather than merely symbolic.","lugPillar4":"Machine-readable trust","lugPillar4b":"In the longer term, software and AI agents could verify who they are interacting with, what a service is allowed to do and which rules apply.","lugRoadmap":"An illustrative progression","lugRoadmapSub":"Each step would require its own governance, legal and technical decisions after a successful ICANN process.","lugStage1":"Secure registry foundation","lugStage1b":"Registry policies, reserved names, institutional domains, abuse handling and operational continuity.","lugStage2":"Verified ecosystem","lugStage2b":"Qualified organizations, services and local actors with clear conditions of use and transparent responsibilities.","lugStage3":"Digital commons layer","lugStage3b":"Shared technical components, metadata and interoperable services that reduce friction for the local ecosystem.","lugStage4":"Agent-ready trust","lugStage4b":"Machine-readable identity, delegation and service discovery so intelligent systems can evaluate trust as well as humans do.","luganoGuard":"Nothing in this section implies that ICANN has assigned .lugano or that these services already exist. It describes a possible design direction, subject to the application outcome and future decisions by the City.","geoSourceLine":"Method: policy, root coordination, contracts, technical backend and demand are separate layers. Geographic concentration is evidence of asymmetry, not proof that one place or company “controls the Internet”.","beyondQuote":"Some of the clearest examples show that a TLD can encode more than a name: eligibility, identity, security, community rules and shared services.","eyebrow":"INTERNET NAMES · PEOPLE · POWER","fact":"FACT","reading":"READING","vision":"VISION","legendText":"official/source facts · derived measures · editorial interpretation · future scenario","delegationRecords":"delegation records · 2012 round","delegationCaveat":"ICANN reports 1,241 delegations from the 2012 round; this total is not adjusted for TLDs later terminated or removed from the root zone.","source2012":"ICANN 2012 statistics","sourceReveal":"ICANN Reveal Day","mobileNavigate":"Section","roundAtGlance":"Rounds at a glance","roundAtGlanceSub":"Four openings, radically different scales. Values are application counts, not the number of TLDs ultimately delegated.","applicantRegion2012":"Applicant demand · 2012","applicantRegion2012Note":"Official ICANN regional distribution of the 1,930 applications, based on the applicant’s principal place of business. This is not where a TLD “lives”.","rspEvaluated":"evaluated RSPs","knownReveal":"What Reveal Day has shown","unknownReveal":"What it will not tell us yet","knownRevealSub":"On 7 October ICANN published the applied-for primary strings, applicable variants or replacement strings, preliminary identical-string contention sets and the public portions of applications.","unknownRevealSub":"Reveal is the beginning of evaluation, not the assignment of a TLD.","known1":"Applied-for primary strings and the applicants behind them.","known2":"Applicable variant or replacement strings.","known3":"Preliminary contention sets for identical strings.","known4":"Public portions of each application.","unknown1":"Which applications will pass all evaluations.","unknown2":"Who will prevail in unresolved contention sets.","unknown3":"When a successful TLD will be delegated or launch.","unknown4":"That .lugano has been assigned: it remains an application until the process is completed.","revealLocal":"Reveal Day: 7 October 2026 · 18:00 UTC · 20:00 in Lugano","curatedSample":"curated sample","excelSub":"XLSX · publication-ready tables + sources","dataPackSub":"Versioned ZIP · full current public CSVs, official ICANN APS listing, manifests and provenance","footerLeftV05":"ICANN Reveal Day data · 7 October 2026 · updates and source dates in Sources · 1984–2026","footerRightV05":"Primary sources: ICANN, IANA and cited registry / governance sources","roleTagPolicy":"POLICY","roleTagRoot":"ROOT","roleTagGovernance":"GOVERNANCE","roleTagTech":"TECH","roleTagRetail":"RETAIL","roleTagUser":"USER","searchLabel":"Search Strange Internet","roundFilterLabel":"ICANN program / round","categoryFilterLabel":"Filter by category","mapLabel":"Map layer","methodLegendTitle":"How to read this publication","methodLegendBody":"FACT marks information supported by a cited source. READING marks an interpretation of data, history or institutional significance. VISION marks a proposed future direction. Systematic life-history evidence and editorially selected governance cases are kept distinct.","downloadGeoRegions":"2012 applicant geography","downloadGeoRegionsSub":"CSV · official ICANN regional totals","beyondDownload":"Beyond the dot","rspDownload":"RSP geography","secondarySource":"SECONDARY SOURCE","statusApplication2026":"2026 APPLICATION","statusNotImplemented":"NOT YET IMPLEMENTED","luganoStatusTitle":"Status at the documented check","luganoStatusBody":"The City of Lugano has submitted an application for .lugano in the ICANN 2026 round. An application is not an assignment or a delegation: .lugano becomes active only after the applicable evaluation, contracting and root-zone delegation steps.","operator":"Operator source","awaitReveal":"NOT AVAILABLE IN THE OFFICIAL AGGREGATE","awaitRevealShort":"Not available in the ICANN aggregate","afterReveal":"2026 ANALYSIS","provisionalUntilConfirmation":"PROVISIONAL UNTIL 17 NOV","homeUnique2026":"unique primary strings · 2026","homeApplicants2026":"applicant organizations · 2026","homeContention2026":"preliminary identical-string contention sets · 2026","homeBenchmark2012":"2012 benchmark: 1,930 applications · 1,409 distinct strings · 230 exact-match contention sets · 66 geographic applications.","round2026Title":"The 2026 round","round2026Sub":"The official ICANN Reveal Day snapshot of 7 October 2026 is shown separately from the derived secondary inventory. A blank official aggregate is not estimated, and future confirmation is not treated as an outcome.","round2026Snapshot":"Reveal Day snapshot · 7 October 2026","round2026Unique":"unique primary strings","round2026Applicants":"applicant organizations","round2026Contention":"preliminary identical-string contention sets","round2026Geo":"geographic applications","round2026Idn":"IDN applications","dataStatusTitle":"Reveal Day is the first public snapshot, not the final string list","dataStatusSub":"ICANN published the initial Reveal Day snapshot on 7 October. The Replacement Period ran from 8 to 21 October; the confirmed string list and updated contention sets are scheduled for 17 November.","milestoneReveal":"7 OCT · REVEAL DAY","milestoneRevealBody":"Applicants, primary strings, applicable variants/replacements, preliminary identical-string contention sets and public application data.","milestoneReplacement":"8–21 OCT · REPLACEMENT PERIOD","milestoneReplacementBody":"Eligible applicants may switch from their primary string to a replacement string.","milestoneConfirm":"17 NOV · STRING CONFIRMATION","milestoneConfirmBody":"Final applied-for string list and updated contention sets.","round2026Patterns":"What Reveal Day already shows","round2026PatternsSub":"The list is still provisional until 17 November, but several signals are already strong enough to describe without pretending that the round is finished.","patternGeo":"Demand is still geographically asymmetric","patternGeoBody":"Of the 1,615 Reveal Day applications, 864 come from North America and 506 from Europe, compared with 218 from Asia-Pacific, 16 from Africa and 11 from Latin America and the Caribbean. This is application geography, not Internet-user geography.","patternApplicants":"The round is also a portfolio game","patternApplicantsBody":"ICANN reports 333 .Brand applications. A secondary analysis of the public application records also shows several registry specialists filing dozens or more applications, so the round is not simply 481 organizations making one bet each.","patternWords":"AI and agents are the new vocabulary","patternWordsBody":"In the public APS corpus for Reveal Day, 7 October 2026, .agent has 13 applications, .api 10, .robot 9 and .agi 7. OpenAI appears with .openai and .chatgpt. These cases show AI and automation vocabulary in the 2026 applications. They do not, by themselves, measure an overall shift relative to 2012.","patternCompetition":"Scarcity did not disappear","patternCompetitionBody":"Reveal Day produced 263 preliminary identical-string contention sets involving 891 application records. Replacement strings can remove or create pressure, while later singular/plural, string-confusion and similarity processes can still alter contention.","geoLayer2026":"Geographic layer · 2026","geoApps2026":"geographic applications in the 2026 round","geoApps2026Note":"ICANN reports 15 self-identified geographic applications in the 7 October 2026 Reveal Day snapshot.","geoCities2026":"city / territorial strings in 2026","geoCities2026Note":"A broader city/territorial classification would be an editorial layer, not an ICANN metric. It is therefore kept separate from the official count of 15 self-identified geographic applications.","powerStackTitle":"Power is a stack, not a dot on a map","powerStackSub":"Five layers matter, and they describe different things. Collapsing them into one “center of the Internet” would be neat, dramatic and wrong.","powerRules":"Rules","powerRulesBody":"ICANN’s global multistakeholder process develops policy; ICANN org implements the contractual and program framework for gTLDs.","powerRoot":"Root coordination","powerRootBody":"PTI validates root-zone change requests; Verisign implements validated changes as Root Zone Maintainer. Root serving itself is distributed among independent operators worldwide.","powerContracts":"Registry contracts","powerContractsBody":"Each gTLD registry operator holds contractual responsibility for its namespace. Some operators hold large portfolios of registry agreements.","powerBackend":"Technical backend","powerBackendBody":"Registry Service Providers supply registry, DNS and DNSSEC infrastructure across many TLDs. Technical concentration can differ sharply from ownership.","powerDemand":"Demand","powerDemandBody":"Applicants decide which strings are worth pursuing. Their public business locations reveal where commercial, civic and institutional demand originates.","geoMapTitle":"Mapping and measuring power","geoMapSub":"Switch layers to see institutional coordination, evaluated technical supply and applicant demand. They overlap, but they are not the same kind of power.","applicantRegion2026":"Applicant organizations by ICANN region · 2026","applicantRegion2026Note":"This view counts the 481 organizations in the official APS aggregate without multiplying them by their applications. Declared region does not identify DNS infrastructure, ultimate ownership or market served. The application comparison uses separate counts.","demand2012Title":"Applicant demand · 2012","demand2012Sub":"ICANN recorded 1,930 applications from 60 countries and territories. North America and Europe together accounted for 82.2% of them.","demand2012Concentration":"82.2% · North America + Europe","rspSupplyTitle":"Technical supply · evaluated RSPs","rspSupplySub":"By August 2026, 29 organizations had cleared at least one RSP evaluation. Europe alone accounted for 16, or 55.2%.","rspEuropeShare":"55.2% · Europe","demand2026Title":"Applications by region · 2026","demand2026Sub":"1,615 applications at ICANN Reveal Day, 7 October 2026. The same unit as 2012: an application, not an organization. Applicant region does not establish ultimate ownership, DNS-serving location or market served.","topCountries2026Title":"Top applicant countries · 2026","topCountries2026Sub":"The ICANN APS aggregate does not include an official ranking of applicant countries. No third-party country-level normalization is presented as an ICANN statistic.","identityTitle":"Who built the broadest TLD portfolio? Donuts → Identity Digital","identitySub":"Donuts filed 307 applications through subsidiaries in 2012. The business expanded through acquisitions and now operates as Identity Digital. A member biography on the ICANN SSAC website describes it as the world’s largest TLD registry operator; this is not an independently measured market-share ranking.","identityApps":"applications filed by Donuts in 2012","identityPortfolio":"TLDs in Identity Digital’s current portfolio","identityManaged":"TLDs Identity Digital says it manages technically","identityHQ":"headquarters · Bellevue, Washington","identityPortfolioClaim":"Identity Digital describes a portfolio of nearly 300 TLDs. It also says it technically manages 460+ gTLDs, ccTLDs and dotBrands as a Registry Services Provider. Those are different measures: portfolio ownership / operation is not the same as technical backend management.","identityT2012":"2012 · 307 applications","identityT2017":"2017 · Rightside acquired","identityT2020":"2020 · Afilias acquired","identityT2022":"2022 · Identity Digital brand","identityT2026":"2026 · ~300 portfolio TLDs / 460+ managed","identityReadingTitle":"Why does concentration emerge?","identityReadingBody":"Registry operations carry substantial fixed costs in infrastructure, security, compliance and registrar distribution. A broad portfolio lets an operator reuse the same backend and sales channels, spread risk across niche strings and grow further through acquisitions. Donuts explicitly cited economies of scale as a core reason for applying for hundreds of strings in 2012.","identityCaveat":"Applications ≠ awarded TLDs ≠ registry agreements ≠ TLD portfolio ≠ RSP-managed TLDs ≠ Internet governance. The numbers belong to different layers and must not be added together.","identity2026Top":"Largest legal applicant · 2026","identity2026TopSub":"Project-derived count from the official APS export acquired on 11 October 2026: Link Freedom Group Ltd has 158 of the 1,614 published applications. Each base Application ID counts once. This is a legal-applicant ranking, not a corporate-group ranking or an aggregate ranking published by ICANN.","rootDistributedTitle":"The root is coordinated through a small number of institutions, but served globally","rootDistributedBody":"PTI manages root-zone changes and Verisign maintains the root-zone file, while 12 independent root-server operators serve 13 logical root identities through thousands of physical instances worldwide. Institutional coordination and physical delivery therefore have very different geographies.","geoInterpretTitle":"What “Internet power” means here","geoInterpretBody":"The dashboard can show where policy institutions, root functions, registry contracts, applicant headquarters and technical providers cluster. It cannot reduce Internet governance to headquarters, or equate a large TLD portfolio with control over ICANN policy or the DNS root.","contentionTitle2026":"The most contested strings of 2026","contention2026Sub":"ICANN published 263 preliminary identical-string contention sets on Reveal Day, covering 891 application records. The ranking below is a derived view of the public application data and remains provisional until String Confirmation Day.","contention2026Label":"preliminary identical-string contention sets · 2026","contention2012Title":"The 2012 benchmark","contentionCompareTitle":"Same scarcity, different vocabulary","contentionCompareBody":"In 2012, .app led with 13 applications and .home and .inc followed with 11. In the current 2026 public data, .agent leads with 13, followed by .bit with 11 and .api, .brand and .hub with 10. Contention still measures scarcity, but the words reveal what each era thought the next Internet would need.","downloadRound2026":"2026 round data (CSV)","downloadRound2026Sub":"CSV · official 2026 metrics, method and sources","downloadConcentration":"Registry concentration","downloadConcentrationSub":"CSV · Donuts / Identity Digital chronology, metrics and sources","downloadGeo2026":"2026 applicant geography","downloadGeo2026Sub":"CSV · official Reveal Day applicants by ICANN region; not application counts","homeCountries2026":"applicants / ICANN regions · 2026","round2026Countries":"distinct countries and territories (official aggregate)","round2026Community":"community applications","round2026Brand":".Brand applications","identityGap":"In the 2012 round, Donuts filed 307 applications and Google 101. The locally archived Reveal Day list of 13 June 2012 contains 76 records for Amazon EU S.à r.l.","identity2026TopGroup":"Largest corporate group · 2026","identity2026GroupSub":"Corporate-group normalization will use only public parent-company information in ICANN applications and will remain distinct from the legal-applicant ranking. It is not yet published in this snapshot.","downloadRegions2026":"2026 applicant geography (CSV)","downloadRegistryConcentration":"Registry concentration case (CSV)","scaleContrastTitle":"Two different forms of concentration","scaleContrastSub":"Counting TLD strings and counting domains registered underneath them produces two very different pictures of scale.","identityBreadthLabel":"TLDs in Identity Digital’s portfolio · breadth across namespaces","verisignDepthLabel":".com + .net registrations · Q2 2026","verisignInfraLabel":"Root Zone Maintainer · operator of A-root and J-root","scaleContrastReading":"There is no single league table of “Internet power”. Identity Digital concentrates portfolio breadth across many TLDs; Verisign combines exceptional registration depth under .com/.net with important root operational roles. Neither measure is equivalent to control over ICANN policy.","applications2026Download":"2026 verified local application records (CSV)","applications2026DownloadSub":"CSV · partial local APS layer · currently 1 independently verified official record (.lugano); not the full 2026 application corpus","strange2026Pending":"Reveal Day data are public. The Replacement Period runs from 8 to 21 October 2026; publication of the confirmed strings is scheduled for 17 November. The 2026 material is a dated snapshot, not a record of final evaluation outcomes.","tabExplore":"TLD Explorer","exploreTitle":"TLD Explorer","exploreSub":"Search TLDs and applied-for strings, then open sourced profiles. Root-delegated TLDs, historical applications and 2026 proposed strings are different categories: the Explorer does not turn one into the other.","exploreCoverageTitle":"Universal TLD coverage","exploreCoverageBody":"Every entry preserved in the dated IANA database has a technical profile. Documented historical TLDs outside the current IANA database are added separately. Application-only strings remain distinct from delegated TLDs. The 1,849-record core catalog and the frozen 2026 overlay are separate layers; the visible Explorer total is their deduplicated union. The separate official APS public listing now contains 2,783 rows representing 1,614 published applications with 1,169 replacement records; the single pending .wdo is outside that listing.","exploreIana":"Names in the root list","exploreSeed":"Editorially researched records","exploreSearchPlaceholder":"Try .com, .ch, .ai, Lugano, Google…","exploreSearchHint":"Open a result to view the full profile.","exploreRound":"Round","exploreStatus":"Status","exploreType":"Type","exploreTheme":"Theme","allStatuses":"All statuses","allTypes":"All types","allThemes":"All themes","presetAll":"All","preset2026":"2026","presetStrange":"Strange","presetCities":"Cities & places","presetContention":"Contention","presetTrust":"Trust & identity","exploreResults":"records","exploreNoResults":"No results. Try a shorter term or reset the filters.","exploreCorpusNote":"Coverage and limits","exploreCorpusNoteBody":"This is a dated root/IANA snapshot, not a live feed. The 2000, 2004 and 2012 application corpora are complete locally at applied-for-string/applicant level. The 2012 layer covers all 1,930 applications, all 1,409 distinct Reveal-Day strings and all four ICANN-approved post-Reveal string corrections, producing 1,412 application-string lifecycle labels in total. Richer fields from the original 14-column 2012 transport are not all republished, and personal contact/email fields are excluded by design. For 2026, the public ICANN APS Applications CSV is locally archived with 2,783 rows for 1,614 published application IDs (including 1,169 replacement-string records), against 1,615 official applications; .wdo awaits administrative publication. The frozen 980-string secondary inventory used by the Explorer matches exactly the primary-string set derived from the APS listing after excluding the six applications for variants of existing TLDs. Complete individual application questionnaires and PDFs are not locally archived. Selection of replacement strings remains pending String Confirmation, while contention labels are not authoritative until String Evaluation. Alternative roots and special-use names are outside this catalogue. The 1,409 figure is the distinct-string count in the original 13 June 2012 Reveal-Day list. Later ICANN materials sometimes use 1,388 for later active/program-state populations; those figures are not the same snapshot.","exploreColTld":"TLD / string","exploreColStatus":"Status","exploreColRound":"Origin / program","exploreColEntity":"Registry / historical applicant","exploreColGeo":"Represented place / context","exploreColType":"Type","exploreColTheme":"Theme","exploreOpen":"Open record","exploreFactFields":"Documented fields","exploreReadingFields":"Editorial reading","exploreSources":"Sources","exploreClose":"Close","exploreApplicantManager":"Current registry organization","exploreGroup":"Corporate group","exploreGeography":"Represented place","exploreContention":"Competing applications · 2012","exploreContext":"Context","exploreReading":"Interpretation","typeApplication":"Application","typeGeneric":"Generic","typeSponsored":"Sponsored / restricted","typeInfrastructure":"Infrastructure","typeGeographic":"Geographic","typeBrand":"Brand","typeCommunity":"Community","typeCountryCode":"Country-code","legacy":"Legacy / pre-round gTLDs","application":"Application","business":"Business","technology":"Technology","education":"Education","government":"Government","infrastructure":"Infrastructure","ai":"AI & agents","preset":"Preset","strangeExplorerCta":"Explore these and the full namespace","strangeExplorerNote":"Strange Internet is now an editorial lens. Search and filtering live in the Explorer, where these records sit beside the rest of the namespace.","searchLabelExplorer":"Search namespace","statusFilterLabel":"Filter by status","typeFilterLabel":"Filter by type","themeFilterLabel":"Filter by theme","explorePlaceholderBadge":"PROVISIONAL DATASET","exploreIanaDate":"IANA snapshot · {date}","projectByline":"Independent research project by <strong>Robert Bregy</strong>.","projectDisclaimer":"This project does not represent an official position of the City of Lugano.","footerAttribution":"Research, analysis and editorial interpretation by Robert Bregy.","navThemes":"Themes","navRound":"ICANN 2026","navResearch":"Data & method","publicationState":"ICANN DATA · REVEAL DAY","publicationStateNote":"String Confirmation Day","exploreLoadMore":"Load more","exploreShowing":"showing {shown} of {total}","roundAwaitingTitle":"Available on Reveal Day · 7 October 2026","roundAwaitingBody":"The official application dataset will populate unique strings, applicant organizations, countries and territories, contention sets, geographic/community/.Brand applications, and IDN/variant fields.","luganoVisionCondition":"VISION · IF DELEGATED","shareLabel":"Share this view","themeLabel":"Toggle theme","languageLabel":"Language","closeLabel":"Close explorer record","drawerLabel":"Explorer record","mapItemLabel":"Map item: {name}","resourcesNav":"Resources","methodNav":"Methodology & sources","metaDescription":"Connecting the Dots: an independent atlas of Internet TLDs and ICANN applications. Guides, Explorer, stories, the 2026 round, research and verifiable datasets.","metaOgDescription":"A multilingual atlas to understand TLDs through explainers, Explorer, ICANN applications, stories, the 2026 round and scientific sources.","tocLabel":"On this page","homeLabel":"Go to Overview","sectionNavLabel":"Sections","explorerPresetsLabel":"Explorer presets","timelineStageProtocol":"PROTOCOL","timelineStageRoot":"ROOT MILESTONE","timelineStageIdea":"IDEA","timelineStageRound":"APPLICATION ROUND","timelineStageLaunch":"DELEGATION / LAUNCH","roundOverviewNav":"2026 round overview","exploreHistory":"Event history","exploreHistorySub":"Dated applications, registration records and registry changes are separate events. The IANA registration date does not identify the operator at that time. This catalogue is not a complete operator history.","exploreLatestIndexed":"Latest indexed state","exploreProvenance":"Provenance","applicantDisclosure":"Applicant disclosure","applicantDisclosureDate":"Applicant disclosure · 2 Oct 2026","officialRecordAfterReveal":"Official ICANN application record published on Reveal Day","exploreOpenRecordLabel":"Open record for {name}","idea":"Idea","notSelected":"Not selected","current":"Current","luganoProvenanceNote":"Official ICANN Reveal Day record · 7 Oct 2026 · Application CDL2651T-T31516 · Città di Lugano · self-designated geographic application.","cityMiniIdea":"IDEA","cityMiniApplication":"APPLICATION","cityMiniLaunch":"DELEGATION / LAUNCH","asOfLabel":"as of","tocHowAnatomy":"Domain anatomy","tocHowRoles":"Actors & lifecycle","tocHowBirth":"Creating a TLD","tocHowTerms":"Rules, terms & levers","tocHowLuganoCase":".lugano in practice","tocHowLuganoGovernance":".lugano governance","tocHowLuganoLanguage":"Talking about .lugano","driftTitle":"When the dot changes meaning","driftSub":"Some country-code TLDs kept their formal geographic designation while acquiring a second, global cultural meaning.","driftFormalLabel":"Formal meaning","driftAdoptedLabel":"Acquired meaning","driftMedia":"Media","media":"Media","semanticDrift":"Semantic drift","driftIoFormal":"ccTLD · British Indian Ocean Territory","driftIoAdopted":"input/output · tech & startups","driftIoReading":"The registry itself now markets .io as “the domain for tech websites”, explicitly acknowledging the input/output reinterpretation and its place in startup culture.","driftAiFormal":"ccTLD · Anguilla","driftAiAdopted":"artificial intelligence","driftAiReading":"A geographic code became almost perfectly aligned with a later technology acronym; the registry now presents .AI as the “Domain of Artificial Intelligence”.","driftTvFormal":"ccTLD · Tuvalu","driftTvAdopted":"television · video · creators","driftTvReading":"The registry markets .TV around video and creator culture: the geographic code now communicates a media format to users worldwide.","driftMeFormal":"ccTLD · Montenegro","driftMeAdopted":"personal identity · personal branding","driftMeReading":"A country code became a pronoun and a personal-branding device. The registry explicitly describes .ME as globally personal rather than merely geographic.","driftCoFormal":"ccTLD · Colombia","driftCoAdopted":"company · business","driftCoReading":"The shift was unusually explicit: IANA records early plans to commercialise .CO globally as a de-facto generic domain associated with “company”.","driftFmFormal":"ccTLD · Federated States of Micronesia","driftFmAdopted":"radio · streaming · audio","driftFmReading":"The registry has deliberately positioned .FM as an industry-specific namespace for broadcasting, streaming, podcasting and social media.","driftReadingTitle":"A namespace can be governed, but meaning also emerges from use.","driftReadingBody":"A TLD has a formal status, but not necessarily a fixed cultural meaning. Registries define rules and eligibility; language, technology and communities can rewrite what people read after the dot. The same principle matters for .lugano: governance can define the perimeter, while actual use will shape what the namespace comes to mean.","downloadDrift":"Semantic drift of ccTLDs","downloadDriftSub":"CSV · formal origin, acquired association and sources","odditiesTitle":"When the DNS remembers a different world","odditiesSub":"Country codes can outlive states, disappear with them, remain reserved, or describe places that barely behave like ordinary domain markets. The root is part directory, part institutional fossil record.","oddityReadingTitle":"The root is not a clean map of today.","oddityReadingBody":"The DNS inherits geography from standards, but transitions take time and historical exceptions survive. That makes the root a record of political change, administrative convention and technical continuity — not merely a list of current countries.","oddityGhostsTitle":"Ghosts of geopolitics","oddityGhostsSub":"Some country-code domains survive political entities; others are deliberately retired after a transition.","oddityNeverTitle":"A reused code that was not redelegated","oddityNeverSub":"The history of a string and the history of the country code assigned to it can diverge.","oddityHistoryTitle":"A historical exception that stuck","oddityHistorySub":"The United Kingdom is one of the clearest reminders that DNS history predates today’s tidy eligibility rules.","oddityTerritoriesTitle":"Namespaces with almost no market","oddityTerritoriesSub":"A territory can qualify for a ccTLD even when ordinary public registrations are absent or the place itself has no conventional population.","oddityBeyondTitle":"Beyond the nation-state — and beyond Latin script","oddityBeyondSub":"The ccTLD system can represent a supranational entity and the same identity in multiple writing systems.","oddSuFact":".SU was delegated in 1990 and IANA still marks it ACTIVE; IANA historical material identifies it as the Soviet Union code.","oddSuReading":"A DNS label can outlive the state that originally gave it meaning.","oddYuFact":".YU was delegated for Yugoslavia in 1989 and removed from the root on 1 April 2010 after the transition to .RS and .ME.","oddYuReading":"Retiring a ccTLD is a migration problem, not an instant edit to the map.","oddAnFact":".AN represented the Netherlands Antilles. After the territory dissolved in 2010, the code lost eligibility and the domain was retired while successor namespaces were introduced.","oddAnReading":"Political fragmentation can require several successor namespaces rather than a one-for-one rename.","oddTpFact":".TP represented Portuguese Timor. After Timor-Leste became independent, .TL replaced it and .TP was removed from the root in 2015.","oddTpReading":"A ccTLD can become a legacy dependency that must be wound down years after the political transition.","oddCsFact":".CS was formerly delegated for Czechoslovakia and later retired. ISO reassigned CS to Serbia and Montenegro in 2003, but .CS was not redelegated for that country, which continued using .YU until .RS and .ME were created.","oddCsReading":"Reusing a country code does not automatically revive its former Internet delegation.","oddGbUkFact":"ISO uses GB, yet the operational British ccTLD is .UK. IANA lists .GB as a reserved domain and treats .UK as a grandfathered historical exception.","oddGbUkReading":"Internet naming history sometimes wins over later standardisation.","oddAqFact":".AQ is the ccTLD for Antarctica and has been delegated since 1992.","oddAqReading":"The root can assign a geographic identity even to a continent without a normal nation-state or permanent population.","oddBvFact":".BV belongs to Bouvet Island. Norid says the TLD has never been opened for domain registrations.","oddBvReading":"A TLD can exist technically without becoming a public namespace market.","oddSjFact":".SJ covers Svalbard and Jan Mayen. Norid says it has never been opened for registrations and points users toward .NO instead.","oddSjReading":"Eligibility for a ccTLD does not imply that second-level registrations must be offered.","oddEuFact":".EU is treated as a country-code TLD even though the European Union is not a state; IANA recognises EU through an exceptionally reserved eligibility path.","oddEuReading":"The country-code system contains institutional exceptions that do not map neatly onto sovereign states.","oddEuIdnFact":"The European Union also has Cyrillic .ею and Greek .ευ forms, delegated through the IDN ccTLD process.","oddEuIdnReading":"One political identity can exist at the top level in several scripts while remaining part of the same global root.","activeLegacy":"Active legacy","retired":"Retired","reserved":"Reserved","geopolitics":"Geopolitics","history":"History","scripts":"Scripts","territory":"Territory","supranational":"Supranational","downloadOddities":"DNS institutional oddities","downloadOdditiesSub":"CSV · geopolitical ghosts, historical exceptions and unusual ccTLDs","tocStrangeFossils":"Era fossils","tocStrangeDrift":"Meaning shifts","tocStrangeOddities":"DNS oddities","dotMapTitle":"What is a dot?","dotMapSub":"A domain is a technical identifier with legal, economic, institutional and cultural dimensions. These eight perspectives explain what can gather around one name.","dotMapReadingTitle":"The dot is technical. What societies build around it is not.","dotMapReadingBody":"A useful way to read a TLD is to ask which layer is doing the work: addressing, allocation, governance, trust, market value, identity or cultural meaning.","dimAddress":"Address","dimAddressBody":"A human-readable identifier that helps users and software find Internet resources.","dimContract":"Contract","dimContractBody":"A registration exists through agreements among registrant, registrar and registry.","dimMarket":"Market","dimMarketBody":"Names can carry retail prices, premium pricing, renewals and secondary-market value.","dimJurisdiction":"Jurisdiction","dimJurisdictionBody":"Eligibility, policies and legal duties vary across namespaces and operators.","dimIdentity":"Identity","dimIdentityBody":"A domain can accumulate reputation and become a persistent identifier for a person, place or institution.","dimTrust":"Trust boundary","dimTrustBody":"Verification, DNSSEC and registry rules can add trust signals around a name.","dimCulture":"Cultural sign","dimCultureBody":"Communities and language can give a TLD meanings that its original designation never anticipated.","dimPolitics":"Political object","dimPoliticsBody":"Country codes, city names and allocation disputes can reflect institutions, borders and public authority.","domainRightTitle":"What do you actually get when you register a domain?","domainRightSub":"Registration is not the purchase of a piece of the Internet. A registrant enters a contractual relationship, and the name remains subject to renewal, registry policy and the applicable lifecycle.","lifeAvailable":"Available","lifeAvailableBody":"The name is not currently registered and may be eligible under the registry policy.","lifeRegistered":"Registered","lifeRegisteredBody":"A registrant enters an agreement through a registrar and the registry records the name.","lifeRenewed":"Renewal loop","lifeRenewedBody":"A registration can be renewed for another term; renewal is a possible loop, not a mandatory step before expiration.","lifeExpired":"Expired","lifeExpiredBody":"If the registration term ends, post-expiration rules apply. Renewal or restoration may still be possible depending on policy and timing.","lifeRedemption":"Redemption period","lifeRedemptionBody":"If an applicable gTLD registration is deleted by the registrar, it enters a 30-day redemptionPeriod during which restoration may be possible.","lifeDeleted":"Deleted / available again","lifeDeletedBody":"After the lifecycle completes, a deleted name may become available for a new registration.","lifeCaveat":"This is a simplified gTLD-oriented map, not one universal lifecycle. Expiration does not automatically equal deletion. Registry, registrar and ccTLD policies can differ.","economicsTitle":"The economics of the dot","economicsSub":"The same domain can sit inside several markets at once: registration, renewal, premium pricing, registry services and secondary-market trading.","valueRegistrant":"Registrant","valueRegistrantBody":"Chooses the name and pays the retail registration or renewal price.","valueRegistrar":"Registrar","valueRegistrarBody":"Provides the retail channel and submits registration transactions to the registry.","valueRegistry":"Registry","valueRegistryBody":"Operates the TLD database and sets the registry-side commercial and policy model within its framework.","valueIcann":"ICANN layer","valueIcannBody":"For gTLDs, contractual and fee relationships also sit above the registry and registrar ecosystem.","valueAftermarket":"Aftermarket","valueAftermarketBody":"Existing registrations can acquire resale value independently of the registry’s wholesale price.","economicsReading":"A domain price is not one thing. Wholesale, retail, premium and aftermarket value belong to different layers.","controlLeversTitle":"Who can actually pull which lever?","controlLeversSub":"A website, a domain registration, DNS resolution and browser access are different layers. Different actors can intervene at different points; no single organization “controls the Internet”.","leverHosting":"Hosting provider","leverHostingBody":"Can remove or disable content or infrastructure it hosts.","leverDns":"DNS provider","leverDnsBody":"Can stop serving authoritative DNS or alter the zone it operates.","leverRegistrar":"Registrar","leverRegistrarBody":"Maintains the registrant relationship and submits registration changes to the registry.","leverRegistry":"Registry operator","leverRegistryBody":"Maintains the TLD registry and processes additions, changes or deletions under applicable rules.","leverBrowser":"Browser / security layer","leverBrowserBody":"Can warn, block or distrust a destination without changing the registration itself.","leverIcann":"ICANN","leverIcannBody":"Coordinates gTLD policy and contracts, but is not a general-purpose content regulator or Internet police force.","controlReadingTitle":"Same website, different levers.","controlReadingBody":"Taking content offline, suspending a registration, changing DNS and blocking access are not the same action. Governance starts by identifying the layer.","modelTitle":"Two different questions: what kind of application is it, and who can register?","modelSub":"ICANN application types and day-to-day registration access are different axes. A namespace can be geographic, community or brand-oriented and still impose very different access rules.","modelApplicationAxis":"2026 application types / designations","modelAccessAxis":"Operational access model","modelGeneral":"General","modelGeneralBody":"An application that does not fall into a specialized application type.","modelCommunity":"Community","modelCommunityBody":"A specialized application linked to a clearly delineated community and the additional commitments required for that designation.","modelGeo":"Geographic Name","modelGeoBody":"A specialized application for a string meeting the 2026 geographic-name criteria and applicable support or non-objection requirements.","modelBrand":".Brand","modelBrandBody":"A specialized application for an eligible string tied to a registered trademark and qualifying brand operator.","modelIdn":"IDN","modelIdnBody":"An application for a TLD string represented in a script supported through the Internationalized Domain Name framework.","modelGov":"Government / IGO","modelGovBody":"An application submitted by a government or intergovernmental organization and treated as specialized.","modelOpen":"Open registration","modelOpenBody":"A namespace broadly open to eligible registrants under standard registry rules.","modelVerified":"Restricted / verified","modelVerifiedBody":"A namespace where eligibility must be verified; .BANK is an example for regulated banking organizations.","modelNote":"These labels describe ICANN’s 2026 application designations. Historical TLDs are not presented here as if they were 2026 examples. “Open” or “restricted” instead describes practical registration access after delegation.","invisibleTitle":"The invisible DNS","invisibleSub":"The DNS already does much more than turn names into IP addresses. It routes mail, publishes authentication data and certificate-authorization policy, advertises services and can carry cryptographic trust.","dnsMail":"Mail routing","dnsMailBody":"MX records tell sending systems which mail servers handle email for a domain.","dnsAuth":"Email authentication","dnsAuthBody":"SPF, DKIM and DMARC use DNS-published data to help receivers evaluate authorized and authenticated mail.","dnsCert":"Certificate authorization","dnsCertBody":"CAA records publish which certificate authorities are authorized to issue certificates for a domain.","dnsIntegrity":"Integrity and origin authentication","dnsIntegrityBody":"DNSSEC signs DNS data so resolvers can validate its origin and integrity through a chain of trust.","dnsDiscovery":"Service discovery","dnsDiscoveryBody":"SRV records can advertise the hostname and port of services associated with a domain.","dnsHttps":"Connection hints","dnsHttpsBody":"SVCB and HTTPS records can publish service endpoints and connection parameters before a client connects.","invisibleReading":"The DNS is already an infrastructure layer for routing, discovery and trust. “Beyond the domain” is therefore an extension of existing capabilities, not a departure from what DNS already does.","scriptsTitle":"The Internet is not written only in Latin characters","scriptsSub":"Internationalized Domain Names allow top-level domains in non-ASCII scripts. Universal Acceptance is the practical requirement that browsers, email systems and applications actually accept them.","idnExamplesLabel":"Examples in the public root","uaTitle":"Universal Acceptance","uaBody":"A name can be valid in the global DNS and still fail in an application that assumes old ASCII-only or short-TLD rules. Universal Acceptance is about removing those technical barriers.","uaReading":"Global naming requires more than delegation. Software has to recognize the names people are actually allowed to use.","universesTitle":"One dot, different universes","universesSub":"A dot-separated string can look like a domain while belonging to a different naming regime. Visual similarity does not mean the same root, resolver or governance system.","universePublic":"Public DNS root","universePublicBody":".com, .ch, .中国 and other delegated TLDs live in the globally coordinated DNS root.","universeSpecial":"Special-use DNS names","universeSpecialBody":".test, .localhost, .invalid, .onion and home.arpa have standards-defined special behavior; they are not ordinary public-root TLD registrations.","universeAlternative":"Alternative naming systems","universeAlternativeBody":".eth is part of Ethereum Name Service; Handshake describes itself as an experimental peer-to-peer root naming system. These are different naming systems, not ICANN-root TLDs.","universeReadingTitle":"Looks like a domain ≠ is a public DNS TLD.","universeReadingBody":"The important question is not only what comes after the dot, but which root and resolution system gives that label meaning.","digitalResourceTitle":"A country code can become a digital natural resource","digitalResourceSub":"Semantic accidents can create real economic value. Anguilla’s .AI is the clearest current example: a two-letter country code became globally aligned with artificial intelligence.","aiRevenueLabel":"2026 Anguilla budget estimate · Domain Name Registration","aiRevenueCaveat":"EC$253.6 million · budget estimate, not realized revenue","digitalResourceReadingTitle":"Tiny geography, global namespace.","digitalResourceReadingBody":"The economic value comes from a global meaning that the country did not invent but can govern and monetize. A ccTLD can therefore behave like a scarce national digital asset.","wordTitle":"Who gets to control a word?","wordSub":"Contention is only one version of scarcity. A second question is whether a generic word should ever become an exclusive namespace for one operator.","closedTitle":"Closed generics","closedBody":"ICANN defines a closed generic as a generic term proposed to be operated exclusively for the applicant’s own benefit. For the 2026 round, closed generic applications are not permitted unless and until an approved public-interest methodology exists.","closedExample":"The issue is not “who won the auction?” but whether a generic word should be closed at all.","wordReadingTitle":"Some strings are not merely brands. They are pieces of language.","wordReadingBody":"When a namespace corresponds to a generic word, allocation can raise questions about competition, access and public interest that raw contention counts do not capture.","successTitle":"What makes a TLD successful?","successSub":"Registration volume is only one metric. A brand TLD, a verified community namespace and a civic TLD may have completely different purposes, so success has to be measured against mission.","successScale":"Scale","successScaleBody":"How many names are registered, and how quickly the namespace grows or contracts.","successUse":"Active use","successUseBody":"Whether names resolve to real services, websites, email or machine-readable endpoints.","successRenewal":"Renewal","successRenewalBody":"Whether registrants keep names over time rather than treating the namespace as disposable inventory.","successTrust":"Trust and abuse","successTrustBody":"Security controls, abuse rates, verification requirements and credibility attached to the namespace.","successDiversity":"Diversity","successDiversityBody":"How registrations are distributed across registrants, registrars, sectors and geographies.","successPurpose":"Purpose fulfilment","successPurposeBody":"Whether the namespace achieves the mission for which it exists, even with modest raw volume.","successCivic":"Public value","successCivicBody":"For civic namespaces: local adoption, verified actors, useful services, interoperability and measurable public value.","successReading":"This is a framework, not a ranking. A TLD should be judged against the job it is meant to do.","downloadDimensions":"Namespace dimensions","downloadDimensionsSub":"CSV · address, contract, market, identity, trust and politics","downloadDnsCapabilities":"DNS capabilities beyond naming","downloadDnsCapabilitiesSub":"CSV · mail, authentication, certificates, DNSSEC and discovery","downloadLifecycle":"Domain registration lifecycle","downloadLifecycleSub":"CSV · availability, registration, expiration and redemption","downloadModels":"TLD models and access","downloadModelsSub":"CSV · 2026 application types and registration access patterns","downloadSuccess":"TLD success framework","downloadSuccessSub":"CSV · scale, use, renewal, trust, diversity and purpose","downloadLevers":"Control levers","downloadLeversSub":"CSV · hosting, DNS, registrar, registry, browser and ICANN layers","tocHowLifecycle":"Registration lifecycle","tocHowEconomics":"Economics & new TLDs","tocHowModels":"TLD models","tocHowLevers":"Control levers","tocBeyondInvisible":"Invisible DNS","tocBeyondScripts":"Scripts & UA","tocBeyondUniverses":"Different roots","tocGeoResource":"Digital resource","tocContentionWords":"Generic words","tocSourcesSuccess":"Success framework","roleMaintainer":"Root Zone Maintainer","roleMaintainerBody":"Verisign implements validated root-zone changes as the operational Root Zone Maintainer under contract with ICANN.","roleTagMaintain":"MAINTAIN","lifePendingDelete":"PendingDelete","lifePendingDeleteBody":"If it is not restored during redemptionPeriod, the name enters pendingDelete for five days and cannot be restored.","lifeAvailableAgain":"Available again","lifeAvailableAgainBody":"After purge from the registry database, the name may become available for registration again under the registry policy.","lifeDeletionPath":"If the registrar deletes an applicable gTLD registration","modelReserved":"Reserved Name","modelReservedBody":"A specialized application involving a name reserved under the 2026 Applicant Guidebook rules.","modelVariant":"Variant string","modelVariantBody":"An application involving variant strings evaluated together with the primary applied-for string.","modelSupport":"Applicant Support","modelSupportBody":"An application from an applicant qualifying for assistance through the Applicant Support Program is treated as specialized.","cityMiniDisclosure":"APPLICANT DISCLOSURE","cityTimelineAria":"City TLD milestones","controlSourceNote":"ICANN states that it does not control Internet content and is not a general-purpose website takedown authority.","metaTitle":"Connecting the Dots | TLD history, governance & society","languageVersions":"Language versions","staticReading":"JavaScript is disabled. All sections are available below; use the links to navigate or choose another language.","metaDatasetName":"Connecting the Dots — TLD history, governance, economics and social life","metaDatasetDescription":"Structured data on TLDs, ICANN application archaeology, life histories, governance disputes, economic cases, social cases and the 2026 ICANN round.","exploreApplicantEntity":"Historical applicant, where documented","exploreCurrentRoot":"Root status at the documented check","exploreCurrentAsOf":"Status check date","exploreRegistryCountry":"Registry organization country","exploreTechnicalOrg":"IANA technical-contact organization","exploreTechnicalCountry":"Technical-contact country","exploreTechnicalCaveat":"The IANA technical contact is not necessarily the contracted Registry Service Provider.","exploreRegistrationDate":"Registration date in the IANA record","typeGenericRestricted":"Generic, restricted","typeNotApplicable":"No current IANA type","exploreDesignation":"Editorial classification","exploreStateHelp":"Macro-themes are an editorial index applied to every Explorer record; they are not an ICANN/IANA classification. Type, status, program / round and registry country remain separate factual fields. Ambiguous records fall under Other / general.","staticExplorerNotice":"The complete IANA catalogue and technical records are available in the downloadable data pack. Search and record panels require JavaScript.","ianaRegistration":"IANA registration record","exploreOriginRound":"Origin round of the current TLD","registryTransition":"Registry operator transition","registryTransferReport":"IANA registry transfer report","placeCzechoslovakia":"Czechoslovakia","placeCsHistory":"Czechoslovakia; Serbia and Montenegro (code reassignment)","placeSovietLegacy":"Former Soviet Union (legacy code)","placeSerbiaMontenegro":"Serbia and Montenegro","placeNetherlandsAntilles":"Netherlands Antilles","placePortugueseTimor":"Portuguese Timor","placeYugoslavia":"Yugoslavia","placeCatalanCommunity":"Catalan-speaking community","placeZurich":"Canton of Zurich","placeReserved":"Reserved domain — IANA","placeBerlin":"Berlin","placeLondon":"London","placeCologne":"Cologne","placeVienna":"Vienna","placeHamburg":"Hamburg","placeParis":"Paris","placeBarcelona":"Barcelona","placeQuebec":"Quebec","homeGuideLabel":"START HERE · FIVE QUESTIONS TO GET ORIENTED","homeGuideTitle":"A name. A history. Rules, money, people. A place in the world.","homeGuideIntro":"In www.example.com, .com is the top-level domain (TLD) and example.com is the registered domain. The questions below explain who makes the decisions, who runs the infrastructure and how names affect markets and communities.","homeWhoDecides":"Who decides?","homeWhoDecidesBody":"ICANN, IANA, registries and registrars have different responsibilities. Knowing which actor can make which decision is the first step towards understanding the system.","homeWhoRuns":"Who runs it?","homeWhoRunsBody":"Rules, TLD operators and technical infrastructure are not all located in the same place. Seeing where they concentrate makes the system easier to understand.","homeWhatChanges":"What happens when names collide?","homeWhatChangesBody":"A proposed TLD name can also be a trademark, a region, a culture or a religious term. Objections, governments, auctions and review mechanisms can change what happens next.","homeReadRoles":"Understand the roles","homeReadPower":"Explore the geography","homeReadRound":"See where strings collide","homeExplorerLink":"Search a TLD","homeHistoryLink":"Explore the history","homeScope":"An independent project by Robert Bregy: explained stories, accessible data and sources to check each step.","authorDisclosureTitle":"The author’s position","authorDisclosureBody":"Robert Bregy is the municipal secretary of the City of Lugano and is involved in the .lugano project. This publication is a personal, independent research project. Its interpretations and scenarios do not constitute an official City position or decision.","publicQuestionsTitle":"A vision to test against public evidence","publicQuestionsIntro":"These are editorial questions for assessing a possible civic namespace. They describe neither adopted municipal targets nor services already delivered.","publicNeedTitle":"A clear use","publicNeedBody":"Which task would become easier or more reliable for a person, organization or service? A distinctive suffix alone does not demonstrate that benefit.","publicAdoptionTitle":"Actual adoption","publicAdoptionBody":"Do names lead to services people use? Published examples of active use would be stronger evidence than the number of names reserved or announced.","publicRulesTitle":"Rules that can be checked","publicRulesBody":"Can users understand who is eligible, who is responsible and how a problem can be reported? Confidence should rest on published rules and accountable practice.","publicOpenTitle":"Working connections","publicOpenBody":"Can people and software use the services through documented interfaces and accepted standards? Public documentation and working demonstrations would make this claim testable.","recordCurated":"Curated record","presetCurated":"Curated records","exploreAscii":"ASCII / DNS form","exploreRootListed":"Present in the IANA root list","rootListedYes":"Yes, at the stated date","rootListedNo":"No, at the stated date","exploreProfiles":"IANA profiles","ianaRecordNote":"Official fields from the preserved IANA record. An empty field means that IANA does not report it or it could not be reliably identified. The registry country is the country of the listed organization, not necessarily the place represented by the TLD. Personal contact details are available in the source.","recordIana":"IANA profile","allCountries":"All registry countries","countryFilterLabel":"Registry organization country","exploreColCountry":"Registry country","presetRoot":"In the DNS root","presetOutsideRoot":"Outside the DNS root","ianaNotReported":"Not reported","ianaLastUpdated":"IANA record last updated","ianaRetrieved":"Source retrieved","ianaDatesNote":"Registration, record update and retrieval are different dates. They do not establish when the present operator took over or which application round originated the TLD.","ianaContacts":"IANA contact organizations","ianaAdminOrg":"Administrative-contact organization","ianaAdminCountry":"Administrative-contact country","ianaServices":"Registry services","ianaRegistryWebsite":"Registry website","ianaNameServers":"Name servers and IP addresses","ianaReports":"IANA reports","ianaReportsNote":"Reports linked by IANA, with their original titles and publication dates. A report date is not necessarily the effective date of a delegation or transfer. The list is not a complete operator history.","ianaOfficialRecord":"Official IANA record","ianaCuratedContext":"Researched context","exploreAdvancedFilters":"Filter by round and theme","profileLoading":"Loading profile…","profileLoadError":"This profile could not be loaded. Try again or consult the source and downloadable data.","profileRetry":"Try again","profileDataDownload":"Download complete data","updatesTitle":"Versions and updates","updatesIntro":"The publication version and the dates of its sources are separate. An interface update does not refresh the underlying observations.","updatesRelease":"Publication release","updatesResearch":"Editorial snapshot","updatesIana":"IANA root snapshot","updatesPolicy":"Updates are published after source acquisition and checks. Source files and their hashes are preserved; historical events retain their own dates. The 2026 application layer is updated only from official publications, never automatically when a calendar date arrives.","updatesChanges":"Recent changes","updatesManifest":"Data inventory and verification","release078":"Lighter initial loading, shared language assets and profiles loaded on opening. Simpler Explorer controls and a visible update history. The source snapshot is unchanged.","release077":"All 1,595 IANA database profiles imported, covering 1,437 root-list names. Country filters, technical details and reusable exports added.","journeyEyebrow":"START HERE · NO TECHNICAL KNOWLEDGE NEEDED","journeyTitle":"Behind a click: how the Internet works","journeyIntro":"When you open a website, its name helps find the computer that responds. Follow the steps one at a time.","journeyFoundation":"People remember names such as example.com. Networks use numerical addresses: DNS connects the two.","journeyExample":"An educational simulation, with no requests to the example website. 192.0.2.10 is reserved for documentation; it is not the real address of example.com.","journeyAddressLabel":"What you typed","journeyPhaseName":"READ THE ADDRESS","journeyPhaseDns":"FIND THE DESTINATION","journeyPhaseWeb":"REACH THE SERVER AND RECEIVE THE PAGE","journeyStepCount":"Step {step} of {total}","journeyStepsLabel":"Steps in the journey","journeyStart":"Follow a click","journeyNext":"Next","journeyBack":"Back","journeyRestart":"Start again","journeyDetails":"One level deeper","journeyNoJs":"You can read all the steps below even without JavaScript.","journeyDnsLane":"01 · From the name to the destination","journeyDnsNote":"If the answer is not already cached, the resolver follows delegations: the root points to .com, .com points to the DNS for example.com, and the authoritative DNS returns the requested data.","journeyWebLane":"02 · From the IP address to the page","journeyWebNote":"From here, website traffic crosses networks and routers. The DNS servers have finished their job for this lookup.","journeyResolver":"Resolver · finds where the name is","journeyRoot":"DNS root","journeyTld":"TLD servers","journeyAuthority":"Authoritative DNS","journeyBrowser":"Your browser","journeyNetworks":"Networks and routers","journeyServer":"Web server","journeyResolved":"DNS lookup result","journeyPageReady":"The page takes shape","journeyPageSample":"An example page","journeyTakeaway":"The whole path: URL → name → DNS → IP → networks → HTTPS → request → page.","journeySimplified":"This journey is simplified: caches and existing connections can skip some steps; others can overlap. A nearby server holding a copy may also deliver the page.","journeyExplore":"Explore top-level domains","journeyAnatomy":"See the parts of a domain","journeyHomeTitle":"What happens when you open a website?","journeyHomeBody":"From IP packets to domain names: a short simulation of what happens behind a click.","journeyHomeLink":"Follow a click","tocHowBasics":"Behind a click","tocHowDimensions":"From dot to domain","journey0Label":"The address","journey0Title":"A URL tells the browser how, where and what to ask for","journey0Body":"You typed https://www.example.com/news. https:// says how to communicate; www.example.com is the name to reach; /news is the requested resource. Before anything travels, the browser separates those jobs.","journey0Detail":"A URL can also contain a port, query parameters and a fragment. We use a deliberately simple address here so the roles are visible without extra syntax.","journey1Label":"The name","journey1Title":"The browser has to find www.example.com","journey1Body":"People use readable names; the network needs to know which IP address should receive the data. The browser therefore relies on a DNS resolver to find where www.example.com can be reached.","journey1Detail":"If a valid answer is already cached on the device or resolver, much of the DNS lookup can be skipped. The resolver may be supplied by your provider, organization or a service you choose.","journey2Label":"The TLD","journey2Title":"DNS follows a hierarchy","journey2Body":"If the answer is not already cached, the resolver can start at the DNS root. The root points to .com; the .com servers point to the DNS responsible for example.com. The final dot in www.example.com. represents the root and is normally omitted.","journey2Detail":"The root and TLD mostly provide delegations: directions about where to ask next. They do not host the web page or carry its content. Running a TLD is a different role from hosting a website.","journey3Label":"Name → IP","journey3Title":"The name becomes an IP address","journey3Body":"The authoritative DNS for example.com returns the requested data. The resolver gives it to your device. In this simulation: www.example.com → 192.0.2.10.","journey3Detail":"Authoritative DNS servers publish data for the domains they serve. A records contain IPv4 addresses; AAAA records contain IPv6 addresses. One name can have several addresses, and several names can share an address.","journey4Label":"To the server","journey4Title":"Now the network knows where to send the packets","journey4Body":"With the IP address available, packets can cross routers and networks until they reach the server. The Internet is not one network: it is a network of networks exchanging traffic.","journey4Detail":"IP handles addressing and forwarding without guaranteeing delivery on its own. HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 normally use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP. These transport choices sit above IP and help the exchange arrive reliably and efficiently.","journey5Label":"HTTPS","journey5Title":"The browser creates a protected connection","journey5Body":"For HTTPS, the browser checks the certificate presented by the server for the requested name and establishes an encrypted connection. Data can now travel protected between browser and server.","journey5Detail":"HTTPS protects communication and authenticates the server for that name. It does not certify that the operator is honest or the content is true. DNSSEC has a different job: verifying the origin and integrity of DNS data.","journey6Label":"The page","journey6Title":"The browser asks for /news and builds what you see","journey6Body":"Over the ready connection, the browser requests /news. The server responds with content and instructions; the browser interprets HTML, CSS, images and JavaScript and assembles the page.","journey6Detail":"A visit may require more files, other servers and additional DNS lookups. A domain name and a website are separate things: you can move the content while keeping the same name and changing DNS.","release079":"Adds “Behind a click”: a seven-step guided journey with accessible explanations, optional detail and separate views of DNS and web traffic. Research data unchanged.","journeySources":"Sources and technical details","geoLayersLabel":"Map layers","regionNorthAmerica":"North America","regionEurope":"Europe","regionAsiaPacific":"Asia-Pacific","regionLatinAmerica":"Latin America and the Caribbean","regionAfrica":"Africa","profileLinkMissing":"This profile is not available in this edition. Search the Explorer to find the domain.","profileShare":"Share profile","profilePermalink":"Direct profile link","feedbackLink":"Report an error","feedbackTitle":"Found something to correct?","feedbackIntro":"Tell us which fact, source, translation or feature needs correcting. The link opens an editable report with the page, version and language already filled in. If possible, include a source that supports your correction.","feedbackOpen":"Prepare a report on GitHub ↗","feedbackNote":"Reports are public on GitHub. An account is required; you review and submit the report there.","feedbackIssueTitle":"Correction report","feedbackPage":"Page","feedbackVersion":"Version","feedbackLanguage":"Language","feedbackRecord":"Profile","feedbackDescription":"What is wrong?","feedbackCorrection":"What should be changed?","feedbackSource":"Supporting source or steps to reproduce the problem:","release0710":"Localizes regional maps, exposes selected controls to assistive technology, and adds direct profile links, sharing and public correction reports. Research data unchanged.","exploreIntroductionTitle":"Origin and introduction path","exploreIntroductionPath":"Introduction path","exploreProgramRound":"ICANN program / round","exploreIntroductionBasis":"Evidence basis","exploreIntroductionNote":"Program classifications marked as derived use the IANA registration date and the documented ICANN program era; they are not applicant-supplied fields.","notApplicable":"Not applicable","basisDirect":"Directly documented","basisFormalType":"IANA formal type","basisDocumentedSet":"Documented program set","basisDerivedEra":"Derived from IANA date + program era","introCcTld":"Country-code TLD (ccTLD)","introIdnCcTld":"IDN country-code TLD pathway","introHistoricalCcTld":"Historical / retired ccTLD","introHistoricalTld":"Historical / retired TLD","introInfrastructure":"Internet infrastructure TLD","introTest":"IDN test TLD","introLegacyGtld":"Legacy / pre-round gTLD","intro2000":"2000 proof-of-concept round","intro2004":"2004 sponsored TLD round","intro2012":"2012 New gTLD Program","intro2026Application":"2026 application (not delegated)","introApplicationOnly":"Application / proposal only","introUnknown":"Unclassified introduction path","transition":"Transition","release0711":"Universal Explorer: origin/introduction path for every record, complete IANA profiles, and historical .ZR plus .NATO coverage.","downloadTldUniverse":"Universal TLD dataset","downloadTldUniverseSub":"CSV · complete dated TLD/profile universe","downloadApplicationOnly":"Application-only strings","downloadApplicationOnlySub":"CSV · all application-only Explorer records · complete 2000/2004/2012 historical coverage + verified Reveal Day 2026 records currently archived in the project","recordApplication":"Application-only record","applicationArchaeologyTitle":"Application archaeology","applicationArchaeologySub":"Formal application history is kept separate from delegation. A proposed string is not presented as a TLD unless it actually entered the root. Personal contact names and email addresses from the 2012 Reveal Day file are intentionally excluded.","applicationSingular":"application link","applicationsPlural":"application links","unknownApplicant":"Applicant not reported","applicationIdn":"IDN","applicationCommunity":"Community","applicationGeographic":"Geographic","relation_item_e2_requested":"Item E2 requested string","relation_mentioned_alternative":"Mentioned alternative","relation_mentioned_alternative_selected":"Mentioned alternative · later selected","outcome_selected":"Submission selected","outcome_not_selected":"Not selected","outcome_returned":"Returned","outcome_withdrawn":"Withdrawn","outcome_delegated":"Delegated","outcome_delegated_2012":"Delegated 2012","outcome_delegated_2011":"Delegated 2011","applicationCorpusLoading":"Application archive: complete local 2000, 2004 and 2012 string/applicant corpora are being prepared.","applicationCorpusReady":"The locally archived 2000/2004/2012 applicant–string archive contains {count} records ({rounds}). The Explorer has {records} browsable TLD/string records including {strings2026} proposed 2026 strings from a secondary index. Separately, the new official ICANN APS CSV lists 2,783 rows for 1,614 public applications plus 1,169 replacement records. One of ICANN's 1,615 total (.wdo) lacks a public listing. Only {official2026} detailed APS dossier is independently archived locally; full public questionnaires/PDFs are not yet archived.","applicationCorpusError":"The local application archive could not be rendered correctly. The post-Reveal 2026 layer is deliberately kept separate from any unsupported completeness claim.","exploreApplications":"historical applications","downloadApplications2000":"2000 applications","downloadApplications2000Sub":"CSV · all 47 submissions","downloadApplicationStrings2000":"2000 proposed strings","downloadApplicationStrings2000Sub":"CSV · 223 Item E2 links + documented alternatives","downloadApplications2004":"2004 applications","downloadApplications2004Sub":"CSV · all 10 submissions / 9 strings","downloadApplications2012":"2012 Reveal Day applications","downloadApplications2012Sub":"CSV · all 1,930 applications · 1,409 Reveal-Day strings + 4 approved string corrections · contact/email fields excluded","release0712":"Application Archaeology: complete 2000 and 2004 submission corpora are archived in the project; the full 2012 Reveal Day corpus is retrieved from an archival copy, reduced in-browser to non-contact application metadata, locked to official ICANN totals and merged into the Explorer at runtime.","applicationSubmitted":"Application / proposal","registryAgreementSigned":"Registry Agreement signed","registryAgreementTerminated":"Registry Agreement terminated","rootDelegation":"Root delegation","registryTransfer":"Registry transfer / redelegation","ianaReportEvent":"IANA change report","removedFromRoot":"Removed from root","revoked":"Revocation","lifeHistorySub":"Chronology assembled from formal applications, ICANN Registry Agreement lifecycle data, IANA registration records, delegation/transfer reports and the current root state.","lifeHistoryMethod":"Dates are shown only when supported by an attributable source. Missing phases are left missing rather than inferred. This Reveal Day snapshot uses the preserved local chronology plus verified 2026 records; browser-time ICANN contract-lifecycle enrichment remains disabled so every visitor sees the same reproducible dataset.","gtldAgreementHistoryTitle":"ICANN contract lifecycle","applicationIdLabel":"Application ID","registryOperatorLabel":"Registry operator","agreementDateLabel":"Registry Agreement date","delegationDateLabel":"Delegation date","contractTerminatedLabel":"Contract terminated","removalDateLabel":"Root removal date","yes":"Yes","no":"No","release0713":"TLD Life Histories: chronological application, contract, delegation, transfer, revocation and current-state evidence added across the Explorer.","ianaDelegationReport":"IANA delegation report","ianaTransferReport":"IANA transfer / redelegation report","ianaRevocationReport":"IANA revocation report","ianaRetirementReport":"IANA retirement / removal report","guard5":"Economic metrics retain their original accounting basis and currency. Public revenue, company revenue, operating income, transaction value, auction price and application fees are never treated as one comparable measure.","tabDisputes":"Disputed Dots","disputesTitle":"Disputed Dots","disputesSubtitle":"When a string becomes politics.","disputesIntro":"A contention set means several applicants want the same or a confusingly similar string. A governance dispute is broader: governments, communities, rights holders or applicants can challenge who should operate a name, under what conditions, or whether it should exist at all.","disputesSelectionNote":"These cases are selected because each exposes a different governance problem. They are not a league table and not a complete catalogue of every dispute in ICANN history.","disputesMechanismsTitle":"How a TLD dispute can enter the system","disputesMechanismsSub":"Not every fight is a lawsuit. The new-gTLD process has several formal routes for public-policy concerns, rights claims, community claims, competition and accountability review.","disputesCasesTitle":"Nine disputes that changed what the dot means","disputesCasesSub":"Each case separates the documented record from the question it raises about power, legitimacy and naming.","caseCoreQuestion":"The question","caseWhyMatters":"Why it matters","caseOutcome":"Outcome","caseMilestones":"Key milestones","governanceCaseBadge":"GOVERNANCE CASE","governanceCaseDrawerTitle":"Governance case","governanceCaseDrawerSub":"This string is part of a selected dispute case in Connecting the Dots. The case narrative is editorial; the milestones and outcome are tied to cited primary sources.","governanceCaseOpen":"Read the full case","disputesExplore":"Open in Explorer","mechanismGac":"GAC advice","mechanismGacBody":"Governments can raise public-policy concerns through the Governmental Advisory Committee. Board treatment depends on the form of the advice and the applicable rules.","mechanismObjection":"Formal objection","mechanismObjectionBody":"Applications can face String Confusion, Legal Rights, Community or Limited Public Interest objections before independent dispute-resolution providers.","mechanismCpe":"Community Priority Evaluation","mechanismCpeBody":"A qualifying community application in a contention set can seek priority by meeting the Applicant Guidebook criteria.","mechanismAuction":"Contention resolution / auction","mechanismAuctionBody":"When identical-string contention remains unresolved, an ICANN auction of last resort can determine which eligible applicant proceeds.","mechanismAccountability":"Accountability review","mechanismAccountabilityBody":"Reconsideration and the Independent Review Process test whether ICANN followed its Bylaws, Articles and established procedures.","mechanismBoard":"ICANN Board decision","mechanismBoardBody":"Some disputes ultimately require the Board to weigh application rules, GAC advice, accountability findings and the public interest.","caseCategoryPublicPolicy":"Public policy","caseCategoryAccountability":"Accountability","caseCategoryGeography":"Geographic identity","caseCategoryGovernment":"Government advice","caseCategoryCompetition":"Competition","caseCategoryEconomicControl":"Economic control","caseCategoryLegalRights":"Legal rights","caseCategoryCommunity":"Community","caseCategoryReligion":"Religious sensitivity","caseXxxQuestion":"Can ICANN reverse course on a controversial string, and what happens when its own accountability process disagrees?","caseXxxFact":".XXX moved from an unsuccessful 2000 proposal to the 2004 sponsored-TLD process. The Board later denied the application; ICM initiated the first IRP request heard by a panel, prevailed in 2010, and .XXX was delegated in 2011.","caseXxxReading":"The case made ICANN accountability tangible: a politically sensitive string became a test of how far Board discretion can go and how that discretion can be reviewed.","caseXxxOutcome":".XXX was delegated in 2011 to ICM Registry LLC.","caseXxxEvent1":"ICM proposed .xxx and .kids during the 2000 proof-of-concept round.","caseXxxEvent2":"The Board authorized negotiations on commercial and technical terms for a .xxx registry agreement.","caseXxxEvent3":"The Board denied the sponsored-TLD proposal.","caseXxxEvent4":"ICM filed for Independent Review; the panel decided in ICM’s favor in 2010. It was the first IRP request heard by a panel.","caseXxxEvent5":".XXX entered the DNS root.","caseAmazonQuestion":"Who should be able to operate a TLD string that is also the name of a transnational region?","caseAmazonFact":"Amazon applied for .AMAZON and two IDN equivalents in 2012. Brazil and Peru raised public-policy concerns, GAC consensus advice said the applications should not proceed, and the Board initially accepted that advice. Amazon later prevailed in an IRP; after further Board and government engagement, processing resumed and .AMAZON was delegated in 2020.","caseAmazonReading":"The dispute exposed a fundamental collision between trademark-based corporate identity and geographic, cultural and governmental claims over the same word.","caseAmazonOutcome":".AMAZON was delegated in 2020 to Amazon Registry Services, Inc.","caseAmazonEvent1":"Amazon submitted applications for .AMAZON and Chinese and Japanese IDN equivalents.","caseAmazonEvent2":"GAC consensus advice said the applications should not proceed; the Board accepted that advice in 2014.","caseAmazonEvent3":"Amazon challenged the decision through IRP and was declared the prevailing party in 2017; the panel recommended a fresh merits-based evaluation.","caseAmazonEvent4":"After further negotiations and Board consideration, ICANN directed the applications to continue processing.","caseAmazonEvent5":".AMAZON entered the DNS root.","caseAfricaQuestion":"How should a continent-name TLD be awarded when applicants disagree over legitimacy and government support?","caseAfricaFact":"Two applicants sought .AFRICA. GAC consensus advice said DotConnectAfrica’s application should not proceed. DotConnectAfrica later prevailed on important process claims in an IRP, forcing ICANN to resume evaluation. The competing Registry.Africa application ultimately proceeded to delegation.","caseAfricaReading":"The dispute became a landmark test of government advice, transparency and ICANN accountability in a geographically symbolic namespace.","caseAfricaOutcome":".AFRICA was delegated in 2017 to ZA Central Registry NPC trading as Registry.Africa.","caseAfricaEvent1":"DotConnectAfrica Trust and ZA Central Registry applied for .AFRICA.","caseAfricaEvent2":"GAC consensus advice said DotConnectAfrica’s application should not proceed, and ICANN halted its evaluation.","caseAfricaEvent3":"An IRP panel found in favor of DotConnectAfrica on process-related claims and recommended that evaluation resume.","caseAfricaEvent4":"The Board addressed the IRP outcome and the path toward delegation of the Registry.Africa application.","caseAfricaEvent5":".AFRICA entered the DNS root.","caseWebQuestion":"Can funding and future-control arrangements behind an applicant change who is entitled to win a contention auction?","caseWebFact":"Seven applicants sought .WEB. Nu Dot Co won the 2016 ICANN auction with a US$135 million bid. Verisign then disclosed that it had funded the bid under an arrangement contemplating future assignment of the registry agreement. Afilias challenged ICANN through a lengthy IRP. .WEB was finally delegated to Verisign in 2026.","caseWebReading":"A simple contention set turned into a long argument about applicant identity, control, auction integrity and the boundary between financing and substitution.","caseWebOutcome":".WEB was delegated in July 2026 to VeriSign, Inc.","caseWebEvent1":"Seven applicants entered the .WEB contention set.","caseWebEvent2":"Nu Dot Co won ICANN’s auction of last resort with a US$135 million bid.","caseWebEvent3":"Verisign disclosed that it had funded Nu Dot Co’s bid in exchange for, among other things, a contemplated future assignment of the .WEB registry agreement subject to ICANN consent.","caseWebEvent4":"Afilias pursued an Independent Review Process challenging ICANN’s treatment of the arrangement and the auction outcome.","caseWebEvent5":".WEB entered the DNS root under VeriSign, Inc.","caseWineQuestion":"How far should geographic-indication protections reach inside a global domain namespace?","caseWineFact":".WINE and .VIN triggered sustained government and producer concerns about names such as protected wine regions and appellations. The GAC had no consensus on additional safeguards. ICANN commissioned independent legal analysis and allowed the applications to proceed; both TLDs were delegated in 2015.","caseWineReading":"The dispute showed that the DNS can become a venue for unresolved differences between trademark law, geographic indications and national trade-policy traditions.","caseWineOutcome":".WINE and .VIN were delegated in 2015.","caseWineEvent1":"Applications for .WINE and .VIN entered the 2012 New gTLD Program.","caseWineEvent2":"Governments and wine-sector organizations raised concerns over protection of geographic indications; the GAC did not reach consensus on extra safeguards.","caseWineEvent3":"ICANN commissioned legal analysis and continued Board consideration of whether special protections were required.","caseWineEvent4":".WINE and .VIN entered the DNS root.","casePatagoniaQuestion":"What happens when a trademark is also the name of a geographic region shared by more than one country?","casePatagoniaFact":"Patagonia, Inc. applied for .PATAGONIA. Argentina raised a GAC Early Warning and the application faced Community Objections, including from Argentina’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the Independent Objector. The application was withdrawn.","casePatagoniaReading":"Few cases explain the collision between brand identity and geographic identity more cleanly. The fact that a company owns a mark does not make every geographic meaning disappear.","casePatagoniaOutcome":"The .PATAGONIA application was withdrawn and the string was not delegated.","casePatagoniaEvent1":"Patagonia, Inc. applied for .PATAGONIA in the 2012 round.","casePatagoniaEvent2":"The application received a GAC Early Warning and Community Objections connected to the geographic meaning of Patagonia.","casePatagoniaEvent3":"Patagonia, Inc. withdrew the application.","caseIslamQuestion":"Can a private applicant operate a globally sensitive religious term without broad support from the community it invokes?","caseIslamFact":"Asia Green IT Systems applied for .ISLAM and .HALAL. GAC members raised sensitivities and concerns about community involvement and support, and the applications were held. The applicant later prevailed in an IRP on process issues, but after re-review the Board directed in 2018 that both applications not proceed.","caseIslamReading":"The case tests a hard question the DNS cannot solve technically: who, if anyone, can legitimately speak for a global religious community in a top-level namespace?","caseIslamOutcome":".ISLAM and .HALAL did not proceed and were not delegated.","caseIslamEvent1":"Asia Green IT Systems applied for .ISLAM and .HALAL.","caseIslamEvent2":"GAC members and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation raised concerns; ICANN placed the applications on hold while those concerns were considered.","caseIslamEvent3":"An IRP panel found the applicant to be the prevailing party on process claims, leading the Board to order a fresh review of the relevant advice and correspondence.","caseIslamEvent4":"The Board directed that the .HALAL and .ISLAM applications not proceed.","caseMerckQuestion":"What do “trademark rights” mean when different companies legitimately use the same historic name in different markets?","caseMerckFact":"Merck KGaA and Merck Registry Holdings pursued overlapping MERCK-related strings and filed objections against one another’s applications. The dispute moved through objection, community-priority and accountability mechanisms. A .MERCK TLD was finally delegated in 2026 to Merck Registry Holdings, Inc.","caseMerckReading":"The case breaks the comforting fiction that a famous brand name necessarily has one uncontested global owner. Domain governance has to operate across territorial trademark histories.","caseMerckOutcome":".MERCK was delegated in April 2026 to Merck Registry Holdings, Inc.","caseMerckEvent1":"Merck-related applications entered the 2012 round from different corporate groups with overlapping naming claims.","caseMerckEvent2":"The parties used Legal Rights, Community and related objection procedures against competing MERCK applications.","caseMerckEvent3":"The dispute continued through Community Priority Evaluation and ICANN accountability proceedings.","caseMerckEvent4":".MERCK entered the DNS root.","caseMusicQuestion":"Who gets to define a global “music community” strongly enough to receive priority for .MUSIC?","caseMusicFact":"Eight applicants sought .MUSIC. Competing applicants and music-industry organizations used objections and Community Priority Evaluation, followed by reconsideration activity. DotMusic Limited ultimately signed a community Registry Agreement in 2021 and .MUSIC was delegated later that year.","caseMusicReading":"The case shows that “community” is not a decorative label. In the new-gTLD process it can become a formal claim about representation, boundaries, support and enforceable registration policies.","caseMusicOutcome":".MUSIC was delegated in 2021 to DotMusic Limited under a community Registry Agreement.","caseMusicEvent1":"Eight applications for .MUSIC entered the 2012 round.","caseMusicEvent2":"Community Priority Evaluations considered competing claims to represent a defined music community.","caseMusicEvent3":"The CPE process and related decisions were challenged through ICANN reconsideration and review activity.","caseMusicEvent4":"DotMusic Limited signed the .MUSIC Registry Agreement and the TLD was delegated in 2021.","release0714":"Disputed Dots: nine governance cases, Explorer cross-links and a rewritten site narrative connect applications and life histories to the disputes that shaped ICANN decision-making.","downloadGovernanceCases":"Governance cases","downloadGovernanceCasesSub":"CSV · selected disputes, mechanisms and sources","downloadGovernanceEvents":"Governance case milestones","downloadGovernanceEventsSub":"CSV · dated events and sources","explorePresetDisputes":"Governance cases","tabEconomics":"Economics","homeWhoProfits":"Who makes money from a dot?","homeWhoProfitsBody":"A TLD can behave like national revenue, recurring infrastructure rent, a corporate asset, an auction prize or even a profitable withdrawal.","homeReadEconomics":"Follow the money","explorePresetEconomics":"Economic cases","economicsCasesTitle":"The economics of the dot","economicsCasesSubtitle":"What is a dot worth?","economicsCasesIntro":"TLDs are naming infrastructure, but they can also create public revenue, recurring registry income, scarcity rents, acquisition value and financial barriers to entry. These cases keep unlike metrics separate rather than forcing them into one league table.","economicsSelectionNote":"Selection rule: each case illustrates a distinct economic mechanism and is supported by attributable financial or institutional evidence. Revenue, valuation, auction price and public receipts are not interchangeable measures.","economicsModelsTitle":"Eight ways money enters the namespace","economicsModelsSub":"The same DNS root can generate very different economic relationships.","economicsCasesGridTitle":"Economic cases","economicsCasesGridSub":"Read each number in its own accounting context. The purpose is comparison of mechanisms, not a ranking of “most valuable TLDs”.","economicCaseBadge":"ECONOMIC CASE","economicCaseDrawerTitle":"Economics of this string","economicCaseDrawerSub":"Documented financial evidence linked to this TLD. Different metric types are deliberately kept separate.","economicCaseOpen":"Open economic case","economicCaseExplore":"Open in Explorer","economicSystemCase":"Program-level case","economicCoreQuestion":"Core economic question","downloadEconomicCases":"Economic cases","downloadEconomicCasesSub":"CSV · economic mechanisms, strings and sources","downloadEconomicMetrics":"Economic metrics","downloadEconomicMetricsSub":"CSV · values, currencies, accounting basis and sources","econModelSovereignRent":"Sovereign digital rent","econModelSovereignRentBody":"A country-code namespace becomes a material source of government revenue because global demand attaches value to its two-letter code.","econModelNationalContract":"National contract revenue","econModelNationalContractBody":"A state monetizes its ccTLD through a commercial registry or marketing agreement rather than operating a mass-market registrar business itself.","econModelOperatorAcquisition":"Operator acquisition","econModelOperatorAcquisitionBody":"The company operating one or more namespaces is bought, turning expected registry cash flows into a transaction value.","econModelRecurringRegistry":"Recurring registry economics","econModelRecurringRegistryBody":"Large installed bases and annual renewals can create highly recurring infrastructure revenue, but company-level results must not be mislabeled as revenue from one TLD.","econModelValuationRegulation":"Valuation & regulation","econModelValuationRegulationBody":"A registry contract can underpin billion-dollar valuation while price controls, public-interest duties and change-of-control review shape the asset.","econModelScarcityAuction":"Scarcity & auction","econModelScarcityAuctionBody":"When multiple applicants want the same string, scarcity can be converted into an auction price before the registry has sold a single domain.","econModelPrivateSettlement":"Private contention settlement (2012 · historical)","econModelPrivateSettlementBody":"Historical 2012-round mechanism: applicants could receive consideration for withdrawing from contention, so abandoning a string could generate accounting gains. Private contention resolution, including settlement agreements and compensation for withdrawal, is prohibited in the 2026 round.","econModelEntryBarrier":"Cost of entry","econModelEntryBarrierBody":"Application fees and additional evaluation costs determine who can afford to compete for a place in the root.","econAiQuestion":"Can two letters become a national revenue asset?","econAiFact":"Anguilla’s 2026 budget estimates EC$253.6 million from Domain Name Registration, up from EC$104.3 million actual in 2024.","econAiReading":"The AI boom turned a legacy country code into something resembling a digital natural resource: global semantic demand generates public revenue for a small jurisdiction.","econTvQuestion":"Can a country lease the commercial meaning of its country code?","econTvFact":"Tuvalu’s 2024 budget allocated A$5.60 million to the Dot TV Marketing Agreement and projected A$5.87 million for 2026.","econTvReading":".tv shows a different sovereign model from .ai: value is captured through a commercial agreement tied to the global meaning of “TV”.","econIoQuestion":"What is the operator of a fashionable ccTLD worth?","econIoFact":"A secondary industry source reported that Afilias acquired Internet Computer Bureau, operator of .io, .ac and .sh, for US$70.17 million in cash in 2017.","econIoReading":"The case shows that economic value can sit in the operating company and its registry cash flows, not in a simple notion that a TLD itself has a sale price.","econComQuestion":"What does mature registry scale look like?","econComFact":"Verisign reported US$1.66 billion of company revenue and US$1.12 billion of operating income in 2025; .com and .net ended the year with 173.5 million registrations combined.","econComReading":"Recurring renewals, scale and high switching friction make mature registry infrastructure economically unlike a normal one-off domain sale. The figures are Verisign company results, not .com-only revenue.","econOrgQuestion":"Can a registry contract underpin a billion-dollar valuation?","econOrgFact":"The proposed 2019 acquisition of Public Interest Registry was publicly priced at US$1.135 billion, financed in part by a US$360 million term loan facility; ICANN ultimately withheld consent.","econOrgReading":".org exposes the tension between financial value and stewardship: an economically valuable recurring namespace can still be constrained by contractual approval, regulation and public-interest expectations.","econAuctionQuestion":"How much will applicants pay for scarcity before launch?","econAuctionFact":"ICANN last-resort auctions produced winning prices of US$135 million for .web, US$41.501 million for .shop and US$25.001 million for .app.","econAuctionReading":"A string can acquire a nine-figure clearing price before operating revenue exists. The price reflects contention scarcity and strategic expectations, not an appraisal of the TLD as property.","econWithdrawalQuestion":"Can losing or withdrawing be profitable?","econWithdrawalFact":"Rightside reported a US$22.103 million net gain in 2014 and US$9.403 million in 2015 from withdrawals of interests in gTLD applications.","econWithdrawalReading":"Private contention resolution created an unusual incentive: an application could have financial value even when the applicant never became registry operator.","econFeeQuestion":"How expensive is the right to compete for the root?","econFeeFact":"The base evaluation fee was US$185,000 in the 2012 round and is US$227,000 per application in the 2026 round, before certain additional fees or support discounts.","econFeeReading":"The application fee is not a purchase price for a TLD. It is an entry cost into an uncertain evaluation process, and therefore part of the economic barrier to participation.","econMetricActualPublicRevenue":"Domain registration revenue · actual","econMetricEndYearProjection":"Domain registration revenue · projection","econMetricBudgetEstimate":"Domain registration revenue · budget estimate","econMetricActualContractRevenue":"Dot TV agreement · actual","econMetricBudgetContractRevenue":"Dot TV agreement · budget","econMetricProjectedContractRevenue":"Dot TV agreement · projection","econMetricOperatorAcquisition":"Reported operator acquisition price","econMetricReportedRevenueMultiple":"Reported revenue multiple","econMetricCompanyRevenue":"Verisign company revenue","econMetricOperatingIncome":"Verisign operating income","econMetricDomainBase":".com + .net domain base","econMetricComWholesalePrice":".com wholesale annual fee","econMetricProposedAcquisition":"Proposed PIR purchase price","econMetricDebtFacility":"Term-loan facility","econMetricAuctionWeb":".web auction price","econMetricAuctionShop":".shop auction price","econMetricAuctionApp":".app auction price","econMetricAuctionGrossTotal":"Cumulative gross ICANN auction proceeds","econMetricWithdrawalGain2014":"Net gain on application withdrawals","econMetricWithdrawalGain2015":"Net gain on application withdrawals","econMetricApplicationFee2012":"Base application evaluation fee","econMetricApplicationFee2026":"Base application evaluation fee","release0715":"Economics of the Dot: economic cases, financial metrics, Explorer links and source-backed models added without conflating revenue, valuation, auctions or public receipts.","angle6":"How country codes, renewals, auctions, acquisitions and application fees turn naming infrastructure into economic value.","tabSocial":"Social life","homeWhoBelongs":"Who belongs after the dot?","homeWhoBelongsBody":"Some TLDs express language, place, identity, community rules or social protection. A dot can be more than an address: it can become a claim of belonging.","homeReadSocial":"Read the social layer","explorePresetSocial":"Social cases","socialCaseBadge":"SOCIAL CASE","socialCaseDrawerTitle":"Social meaning of this string","socialCaseDrawerSub":"Documented community rules and social purposes are shown as facts; the broader social meaning remains an editorial reading.","socialCaseOpen":"Open social case","socialCasesTitle":"The social life of the dot","socialCasesSubtitle":"Who belongs after the dot?","socialCasesIntro":"TLDs can mark language, territory, identity, professions, rights or community norms. These cases examine what a namespace asks of the people who use it and the community it claims to represent.","socialSelectionNote":"This is a curated social reading, not a ranking of “social success”. Registration volume, community value and cultural significance are different things.","socialModelsTitle":"Six ways a namespace becomes social","socialModelsSub":"These categories describe different social functions and can overlap within the same TLD.","socialCasesGridTitle":"Nine social cases","socialCasesGridSub":"From linguistic communities to local nexus rules, safety policies and non-Latin scripts.","socialCoreQuestion":"Core social question","socialSystemCase":"System case","socialCaseExplore":"Explore this string","downloadSocialCases":"Social cases","downloadSocialCasesSub":"CSV · community, identity, inclusion and social-policy cases","socialModelLanguage":"Language & cultural identity","socialModelLanguageBody":"A namespace can give a language or cultural community a visible place in global naming.","socialModelCivic":"Civic & territorial belonging","socialModelCivicBody":"A local nexus can turn a place name into a membership signal rather than generic branding.","socialModelIdentity":"Identity & representation","socialModelIdentityBody":"A string can become a flag for shared identity, values or representation.","socialModelInstitution":"Community institutions","socialModelInstitutionBody":"Eligibility, verification and governance can bind a namespace to a defined community or institutional movement.","socialModelSafety":"Safety, norms & protection","socialModelSafetyBody":"Registry policies can try to create norms, protections or boundaries for vulnerable or sensitive communities.","socialModelScript":"Script & digital inclusion","socialModelScriptBody":"Internationalized domain names remove the assumption that global naming must be written in Latin characters.","socialSignalCommunity":"Community","socialSignalEligibility":"Eligibility","socialSignalGovernance":"Governance","socialSignalOrganization":"Organization","socialSignalReinvestment":"Reinvestment","socialSignalMemory":"Digital memory","socialSignalPurpose":"Purpose","socialSignalProtection":"Protection","socialSignalVerification":"Verification","socialSignalNexus":"Nexus","socialSignalConnection":"Connection","socialSignalOperator":"Operator","socialSignalDelegation":"Delegation","socialSignalSelfIdentification":"Self-identification","socialSignalEvolution":"Policy evolution","socialSignalRepresentation":"Representation","socialSignalGovernmentConcern":"Government concern","socialSignalOutcome":"Outcome","socialSignalScripts":"Scripts","socialSignalAccess":"Access","socialSignalProcess":"Process","socialCatCommunity":"Catalan linguistic and cultural community","socialCatEligibility":"Use Catalan online, promote Catalan culture, or address that community","socialCatGovernance":"Community-based sponsored origin, now operated under an ICANN registry agreement","socialGalOrganization":"PuntoGal is a non-profit organization rooted in Galician civil society","socialGalReinvestment":"Profits are reinvested in promoting Galician language and culture","socialGalMemory":"More than 7,000 .gal domains were collected for digital preservation in 2024","socialGayPurpose":"A namespace explicitly framed around LGBTQ identity and a safer online space","socialGayDonation":"The 2020 policies filed with ICANN committed 20% of registration revenue to recognized LGBTQ advocacy and activism charities","socialGayProtection":"Additional measures targeted hate speech, cyberbullying and harassment","socialCoopCommunity":"The international cooperative movement","socialCoopEligibility":"Restricted to cooperatives and eligible organizations supporting them","socialCoopVerification":"Eligibility is verified and may be audited","socialNycNexus":"Registrants must maintain a substantive lawful connection with New York City","socialNycConnection":"The policy recognizes economic, cultural, historical, social or other lawful connections","socialNycOperator":"City of New York","socialKidsCommunity":"A community application centered on children and child-related stakeholders","socialKidsProtection":"The application framed .kids as a kids-friendly and kids-safe namespace","socialKidsDelegation":"Delegated in 2022 to DotKids Foundation Limited","socialXxxSelfIdentification":"The original sponsored model was based on voluntary self-identification by the adult-entertainment community","socialXxxPurpose":"The charter included responsible practices, user choice and measures against illegal abusive material","socialXxxEvolution":"The 2024 renewal moved from sponsorship to the Base Registry Agreement while carrying key commitments into PICs","socialIslamRepresentation":"The applications sought to operate globally meaningful religious terms","socialIslamGovernment":"GAC members and OIC states questioned community involvement, support and legitimacy","socialIslamOutcome":"The strings were not delegated","socialIdnScripts":"Examples include Cyrillic, Arabic, Chinese and Devanagari top-level labels","socialIdnAccess":"Users can navigate domain names in local languages and scripts instead of transliterating everything into Latin characters","socialIdnProcess":"The IDN ccTLD Fast Track has enabled eligible countries and territories to request non-Latin country-name TLDs since 2009","socialCatQuestion":"Can a TLD represent a language community rather than a state?","socialCatFact":"The .cat charter says the namespace serves the Catalan Linguistic and Cultural Community. Eligibility includes using Catalan online, promoting Catalan culture or addressing that community.","socialCatReading":"It separates cultural-linguistic belonging from state borders: a top-level domain can represent a community that is not reducible to one country.","socialGalQuestion":"Can a namespace become part of a community’s cultural memory?","socialGalFact":"PuntoGal is a non-profit organization that reinvests profits in Galician language and culture. In 2024, its official site reported that more than 7,000 .gal domains had been collected for preservation by national and regional libraries.","socialGalReading":"The namespace functions not only as an identity marker but also as a corpus of digital life worth preserving.","socialGayQuestion":"Can registry policy deliberately shape the social norms of a namespace?","socialGayFact":"Policies filed with ICANN in 2020 committed 20% of registration revenue to recognized LGBTQ advocacy and activism charities and added measures against hate speech, cyberbullying and harassment.","socialGayReading":"Here the registry is not neutral about social purpose: identity, redistribution and behavioral norms are built into the policy design.","socialCoopQuestion":"What changes when a TLD verifies membership in a social and economic movement?","socialCoopFact":"The .coop charter serves the international cooperative community. Current eligibility rules restrict registrations to cooperatives and qualifying support organizations and provide for verification.","socialCoopReading":"The domain works as a membership signal: the string says something about the organizational model behind the registrant, not merely its website address.","socialNycQuestion":"What does it mean to be “local” on a global network?","socialNycFact":"The .nyc Nexus Policy requires registrants to maintain a substantive lawful connection with New York City and recognizes economic, cultural, historical, social and other lawful connections.","socialNycReading":"The DNS becomes a boundary-making device: “New Yorker” is translated from a social idea into an eligibility rule.","socialKidsQuestion":"Can a namespace make the Internet meaningfully safer for children?","socialKidsFact":"DotKids Foundation applied for .kids as a community namespace centered on children and child-related stakeholders, framing it as kids-friendly and kids-safe. The TLD was delegated in 2022.","socialKidsReading":"The case exposes the gap between labeling and protection: a trusted social meaning requires governance, enforcement and adoption, not merely a reassuring string.","socialXxxQuestion":"Does putting sensitive content in a distinct namespace protect users, stigmatize a community, or both?","socialXxxFact":"The original .xxx sponsored charter used voluntary self-identification by the adult-entertainment community and included goals around responsible practices and user choice. The 2024 renewal moved key commitments into Public Interest Commitments under the Base Registry Agreement.","socialXxxReading":"A TLD can act like digital zoning. The social question is whether separation produces useful signaling and protection or simply relocates stigma into the namespace.","socialIslamQuestion":"Who has the legitimacy to represent a global religious community in the DNS?","socialIslamFact":"During the 2012 round, governments and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation challenged the .islam and .halal applications over community involvement, support and representational legitimacy. Neither string was delegated.","socialIslamReading":"Some words are socially too large to behave like ordinary labels. The conflict was not only about technical competence but about who could credibly speak for a global community.","socialIdnQuestion":"Who should have to transliterate their identity into someone else’s alphabet?","socialIdnFact":"ICANN’s IDN framework supports domain names in local languages and scripts. Since 2009, the ccTLD Fast Track has enabled eligible countries and territories to request country-name TLDs in non-Latin scripts.","socialIdnReading":"Internationalized TLDs turn script choice into an inclusion question: the global naming system becomes less dependent on Latin characters as the default doorway to the Internet.","guard6":"Social significance is not inferred from registration volume. Registry charters, eligibility rules and documented community policies are FACT; the broader meaning assigned to them in this publication is a READING.","angle7":"When does a TLD become a marker of language, locality, identity or belonging rather than simply an address?","release0716":"The Social Life of the Dot: nine social cases, six social models, Explorer links and source-backed evidence on language, community, identity, safety and script inclusion.","release0717":"Final audit hardening: corrected current ISO country codes in Explorer geography, aligned the publication snapshot to 4 October, clarified 2012 privacy handling and restored a self-checking release package.","econHistorical2012Prohibited2026":"2012 ROUND · HISTORICAL · PROHIBITED IN 2026","release0718":"Release integrity hardening: publication dates now derive from one state object; 2012 runtime enrichment no longer marks the full application corpus complete; archaeology counts are data-derived; 2026 round comparisons separate 1,663 submitted applications from 1,616 proceeding; historical private contention settlement is explicitly prohibited in 2026; release packaging, build metadata and QA are hardened.","skipToContent":"Skip to main content","heroPlain":"In example.com, .com is the ending: a top-level domain, or TLD. Explore its history and check the data and sources here. .lugano is an application, not an operational extension.","heroLearnCta":"Start here · 3 minutes","heroExploreCta":"Find an extension","exploreAboutTitle":"What does this archive count?","exploreFiltersTitle":"Filters","release0719":"Mobile journey hardening: the guided Internet walkthrough is now strictly viewport-bounded, puts the active explanation before its diagram on phones, reflows DNS and network diagrams on narrow screens, and keeps the current step in view when readers move forward or back.","release0720":"Navigation clarity: the Italian menu now distinguishes “Stringhe contese” (competing applications for the same string) from “Controversie” (governance, legal or community disputes), removing an avoidable semantic ambiguity without changing the underlying sections or research data.","release0721":"Automatic language entry: the root now respects an explicit saved language choice, otherwise detects the ordered browser language preferences, maps supported regional variants to EN / IT / DE / FR and falls back to English when no supported preference is available. Direct language URLs remain authoritative.","release0722":"Guided-address focus: the sample URL now moves its visual emphasis with the walkthrough: the readable host name, the normally invisible DNS root dot, the TLD, the resolved IP, the network path, the HTTPS scheme and the requested page. The root dot appears only while it is being explained.","journeyRootAnswer":"Points to the .com servers","journeyTldAnswer":"Points to the DNS for example.com","journeyAuthorityAnswer":"Returns the requested address","journeyAddressLane":"An URL gives the browser three instructions","journeyUrlHow":"HOW","journeyUrlHowBody":"Use the Web over a protected connection","journeyUrlWhere":"WHERE","journeyUrlWhereBody":"Find this name and the server behind it","journeyUrlWhat":"WHAT","journeyUrlWhatBody":"Request this resource","journeyNowLabel":"What the Internet is doing now","journeyNow0From":"https://www.example.com/news","journeyNow0To":"the browser separates the URL into parts","journeyNow0Hint":"How to communicate · which name to find · which resource to request.","journeyNow1From":"www.example.com","journeyNow1To":"an IP address is needed","journeyNow1Hint":"The browser asks a DNS resolver where that name can be reached.","journeyNow2From":".","journeyNow2To":".com → example.com","journeyNow2Hint":"The DNS hierarchy moves from the root to the TLD and then to the authoritative DNS.","journeyNow3From":"www.example.com","journeyNow3To":"192.0.2.10","journeyNow3Hint":"The resolver gives the device the address returned by the authoritative DNS.","journeyNow4From":"192.0.2.10","journeyNow4To":"web server","journeyNow4Hint":"Routers and networks forward packets toward the destination.","journeyNow5From":"https://","journeyNow5To":"encrypted connection","journeyNow5Hint":"TLS authenticates the server for that name and protects data in transit.","journeyNow6From":"GET /news","journeyNow6To":"page","journeyNow6Hint":"The server responds; the browser interprets the files and builds what you see.","journeyKeyLabel":"REMEMBER THIS","journey0Key":"A URL is not just a website name: it contains different instructions for the browser.","journey1Key":"Names are for people; IP addresses let networks reach destinations.","journey2Key":".com is not decoration: it is a real level in the DNS hierarchy.","journey3Key":"DNS does not carry the page: it turns a name into information that helps locate the service.","journey4Key":"The IP identifies the destination; routers decide hop by hop how to reach it.","journey5Key":"HTTPS protects the transport and authenticates the server for that name; it does not guarantee that the content is true or trustworthy.","journey6Key":"The page is the response to a request: the domain is the name used to reach the service, not the page itself.","journeyRecapKicker":"RECAP","journeyRecapTitle":"From the URL to the page, at a glance","journeyRecapBody":"These are conceptual stages, not a stopwatch: caches and existing connections can skip or overlap some of them. The chain is what matters.","journeyRecap0":"URL","journeyRecap1":"Name","journeyRecap2":"DNS","journeyRecap3":"IP","journeyRecap4":"Network","journeyRecap5":"HTTPS","journeyRecap6":"Page","release0723":"Teaching-first Internet walkthrough: the seven steps now follow one coherent story from URL to name, DNS hierarchy, IP, networks, HTTPS and page. The DNS root is explained inside the hierarchy instead of as an isolated step; a live “what is happening now” strip, one key takeaway per step, sequential DNS delegation diagram and final recap make the relationship between URL anatomy, name resolution and web delivery explicit.","release0724":"Navigation hardening: every user-initiated section change now lands at the start of the selected section on desktop and mobile; the brand is a real home link and reliably returns to the top of the overview. Internal anchored links, dynamically rendered Explorer cross-links, section indexes and the publication stamp share the same offset-aware navigation behavior.","release0725":"Pre-Reveal freeze hardening: browser-time external research enrichment is disabled so every visitor sees the same frozen corpus, and release/data-pack ZIPs are now written deterministically for repeatable SHA-256 hashes. Research data and the 4 October IANA snapshot are unchanged.","applicationCorpusFrozen":"Reveal Day snapshot: the complete historical local rounds ({rounds}) remain frozen and reproducible, while verified 2026 records are added explicitly. Full local APS coverage is not yet claimed.","release0726":"Home-only Internet walkthrough: the guided seven-step journey now belongs structurally to Overview, disappears from every other section, resets when the reader leaves and returns home, and preserves direct anchored walkthrough links. The How it works section now starts directly with the deeper domain and DNS material. Research data are unchanged.","filterAll":"All","filterRoundShort":"Program / round","filterStatusShort":"Status","filterTypeShort":"Type","filterCountryShort":"Registry country","filterThemeShort":"Theme","typeTest":"Test TLD","topicGeography":"Geography & places","topicTechnology":"Technology & Internet","topicBusiness":"Business & brands","topicFinance":"Finance","topicCultureMedia":"Culture, media & education","topicSociety":"Society & identity","topicPublic":"Public sector & trust","topicLifestyle":"Lifestyle, sport & leisure","topicServices":"Services & professions","topicSpecial":"Special & historical","topicOther":"Other / general","release0727":"Explorer filter repair: filters now use explicit compact labels, the IANA test type is localized instead of appearing as a raw “test” value, and a universal editorial macro-theme index covers every Explorer record. Theme filtering therefore works across the whole database while remaining clearly separate from official ICANN/IANA fields.","release0728":"Navigation offset fix: primary section changes now account for both the sticky top bar and the sticky desktop section navigation, so the first content panel is never hidden beneath the menu. Mobile navigation and anchored in-section links keep their existing offset-aware behavior.","release0729":"Pre-Reveal editorial freeze: aligned all 2012 corpus wording with the frozen runtime state, clarified the unavailable 2012 download and corrected a minor Italian accent. Research data and application behavior are unchanged.","release0730":"Universal historical application archive: integrated all 1,930 Reveal-Day 2012 applications, all 1,409 distinct Reveal-Day strings and all four ICANN-approved post-Reveal string corrections into the Explorer, including never-delegated strings, while keeping browser-time external research fetches disabled.","applicationStringChanged":"Approved string change","relation_approved_string_change_target":"Approved string-change target","applicationStringChangeNote":"ICANN approved a post-Reveal correction from {from} to this string.","tabRefusals":"Application outcomes","refusalsTitle":"Applications that didn't make it","refusalsSubtitle":"Why some applied-for strings never entered the DNS root","refusalsIntro":"This is not a catalogue of 'rejections'. It traces source-backed reasons why selected applications did not reach the DNS root: administrative returns, eligibility failures, non-selection, public-policy decisions, technical-safety blocks and voluntary withdrawals.","refusalsBoundaryTitle":"One story, one primary home","refusalsBoundaryBody":"Contention is competition for the same string. Controversies are conflicts over rights, legitimacy, public interest or control. Application outcomes explain why an application did not reach the root. A case can have more than one dimension, but the editorial story appears once, where its main causal mechanism belongs; the other dimensions remain structured data and cross-links.","refusalsBoundaryLink":"Open Controversies","refusalTaxonomyTitle":"Six different ways an application can stop","refusalTaxonomySub":"'Rejected' is too crude. The process contains different gates, and withdrawal is not a rejection.","refusalTaxAdmin":"Administrative gate","refusalTaxAdminBody":"The application can fail before substantive evaluation: missing fee, incomplete submission or another formal defect.","refusalTaxSupport":"Support eligibility","refusalTaxSupportBody":"An applicant seeking financial assistance can be excluded if it does not meet the support-program criteria.","refusalTaxEvaluation":"Evaluation failure","refusalTaxEvaluationBody":"Financial, operational, geographic or other criteria can prevent an application from advancing, sometimes after Extended Evaluation.","refusalTaxPolicy":"Public-policy stop","refusalTaxPolicyBody":"Consensus GAC advice can create a strong presumption that an application should not be approved, subject to Board consideration.","refusalTaxTechnical":"Technical-safety stop","refusalTaxTechnicalBody":"A string can be stopped because delegation itself is considered unsafe, regardless of who applied for it.","refusalTaxWithdrawal":"Withdrawal","refusalTaxWithdrawalBody":"The applicant can leave voluntarily. That is an outcome, but it must not be mislabeled as an ICANN rejection.","refusalSnapshotTitle":"What the 2012 funnel looked like in August 2013","refusalSnapshotSub":"A snapshot at the end of Initial Evaluation. These categories were not all final outcomes, but they show why “not delegated” is not a useful synonym for “rejected”.","refusalPassedIE":"Passed Initial Evaluation","refusalExtended":"Eligible for Extended Evaluation","refusalNotApproved":"Not approved at that stage","refusalWithdrawn":"Withdrawn","refusalOnHold":"On hold","refusalCasesTitle":"Six outcomes worth understanding","refusalCasesSub":"Selected examples, not a complete catalogue of every non-delegation. Each isolates a different causal mechanism.","refusalCaseBadge":"OUTCOME CASE","refusalDrawerTitle":"Application outcome","refusalDrawerSub":"This string appears in a selected case explaining why an application did not reach the root. Outcome, contention and governance controversy remain separate dimensions.","refusalCaseOpen":"Read the outcome case","refusalExplore":"Open in Explorer","refusalCoreQuestion":"What actually stopped it?","refusalOutcome":"Outcome","refusalWhyHere":"Why it belongs here","refusalTypeAdministrative":"Administrative return","refusalTypeWithdrawal":"Withdrawal","refusalTypeSelection":"Proof-of-concept selection","refusalTypeSupport":"Support eligibility","refusalTypePolicy":"Public-policy stop","refusalTypeTechnical":"Technical-safety stop","refusalFeesQuestion":"Can a TLD application die before anyone evaluates the idea?","refusalFeesFact":"Yes. In the 2000 round, two of the 47 submitted applications were returned because the USD 50,000 application fee had not been paid. One covered .number / .tel / .phone; the other covered .wap.","refusalFeesReading":"This is not a dispute and not a judgment on the string. It is the cleanest example of an administrative gate: no valid fee, no substantive review. The later delegation of .tel and .phone also shows that one failed application does not permanently condemn a label.","refusalFeesOutcome":"The two 2000 applications were returned. .tel later entered the root through the 2004 sponsored round and .phone through the 2012 round; the original applications did not survive.","refusalNycQuestion":"Is a withdrawal the same thing as being rejected?","refusalNycFact":"No. ICANN recorded that the 2000 .nyc application was withdrawn because the applicant and ICANN could not agree on the treatment of confidentiality claims.","refusalNycReading":"The stop came from the applicant-process relationship, not from a finding that .nyc was an unacceptable TLD. The same label later returned in the 2012 round.","refusalNycOutcome":"The 2000 application disappeared from that round; .nyc was later delegated to the City of New York in 2014.","refusalGeoQuestion":"Can a proposal be positively evaluated and still not be selected?","refusalGeoFact":"In the 2000 proof-of-concept round, ICANN leadership later described .geo as having received a very positive staff evaluation, while the Board still declined to select it because potential DNS operational risks could not be fully assessed at that experimental stage.","refusalGeoReading":"The 2000 round was a deliberately small proof of concept, not the later pass/fail pipeline. Non-selection could reflect portfolio design and caution rather than a defective application.","refusalGeoOutcome":".geo was not among the seven proof-of-concept TLDs selected in 2000. It remains outside the root.","refusalSupportQuestion":"What happens when financial assistance is itself a gate to the application?","refusalSupportFact":"Only three 2012 applicants requested financial assistance. .kids met the support criteria; .idn and .ummah did not. Under the support rules then in force, the latter two became ineligible for further review and their USD 47,000 fees were to be refunded.","refusalSupportReading":"The striking point is that the stop did not arise from the semantic validity of .idn or .ummah. It arose from eligibility for a support mechanism that was coupled to continued participation in the round.","refusalSupportOutcome":".idn and .ummah did not continue. Ummah sought reconsideration; the request was denied, while ICANN directed that the concerns raised inform review of the support program.","refusalGccQuestion":"Can public-policy advice stop an application before an objection case decides it?","refusalGccFact":"The GAC issued consensus advice that the .gcc application should not proceed. The New gTLD Program Committee accepted that advice and directed that the application would not be approved.","refusalGccReading":"The decisive event was the institutional public-policy gate. A separate legal-rights objection was terminated once the application could no longer proceed; later accountability challenges are secondary to the reason for the stop.","refusalGccOutcome":".gcc did not proceed in the 2012 round. The applicant’s reconsideration effort was unsuccessful.","refusalCollisionQuestion":"What if the problem is the string itself rather than the applicant?","refusalCollisionFact":"ICANN identified .corp, .home and .mail as exceptionally high-risk for name collisions with private naming systems. Delegation was deferred, and in 2018 the Board directed that the 2012 applications should not proceed, with full fee refunds available upon withdrawal.","refusalCollisionReading":"This is the clearest technical refusal in the round: the applicant could change, but the collision risk attached to the label. It is therefore fundamentally different from a fight over who deserves the string.","refusalCollisionOutcome":"None of the three strings was delegated from the 2012 round. ICANN later reaffirmed that prior applicants would receive no preferential right in a future round.","explorePresetRefusals":"Application outcomes","disputesBoundaryNote":"A failed, returned or withdrawn application is not automatically a governance dispute. Its application outcome is modeled separately from controversy and contention.","disputesBoundaryLink":"See application outcomes","release0731":"Separates formal application stops from governance disputes, adds six sourced stop stories and an Explorer preset, and trims the browser-upload package to 99 files.","release0732":"Reframes the former rejection/stop layer as Application outcomes, keeps contention and controversy independent, and adds source-backed outcome_reason fields to the historical application corpus without inferring undocumented results.","outcome_excluded_from_further_participation":"Excluded from further participation","outcome_not_approved":"Not approved","outcome_did_not_proceed":"Did not proceed","applicationContention":"Contention","applicationControversy":"String controversy","outcomeReasonLabel":"Outcome reason","outcomeEvidence":"outcome evidence","outcomeReason_application_fee_not_paid":"Application fee not paid","outcomeReason_confidentiality_claims_unresolved":"Confidentiality claims unresolved","outcomeReason_selected_in_proof_of_concept_round":"Selected in the proof-of-concept round","outcomeReason_not_selected_in_proof_of_concept_round":"Not selected in the proof-of-concept round","outcomeReason_applicant_support_ineligibility":"Applicant Support eligibility failure","outcomeReason_gac_consensus_advice":"GAC consensus advice","outcomeReason_high_risk_name_collision":"High-risk name collision","opensNewTab":"opens in a new tab","release0733":"Accessibility hardening for screen readers and keyboard navigation: modal focus/inert background, disclosure-menu Escape behavior, nonvisual map data, stronger table semantics and explicit external-link announcements. No research data, layout or resting visual design changed.","profileBriefTitle":"In brief","profileBriefDelegated":"At the documented check, {name} was delegated in the DNS root as {type}.","profileBriefNotDelegated":"At the documented check, {name} was not delegated in the DNS root.","profileBriefRetired":"At the documented check, {name} was a historical TLD no longer present in the DNS root.","profileBriefRegistry":"The IANA record lists {entity} as the manager.","profileBriefApplications1":"The archive preserves 1 formal application for this string, from round {rounds}.","profileBriefApplicationsN":"The archive preserves {count} formal applications for this string, across rounds {rounds}.","profileBriefContention":"In round {round}, {count} applications for this string are recorded in contention.","profileBriefOutcomeReason":"For round {round}, the corpus documents this outcome reason: {reason}.","profileBriefGovernance":"The string is also linked to a documented governance controversy.","profileCurrentStateTitle":"Status at the documented check","profileIanaClass":"IANA classification","typeApplicationOnly":"Applied-for string · no current IANA type","profileGlossaryLink":"Unfamiliar with a term? Open the essential glossary","glossaryTitle":"Essential glossary","glossarySub":"The terms that make the Explorer easier to read. Official ICANN/IANA vocabulary is kept separate from Connecting the Dots methodology fields.","glossaryOfficial":"ICANN / IANA term","glossaryProject":"Project methodology","glossaryCsv":"Download the glossary as CSV","tocHowGlossary":"Essential glossary","release0734":"Improves Explorer profile clarity with a fact-only “In brief” layer, a clearer current-status block, cleaner contextual fields and a visible essential glossary that separates official ICANN/IANA terminology from project methodology. Research corpora remain unchanged except for the expanded glossary dataset.","metaSocialImageAlt":"Connecting the Dots project cover: from .arpa to .lugano, tracing Internet domains through ICANN, DNS, cities and society.","release0735":"Repository hygiene and metadata hardening: aligns deployment documentation to the 99-file ceiling, adds localized social-image alternative text, and tightens release checks without changing research data or Explorer behavior.","release0736":"Removes duplicate location text from Explorer cards when the registry country and represented place are the same, without changing any research data or profile detail.","release0737":"Uses natural-height two-column layout for institutional oddity cards so short cases no longer stretch to match longer neighbours.","release0738":"Completes a full-site responsive layout sweep, removing artificial panel stretching and hardening long translated text down to 320 px.","release0739":"Strengthens 2012 provenance with explicit ICANN primary-corpus authority, documents the 1,409 Reveal-Day definition, completes public release history and standardizes the German guided journey to formal address.","release0740":"Final pre-Reveal polish: centralizes displayed counts on the locale-aware formatter, shortens search descriptions, enriches Dataset structured data with version/provenance metadata, and documents the project-path robots.txt limitation without changing research data or layout.","release0741":"Reveal Day release: replaces the pre-Reveal state with ICANN’s 7 October 2026 snapshot, adds official 2026 aggregate statistics and regional application counts, records 263 preliminary identical-string contention sets, and upgrades .lugano from applicant disclosure to the official ICANN application record CDL2651T-T31516. The complete 2026 APS application corpus is not yet claimed as archived in the project.","round2026Variant":"applications with variants","icannRevealRecord":"Official ICANN Reveal Day application record","release0742":"Post-Reveal Explorer repair: the 2026 filter now enriches the string-centric catalogue with the published Reveal Day string inventory, while keeping application-level completeness claims conservative. Pre-Reveal wording is removed and submitted, active, replacement and archived in the project records are explicitly separated.","release0743":"Explorer 2026 hotfix: replaces the impossible one-shot 980-row request with validated pagination across the public string API, caches the completed inventory locally, and fixes the 2026 and contention presets so cross-round strings remain visible.","derived":"DERIVED","contention2026Derived":"Derived from the public 2026 application data. ICANN’s official high-level statistics do not yet publish a unique-string total; replacement choices and later similarity procedures can still change the picture.","contentionResolution2026Title":"A major rule changed in 2026","contentionResolution2026Body":"The 2012 round allowed private settlements and private auctions. The 2026 round does not: once contention sets are finalized, the permitted paths are Community Priority Evaluation for eligible Community Applications and ICANN’s New gTLD Auction. Private auctions, joint ventures and other private contention-resolution arrangements are prohibited.","strange2026Title":"Reveal Day 2026: the mirror changed","strange2026Sub":"AI agents, corporate identity, trust words and deliberately weird language now sit beside the historical oddities. These are applications, not delegated TLDs.","strangeHistoricalTitle":"Archaeology · 2000–2012","strangeHistoricalSub":"The earlier cases stay here as fossils of previous Internet imaginaries. Their value is stronger now that the 2026 layer can be compared against them.","patternLinkGeo":"Explore application geography","patternLinkApplicants":"Follow the economics","patternLinkWords":"See the strange 2026 layer","patternLinkCompetition":"Compare contention","outcomes2026StatusTitle":"2026 status: too early for outcomes","outcomes2026StatusBody":"Reveal Day opened pre-evaluation. The Replacement Period ended on 21 October 2026. String Confirmation Day is scheduled for 17 November, when the Community Input and Objections Period begins. This chapter’s cases are historical outcomes from earlier rounds, not 2026 decisions.","disputes2026StatusTitle":"2026 status: applications are public, formal objections are not open yet","disputes2026StatusBody":"The Community Input and Objections Period begins on 17 November 2026 and is scheduled to run through 16 March 2027. The cases in this chapter are therefore precedents that explain the mechanisms, not disputes already decided in the 2026 round.","economics2026Title":"2026: the economics start before delegation","economics2026Body":"The standard evaluation fee is USD 227,000 per application before Applicant Support discounts and any conditional fees. ICANN reports 1,615 Reveal Day applications and 333 .Brand applications. These are application-market facts, not registry revenue and not a forecast of how many strings will reach the root.","economics2026Fee":"standard evaluation fee","economics2026Apps":"Reveal Day applications","economics2026Brands":".Brand applications","social2026Title":"2026: social purpose is not the same as an ICANN Community designation","social2026Body":"ICANN reports 16 Community-based applications. Other applicants can still pursue an explicit social mission without using that formal designation. .meow is a useful example: its nonprofit applicant says it intends to channel surplus registry revenue into queer community projects. That mission should not be confused with ICANN’s application-type label.","release0744":"Post-Reveal editorial rebuild: updates contention, Strange Internet, economics, social framing, application outcomes and disputes around the 7 October 2026 data; replaces pre-Reveal future tense with current state; and makes official, derived and editorial claims visibly distinct.","sAgent2026":"The current 2026 public application view shows 13 live applications for .agent, the largest identical-string cluster in the derived ranking.","iAgent2026":"In 2012 the most sought-after label was .app. In 2026 it is .agent: a compact signal that the technological imagination has moved from mobile software toward autonomous agents.","sAgi2026":"The current public data show 7 live applications for .agi.","iAgi2026":"A specialist AI acronym has become valuable enough to trigger a seven-way contest for root-level naming space.","sOfficial2026":"The current public data show 8 live applications for .official.","iOfficial2026":"A word that suggests authority could become part of the naming infrastructure if delegated. Users might read trust into the label before opening the site; whether that trust is justified would depend on eligibility rules, verification and actual operation.","sChatgpt2026":"OpenAI is among the 2026 applicants and .chatgpt appears among its revealed branded strings.","iChatgpt2026":"The namespace is being treated as a product and identity layer, not merely a generic directory. The brand can move to the right of the dot.","sMeow2026":"The nonprofit dotMeow Foundation says it applied for .meow and intends surplus registry revenue to support queer community projects.","iMeow2026":"A deliberately playful string can carry a serious governance and funding model. It is also a reminder that social purpose must not be confused with ICANN’s formal Community application designation.","sItiswhatitis2026":".itiswhatitis appears among the 2026 revealed applications highlighted in post-Reveal coverage.","iItiswhatitis2026":"The root can absorb not only industries and brands but entire pieces of Internet-native speech. The namespace is becoming a record of language as much as a map of institutions.","sFart2026":".fart was publicly disclosed as a 2026 application and became one of the round’s most conspicuous examples before Reveal Day.","iFart2026":"The absurd is not a side effect of namespace expansion. It is evidence that applicants see cultural memorability itself as an asset worth an application fee.","sOpenai2026":".openai is among OpenAI’s revealed 2026 branded applications.","iOpenai2026":"A company can now seek to make its own name part of the DNS hierarchy. That shifts the dot from a destination category toward controlled identity infrastructure.","strangeHistoricalToggle":"Open the historical archive · 2000–2012","exploreSnapshotClocks":"Five publication dates, two kinds of evidence: IANA/root snapshot · 4 Oct 2026; 2026 Reveal Day application snapshot · 7 Oct 2026; archived research RR1 · 9 Oct 2026; archived research RR2 · 11 Oct 2026; latest website update · {updated}. Website updates cannot change either archived release.","release0745":"Post-Reveal integrity hardening: makes the partial 2026 application CSV explicit, exposes the three publication clocks, removes the last pre-Reveal placeholder wording, strengthens 2026 provenance/discrepancy metadata and optimizes the paginated external string-inventory loader while preserving conservative completeness claims.","profile2026InventoryProvenance":"2026 nTLDData secondary string inventory; exact 980-string match independently verified against official ICANN APS CSV (11 October 2026)","profile2026Title":"2026 application snapshot","profile2026Sub":"String-level data available in the Reveal Day snapshot. A 2026 application is not yet a delegated TLD.","profile2026Snapshot":"Snapshot","profile2026Applications":"Applications for this string","profile2026Applicant":"Applicant","profile2026Contention":"Identical-string contention","profile2026ContentionSet":"Contention set","profile2026ApplicationType":"Application characteristics","profile2026NoSpecialType":"No special designation identified in the index","profile2026StringLevelNote":"This profile presents a string-level summary. The 980 primary strings have been checked against the official ICANN APS listing. All 2,783 public APS application/replacement rows are available in the source CSV; complete individual application PDFs are not locally archived.","release0746":"Fixes 2026 Explorer profiles that could fail to load and turns runtime records into self-contained profiles: snapshot, application count, applicant when available, contention and formal characteristics now render without depending on historical profile shards.","release0759":"Research-infrastructure hardening: immutable RR1 citation metadata is separated from technical site versions; Zenodo metadata is restored; the working paper becomes indexable and citable; Explorer counts and 2026 snapshot wording are reconciled; Git/GitHub Pages becomes the canonical deployment workflow.","researchGithubTitle":"GitHub repository","researchGithubSub":"Canonical source, history and issue tracker","insideNav":"Timeline","insideEyebrow":"A process observed as it unfolds","insideTitle":"Timeline","insideIntro":"The 2026 New gTLD Program is not just a historical dataset. We can follow its decisive public events as they happen, with the .lugano application providing an unusually direct vantage point. This is a chronicle of milestones, not an application manual.","insideProgramTitle":"What the rules say","insideProgramBody":"The official ICANN program defines milestones, conditions and possible outcomes. Its documents are the source for dates and procedural claims.","insideLandscapeTitle":"What the round reveals","insideLandscapeBody":"Reveal Day and later decisions show the global field: competing ideas, shared strings, objections, evaluations and changing expectations.","insideCaseTitle":"What .lugano lets us observe","insideCaseBody":"A real candidacy gives the research a participant's perspective, but only verifiable and publishable events enter this public record.","insideTimelineTitle":"Milestones and turning points","insideTimelineIntro":"A concise, source-linked timeline. Entries distinguish documented events, the status at the editorial review date, announced milestones, uncertain timing and conditional outcomes. A future date is not a result.","insideStateObserved":"Documented","insideStateCurrent":"Under way at review date","insideStateScheduled":"Scheduled","insideStateEvolving":"Timing evolving","insideStateConditional":"Conditional","insideScopeProgram":"ICANN program","insideScopeProgramCase":"Program + .lugano","insideScopePossibility":"Possible outcome","insideOpenDate":"30 April 2026","insideOpenTitle":"The application window opens","insideOpenBody":"The 2026 submission period begins. An infrequent policy cycle becomes a concrete opportunity for organizations to propose new top-level domains.","insideCloseDate":"12 August 2026","insideCloseTitle":"Applications close","insideCloseBody":"The submission window closes. From this point the proposed namespaces move toward public disclosure and evaluation; the final number and status are not inferred from the deadline alone.","insideRevealDate":"7 October 2026","insideRevealTitle":"Reveal Day: the round becomes visible","insideRevealBody":"ICANN publishes the applicants and applied-for strings. .lugano appears in the public application record, allowing the program-wide picture to be compared with a first-hand case.","insideReplaceDate":"8–21 October 2026","insideReplaceTitle":"The string replacement period","insideReplaceBody":"Eligible applicants may use a previously declared replacement string. The Reveal Day inventory is therefore a dated snapshot, not the final confirmed list. No replacement action is presumed for .lugano.","insideConfirmDate":"17 November 2026","insideConfirmTitle":"String Confirmation Day","insideConfirmBody":"ICANN is scheduled to publish the confirmed applied-for strings and revised contention sets. This remains an announced future milestone, not an event already completed.","insideReviewDate":"Later stages: timing varies","insideReviewTitle":"Public input and evaluations","insideReviewBody":"String assessment, community input, objections and applicant/application evaluation unfold under the program's rules. The timing and outcome of individual applications cannot yet be asserted.","insideOutcomeDate":"Date and outcome not yet known","insideOutcomeTitle":"The outcome, whatever it is","insideOutcomeBody":"Successful applications may proceed to contracting, onboarding and possible delegation. Other outcomes remain possible. This record will document public evidence, not promise an ending.","insideDisclosureTitle":"A participant-observer, not an institutional account","insideDisclosureBody":"The author is involved in the City of Lugano's .lugano application. Connecting the Dots remains an independent research publication, not an official City statement. Public records and clearly attributed observations are included; confidential application material and unverified assumptions are not.","insideGuideCta":"Read the standalone timeline","insideCaseCta":"Explore the .lugano case","insideProgramCta":"ICANN applicant journey","insideResearchCta":"Research method and RR2","insideReviewedLabel":"Editorial status reviewed","insideSourcesLabel":"Primary sources","insideMethodNote":"This is a living editorial account. RR1 (9 October) and RR2 (11 October) are separate immutable Zenodo research snapshots; the present chronicle does not retrospectively change either.","insideMetaTitle":"Timeline | ICANN 2026 milestones and .lugano","insideMetaDescription":"Follow the ICANN 2026 New gTLD round through verified milestones and public events, with .lugano as a first-hand case study and clearly marked future stages.","release0760":"Adds Inside the Round: a public, four-language milestone chronicle of the 2026 ICANN program, with .lugano as a participant-observer case; preserves the frozen RR1 research release.","sectorEconomyTitle":"What categories appeared in the earlier reconstruction?","sectorEconomyKicker":"Historical comparison · classification by string or applicant","sectorEconomyIntro":"The earlier 2012/2026 map grouped some records by applicant activity and others by the market suggested by the string. It supports an exploratory comparison of those categories within the classified subsets, not a measurement of growth or decline in the industries themselves.","sectorCoverage2012":"2012 applications classified in the historical map","sectorCoverage2026":"2026 records classified in the historical reconstruction","sectorDenominatorNote":"The percentages use the classified subsets: 801 of 1,930 records in 2012 (41.5%) and 690 of 1,608 in the previous 2026 reconstruction (42.9%). The 2026 layer includes records reconstructed from per-string counts. Categories may describe the applicant or the string; these shares do not represent the whole round or a uniform industry census.","sectorChartTitle":"Category shares in the historical map: 2012 and 2026","sectorChartSub":"Percentages within each classified subset, using the earlier mixed classification of string themes and applicant sectors.","sectorChartAlt":"Paired 2012 and 2026 bars compare category shares in the earlier mixed classification, including technology, finance, AI, crypto and digital trust. They do not measure the industries as a whole.","sectorTech":"Technology and infrastructure","sectorMedia":"Media and communications","sectorFinance":"Finance and insurance","sectorAI":"AI and automation","sectorCrypto":"Crypto and digital assets","sectorTrust":"Cybersecurity, identity and trust","sectorHealth":"Health and life sciences","sectorRetail":"Retail and commerce","sectorMovementNewTitle":"AI and crypto appear in the 2026 subset","sectorMovementNewBody":"The 2026 subset contains records classified under AI and crypto; the equivalent editorial categories have no records in 2012. This describes the vocabulary and classification of these subsets. It does not mean those technologies did not exist in 2012, or establish future adoption.","sectorMovementFinanceTitle":"Finance: institutions and generic terms","sectorMovementFinanceBody":"The finance category combines identified organisations with generic financial terms. Corporate or brand names and references to a financial market express different naming strategies; their combined share does not count the financial institutions participating in the round.","sectorMovementMediaTitle":"Media and communications: a smaller share of the subset","sectorMovementMediaBody":"In the historical classification, media and communications account for 13.7% of the 2012 subset and 8.0% of the 2026 subset. The category has a smaller relative share; this does not establish a decline in those industries or in their economic value.","sectorMovementTrustTitle":"Identity and digital trust: a larger share of the subset","sectorMovementTrustBody":"The cybersecurity, identity and trust category accounts for 0.9% of the 2012 classified subset and 5.4% of the 2026 subset. That is a larger share in this map, not a measure of growth in the industry. Any future practical value of the names would depend on governance, services and actual use.","sectorFinanceExampleTitle":"Financial names: brands and shared terms","sectorFinanceExampleBody":"A bank's own TLD and a term such as .bank describe very different allocation models. A string's sector does not establish who may register, who governs it, or whether it will be delegated.","sectorMethodTitle":"Method: classification, coverage and limits","sectorMethodBody":"Economic-sector labels are editorial and applied consistently to the preserved 2012 corpus and the frozen 2026 primary-string inventory. Ambiguous and cross-sector cases are excluded from the classified denominator. The comparison is descriptive and provisional, not an ICANN statistic.","download2026StringSnapshot":"2026 Reveal Day string inventory","download2026StringSnapshotSub":"CSV · frozen secondary index of 980 strings; not the complete official APS application corpus","downloadEconomicMap":"Economic classification of applications","downloadEconomicMapSub":"CSV · reproducible 2012/2026 sector and strategy assignments","downloadPortfolioApplications":"Applicant portfolio comparison","downloadPortfolioApplicationsSub":"CSV · 2012 historical applicants and 2026 secondary inventory; sources and limits","downloadSectorEvolution":"Sector change across rounds","downloadSectorEvolutionSub":"CSV · sector counts, shares and classification coverage","downloadSectorMix":"Sector and string strategies","downloadSectorMixSub":"CSV · brand, generic, geographic and unresolved application types","strangeAcrossRoundsTitle":"Strange Internet, round by round","strangeAcrossRoundsSub":"The unexpected is not unique to 2026. Compare curious applied-for strings, their outcomes and the limits of what the records prove across application rounds.","themeRoundLensTitle":"One theme across four application rounds","themeRoundLensSub":"Compare this theme in 2000, 2004, 2012 and 2026. The evidence, dates and counting units differ between rounds.","homeUnique2026Note":"980 primary strings in the frozen secondary Reveal Day index; not an official ICANN unique-string total.","homeCountries2026Note":"481 applicants across five ICANN regions in the official Reveal Day overview; this is not a distinct-country count.","applicationBrand":".Brand application","themeRoundApplicationsLabel":"applications in the local corpus","strangeCrossRoundLabel":"Across rounds","strangeCasesLabel":"documented cases","round2026UniqueNote":"980 primary strings from the frozen nTLDData secondary index; ICANN has not published a comparable unique-string aggregate.","round2026CountriesValue":"—","round2026CountriesNote":"ICANN's aggregate overview does not report a distinct number of countries and territories; no total is inferred from regional figures.","release0761":"Editorial integrity: restore all four-language labels, update the 2026 Reveal Day figures and prevent unresolved translation keys from passing validation.","portfolioTitle":"Who applies at scale?","portfolioIntro":"A TLD round is not only a collection of individual ideas. Some applicants seek dozens or hundreds of strings, turning naming into a portfolio strategy. Compare the legal applicant, the unit counted and the provenance before drawing conclusions.","portfolio2012Title":"2012 · the portfolio pioneers","portfolio2012Donuts":"Donuts applications","portfolio2012Google":"Google applications","portfolio2012Amazon":"Amazon EU S.à r.l. applications","portfolio2012Body":"Donuts filed 307 applications and Google 101 in 2012. The locally archived Reveal Day list of 13 June 2012 contains 76 records for Amazon EU S.à r.l. These figures describe applications, not delegated TLDs.","portfolio2026Title":"2026 · project-derived ranking from APS","portfolio2026Top1":"Link Freedom Group Ltd · live primary applications","portfolio2026Top2":"XYZ.COM LLC · live primary applications","portfolio2026Top3":"Registry Services, LLC · live primary applications","portfolio2026Body":"The public APS export acquired on 11 October 2026 yields 158 applications for Link Freedom Group Ltd, 139 for XYZ.COM LLC and 77 for Registry Services, LLC, out of 1,614 published base Application IDs. These are project calculations by legal applicant; replacement records do not add applications.","method":"Method","arubaPortfolioTitle":"Aruba: why 39 and 20 can both be correct","arubaPortfolioBody":"Aruba announced 39 applied-for strings including replacements; the frozen secondary index lists 20 live primary applications for Aruba PEC S.p.A. These are different units and scopes. Adding or comparing them as equivalent counts would be misleading.","portfolioQuestion":"Who controls a name: the applicant, the corporate group or the eventual registry operator?","portfolioReadingTitle":"What the ranking really reveals","portfolioReading":"Large application portfolios reveal a strategy, not a guaranteed market position. Applications can fail, enter contention, be withdrawn or remain undelegated. Concentration at application stage is not identical to concentration of functioning TLD registries.","portfolioCaveat":"The 2012 and 2026 figures are not a directly comparable corporate-group ranking. The 2026 counts use 1,614 published applications, including six existing-TLD variant applications; the official reported total is 1,615. They describe the captured applications and their original primary labels, not final confirmed strings or awarded TLDs.","researchCitationNav":"Research & citation","researchPubTitle":"Research that stays citable as the site evolves","researchPubIntro":"Connecting the Dots combines an evolving public atlas with dated, reproducible research releases. The live site may change; each archived research milestone keeps a distinct, stable identity.","researchDoiLabel":"Persistent identifier · current Research Release RR2","researchDoiStatus":"Published on Zenodo · RR2","researchReleaseLabel":"Published research milestone","researchReleaseTitle":"RR2 · Post-Reveal evidence consolidation","researchReleaseBody":"RR2 consolidates evidence from Reveal Day, 7 October 2026. The Zenodo deposit was created on 10 October at 22:04 UTC and has a bibliographic publication date of 11 October 2026; the evidence cutoff remains 10 October. DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.23288603. The complete individual APS dossiers are not locally archived.","researchNextLabel":"Next possible evidence milestone","researchNextTitle":"17 November 2026 · String Confirmation Day","researchNextBody":"ICANN has scheduled String Confirmation Day for 17 November 2026. New evidence about confirmed strings, replacements and contention may justify Research Release RR3 after source review. No future result is assumed.","researchVersionRuleTitle":"A website version is not a research release","researchVersionRuleBody":"Technical versions (v0.x.y) may change navigation, accessibility, translations and current editorial analysis. RR1 and RR2 are separate, permanently archived research snapshots and must not be silently overwritten. A substantial new evidence milestone receives a new research release and DOI.","researchCitationTitle":"How to cite this project","researchCitationText":"Bregy, R. (2026). <em>Connecting the Dots: A Living Research Atlas of Top-Level Domain Expansion</em> (Research Release RR2, Post-Reveal Evidence Consolidation). Zenodo. DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.23288603.","researchDoiPending":"Archived research release and verifiable DOI:","researchCitationFile":"Citation metadata (CFF)","researchManifest":"Research release manifest","researchPaperTitle":"Working paper · method and interpretation","researchPaperBody":"Current working paper: {paperVersion}, revised {paperDate}. This independently updated draft has not been peer reviewed. It sets out the research question, sources, methodology and limitations; the immutable RR1 and RR2 deposits remain separately citable.","researchOpenPaper":"Read the working paper","researchDownloadPaper":"Download the working paper","researchArchiveTitle":"Provenance, archives and reproducibility","researchArchiveBody":"The research manifest, GitHub tags, frozen source data and CITATION.cff support independent reproduction. RR2 is the second version in RR1’s original Zenodo concept series. The current site also archives the public APS listing retrieved on 11 October 2026: 1,614 base application IDs and 1,169 replacement records. Full individual application dossiers are not included. The current analyses and the frozen RR1/RR2 releases remain separate.","luganoApplicantValue":"City of Lugano · 2026 applicant","luganoApplicantLabel":"Applicant","luganoApplicationIdLabel":"ICANN application ID","luganoRevealLabel":"Publicly listed since Reveal Day","luganoNotDelegatedValue":"Application published · not delegated","luganoStatusLabel":"Status as of 9 October 2026","luganoWhyTitle":"Why would a city seek its own top-level domain?","luganoWhyIntro":"The interesting question is not whether a city can place its name after a dot. It is what public value, safeguards and services could justify operating a distinct namespace. These are criteria for evaluation, not promises already fulfilled.","luganoWhyRecognitionTitle":"A name people can recognise","luganoWhyRecognitionBody":"A .lugano address could signal a connection with the city, but the suffix alone would not certify its owner. Any trustworthy use would require clear eligibility, verification and anti-impersonation rules.","luganoWhyGovernanceTitle":"Public-interest governance","luganoWhyGovernanceBody":"A civic namespace raises choices about reserved names, fair allocation, security, abuse handling and review of disputed decisions. These choices must be made transparent if the application succeeds; a domain extension does not settle them automatically.","luganoWhyNameTitle":"An address, not an identity certificate","luganoWhyNameBody":"The DNS translates names into technical destinations. It does not, by itself, prove that a service is official or trustworthy. Verification, accessible public registers and clear responsibility could add that layer.","luganoWhyProgressiveTitle":"Build in stages, if approved","luganoWhyProgressiveBody":"A possible sequence starts with a secure registry and transparent registration policy, then adds verified services and interoperable public-interest components. None of these stages is operational merely because the application is public.","luganoSwissTitle":"What Swiss precedents can and cannot tell us","luganoSwissIntro":"Geographic and place-linked TLDs already exist. They help frame questions about eligibility, operation and public identity, but they do not predetermine the governance or the outcome of the .lugano application.","luganoCityPeersTitle":"Cities and territories have taken different paths","luganoCityPeersBody":"Cases such as .berlin, .nyc and .zuerich show that geographic names can enter the DNS under different operators and registration policies. A shared geographic label does not mean a shared governance model.","luganoExploreCitiesCta":"Explore geographic TLDs","luganoZurichTitle":".zuerich: a Swiss point of reference","luganoZurichBody":"The existing .zuerich Registry Agreement is a useful primary source for examining a Swiss geographic TLD. It is a precedent, not evidence that .lugano has been delegated or would use identical rules.","luganoSwissFirstTitle":"Distinctive value, not an invented first","luganoSwissFirstBody":"Because .zuerich already exists, .lugano should not be presented as Switzerland's first geographic TLD. Its potential originality lies in the proposed public-interest use of naming, trust and digital commons, which would still need to be built and assessed.","luganoTwoPathsTitle":"One application, two distinct sources","luganoTwoPathsIntro":"The City of Lugano documents its institutional project. Connecting the Dots studies the same round independently, with disclosed first-hand involvement and a broader historical lens. Neither should be mistaken for the other.","luganoOfficialTitle":"The City's official account","luganoOfficialBody":"For decisions, stated ambitions and authoritative updates on the municipal initiative, consult lugano.ch. This independent atlas does not speak on behalf of the City.","luganoOfficialCta":"Read the official City of Lugano page","luganoContextTitle":"Where .lugano fits in the 2026 round","luganoContextBody":"The application appeared publicly on 7 October 2026. That is a documented procedural milestone, not an award or delegation. The atlas links this case to the worldwide application list and to the rules that still apply.","luganoRoundCta":"See the 2026 round","luganoExplorerCta":"Open .lugano in Explorer","luganoGeographyCta":"Compare cities and territories","economicsRound2000":"A small launch phase: the business model of new generic names was still being tested.","economicsRound2004":"Sponsored applications show how a restricted community can shape a naming market.","economicsRound2012":"The open expansion enabled larger portfolios, brand applications and new registry business models.","economicsRound2026":"The new application landscape includes portfolio strategies, corporate names and emerging technology terms; results remain open.","socialRound2000":"Early applications already raised questions about audiences and public recognition.","socialRound2004":"Community-specific and sponsored approaches linked registration rules to collective identity.","socialRound2012":"Communities, languages and social causes became more visible in a much larger round.","socialRound2026":"New applications reopen questions about inclusion, representation and who is entitled to a name.","contentionRound2000":"Scarce naming opportunities made selection and competing proposals consequential from the outset.","contentionRound2004":"Restricted or sponsored strings still required choices about priority and eligibility.","contentionRound2012":"The large application set made identical-string contention and its resolution central to the round.","contentionRound2026":"Preliminary contention groups are public, but replacement choices and later procedures can still alter the picture.","outcomesRound2000":"Application, selection and delegation were separate steps even in the first expansion.","outcomesRound2004":"Some sponsored proposals advanced; others did not become delegated TLDs.","outcomesRound2012":"The archive records success, withdrawal, rejection and applications that never reached the DNS root.","outcomesRound2026":"Reveal Day exposes applications, not results: evaluation and eventual delegation remain ahead.","disputesRound2000":"Early naming decisions left lasting questions about competing claims and selection criteria.","disputesRound2004":"Sponsored status placed public-interest and community claims under scrutiny.","disputesRound2012":"Objections, reconsiderations and competing applications produced a documented history of disputes.","disputesRound2026":"Future objections and evaluations must not be reported as decided before the relevant records exist.","strangeRound2000":"Even a limited early round offered names with unexpected associations.","strangeRound2004":"Sponsored proposals highlight unusual links between words, sectors and eligibility rules.","strangeRound2012":"A far larger pool brought more unusual, disputed and culturally revealing strings.","strangeRound2026":"The new list is rich in surprises, but an application is not evidence of an operational TLD.","derivedSnapshot":"DERIVED · FROZEN SECONDARY SNAPSHOT","officialAggregatePending":"NOT REPORTED IN THE ICANN AGGREGATE","insideScopeCase":"The .lugano case","insideCityDate":"8 October 2026","insideCityTitle":"Lugano publishes its project information","insideCityBody":"The City publicly explains the purpose and possible governance of .lugano. Its institutional project page is a primary source for the City's stated intentions, not an endorsement of this independent research. The application remains unapproved and undelegated.","insideInputDate":"17 November 2026 – 16 March 2027","insideInputTitle":"Public input and objections: scheduled window","insideInputBody":"ICANN has announced the window for community input, government early warnings and relevant objections. This is a program-wide timetable, not evidence of an objection concerning .lugano.","insideAPSRecordSource":"ICANN APS · .lugano application","insideCityProjectSource":"City of Lugano · Official project page","release0762":"Research and chronology audit: direct ICANN APS evidence for .lugano, institutional publication milestone, public-input dates, reproducibility checks and RR1 citation dates independent of site updates.","release0763":"SEO refinement: concise, distinctive page titles across four languages and a clearer TLD Explorer description. Decorative logo images retain empty alt text to prevent screen-reader duplication.","navHowOverview":"Overview","release0764":"Unified the How it works navigation with the other disclosure menus, preserving all five topic groups and direct chapter links in four languages. Removed the duplicate in-section chip navigation, hardened mobile chapter selection, anchor URLs, Back/Forward and accessibility.","behindNav":"Field Notes","behindMetaTitle":"Field Notes | ICANN 2026 investigations and open cases","behindMetaDescription":"Source-backed observations on ICANN 2026: Reveal Day figures, the .wdo application and unresolved questions.","behindEyebrow":"OBSERVATIONS · ICANN 2026","behindTitle":"Field Notes","behindIntro":"A timeline records milestones. Here we examine what public numbers and sources do not immediately explain: discrepancies, identities, rules and open questions. Each case separates verified facts from documented interpretation and unproven conjecture.","behindCaseNumber":"CASE 01 · REVEAL DAY 2026","behindCaseTitle":"One application missing. Two different questions.","behindCaseLead":"The figures differed by just one, yet the distinction matters. A paid-application count, an official Reveal Day total and a frozen secondary index do not describe exactly the same publication state.","behindStatusPartial":"Partly explained · one question remains open","behindCountPaid":"ICANN, 22 September · paid applications proceeding","behindCountOfficial":"ICANN, 7 October · official Reveal Day applications","behindCountSecondary":"Secondary snapshot of 7 October · observed on 8 October","behindCountPaidValue":"1,616","behindCountOfficialValue":"1,615","behindCountSecondaryValue":"1,614","behindCountSecondaryCaveat":"The 1,614 figure comes from the frozen nTLDData-based index (480 applicants), not an ICANN-published application-level export.","behindExplorerBridge":"The Explorer’s 2026 layer represents 1,608 applications associated with primary strings (980 distinct primary strings), plus six variant-only applications for existing TLDs. Its 1,614 indexed applications are therefore neither 1,614 new strings nor the complete set of 1,615 applications reported by ICANN.","behindDifference1":"1,616 → 1,615","behindDifference2":"1,615 → 1,614","behindExplainedBadge":"Documented omission","behindOpenBadge":"Cause not established","behindExplainedTitle":"The .wdo application: a documented absence from the public list","behindExplainedBody":"ICANN's APS Applications page explicitly says that application WDO2627T-T45217 for .wdo (World Data Organization, China) is still undergoing an administrative check and is therefore absent from the public applications list. The application detail page independently confirms that status. This gives a documented explanation for why the visible list can contain one fewer application than the official aggregate. The independent secondary snapshot is consistent with that omission; its coverage is not being certified as a complete APS export.","behindOpenTitle":"Why did the official count fall before Reveal Day?","behindOpenBody":"On 22 September ICANN reported 1,616 paid applications proceeding. On 7 October the official Reveal Day total was 1,615. The verified public sources reviewed here do not identify which record accounts for the change or why. Withdrawal, administrative rejection, a payment issue or a timing adjustment would be speculation. This question remains open.","behindWhyTitle":"Why should anyone care about a difference of one?","behindWhyBody":"Because a statistic, an application record and a publishable application list are different objects. The exercise shows why researchers must capture the date, scope and source of a number before comparing it, and why public visibility should never be confused with acceptance, approval or eventual delegation.","behindMethodTitle":"Evidence, not speculation","behindMethodBody":"The 1,616 and 1,615 totals are direct ICANN statements; 1,614 is a time-stamped secondary index preserved by this project. The APS footnote documents .wdo's omission, but does not explain the earlier change in ICANN totals. The two gaps are therefore tracked separately. This episode is a corrigible editorial observation, not a revision of the archived RR1 dataset.","behindSourcesTitle":"Check the evidence","behindReviewDateLabel":"Last editorial review","behindUpdateTitle":"What would close the remaining question?","behindUpdateBody":"A dated ICANN explanation connecting the September and October totals, a change to the .wdo administrative-check record, or a public list that can be reconciled application by application. Any substantive correction will be dated, sourced and documented in the project history.","behindIndependenceTitle":"An independent observation","behindIndependenceBody":"The author participates professionally in the City of Lugano's .lugano application, but this research is independent of the City's institutional communication. Only public evidence and attributable interpretations are included; no confidential application information is used.","behindStandaloneCta":"Read the independent field note","behindChronicleCta":"Back to the process timeline","behindSourcesPreface":"Primary sources are labeled separately from the secondary index and project snapshot.","behindSourceSep":"ICANN · 22 Sep announcement","behindSourceReveal":"ICANN · Reveal Day press release","behindSourceStats":"ICANN APS · Statistics","behindSourceList":"ICANN APS · Applications list and footnote","behindSourceWdo":"ICANN APS · .wdo application","behindSourceSecondary":"nTLDData · Secondary index","behindSourceManifest":"Connecting the Dots · Frozen metadata","release0765":"Adds Behind the Round: a four-language, source-backed and independently indexable field-note series beginning with the two distinct Reveal Day count discrepancies, plus reviewable source provenance and open-question update triggers. RR1 remains immutable.","behindRevisionsTitle":"Dated editorial revisions","behindRevisionInitial":"First publication: separated the two numerical discrepancies; verified the APS administrative-check footnote and explicitly left the earlier official-count change unresolved.","release0766":"Search-metadata polish for the four Behind the Round articles: shorter descriptions and a proper Article image reference for Google rich-result eligibility. Editorial evidence remains unchanged.","release0767":"Renames the ICANN 2026 submenus to Timeline and Field Notes in all four languages, with consistent page headings, SEO titles, research references and stable permalinks. Dataset RR1, the original evidence and DOI remain unchanged.","behindRegionalTitle":"The regional discrepancy: a second check","behindRegionalLead":"Only Asia-Pacific differs between the official Reveal Day application counts and the project's preserved secondary index. The applicant count also differs by one in precisely that region.","behindRegionHeader":"Region","behindRegionOfficial":"ICANN","behindRegionVisible":"Index","behindRegionDelta":"Gap","behindRegionNA":"North America","behindRegionEUR":"Europe","behindRegionAP":"Asia-Pacific","behindRegionAF":"Africa","behindRegionLAC":"Latin America & Caribbean","behindRegionalConclusion":"Across five regions, official totals add to 1,615 applications from 481 applicants; the secondary index has 1,614 from 480. The .wdo applicant is in China. The regional match supports the APS footnote but does not replace a one-to-one reconciliation of all application IDs.","behindIndependentTitle":"An independent researcher's CSV check","behindIndependentBody":"On 8 October, Stéphane Bortzmeyer examined the public CSV: 2,783 total rows, 1,614 applications when replacement-string rows are removed, and 480 applicants. Exported rows, applications and requested strings are not interchangeable because one application can yield several rows. The independent check corroborates the visible shortfall but does not explain the earlier change in the official total.","behindCoverageTitle":"A widely reported explanation is not a complete reconciliation","behindCoverageBody":"CircleID placed the .wdo administrative check alongside the change from 1,616 to 1,615. In its 8 October notice JPNIC similarly described 1,616 paid applications, 1,615 published and one under administrative check. Yet APS already reports 1,615 active in its statistics while the independent public index identifies 1,614. These accounts are relevant interpretations, not an ID-by-ID reconciliation proving which application caused the earlier official decrease.","behindRevisionReconciled":"Second revision: added a region-by-region check, independent CSV analysis and a distinction between CircleID's interpretation and what can actually be reconciled. The 1,616 → 1,615 change remains unresolved.","behindSourceRegionalSecondary":"nTLDData · Applications by region","behindSourceBortzmeyer":"S. Bortzmeyer · CSV analysis","behindSourceCircleID":"CircleID · Reveal Day report","behindSourceAPSHelp":"ICANN APS · Public-list rules","wdoCaseNumber":"CASE 02 · NAMES AND PRIOR RIGHTS","wdoCaseTitle":"Two organizations, one acronym: who may seek .wdo?","wdoCaseLead":"The .wdo applicant is the World Data Organization, launched in Beijing in 2026. But the World Design Organization dates back to 1957, uses WDO and holds a registered trademark. Is there already a legal dispute? Public evidence does not establish one.","wdoCaseStatus":"Shared acronym documented · No verified dispute","wdoIdentityTitle":"First distinction: they are not the same organization","wdoIdentityBody":"ICANN names World Data Organization, located in China, as the applicant for .wdo under WDO2627T-T45217, still undergoing an administrative check. World Design Organization is a separate Montréal-based body involved in industrial design and using wdo.org. Sharing the initials WDO proves no corporate or institutional connection between them.","wdoOriginsTitle":"Two histories, almost seventy years apart","wdoOriginsBody":"World Data Organization was launched in Beijing on 30 March 2026; the Chinese Academy of Sciences reports that Tan Tieniu was elected council chair. World Design Organization was founded in 1957 as Icsid and adopted its current name in 2015. The application therefore concerns initials with an established public history, but that history alone cannot determine entitlement to the TLD.","wdoTrademarkTitle":"A verifiable earlier right is not an automatic veto","wdoTrademarkBody":"Canada's CIPO registry records the word mark WORLD DESIGN ORGANIZATION (WDO), filed on 23 October 2015 and registered as TMA1008130 on 2 November 2018, with active registration. Trademark protection has a defined scope: it does not automatically confer ownership of the .wdo top-level domain, nor prove that the organization has filed an objection.","wdoProcessTitle":"What does the ICANN procedure allow?","wdoProcessBody":"The 2026 Applicant Guidebook permits a Legal Rights Objection by a party with standing claiming infringement of existing rights, backed by evidence. ICANN announced a public-input and objections window running from 17 November 2026 to 16 March 2027. Possible standing is a starting point, not an adjudicated claim. As of 10 October, no objection from World Design Organization concerning .wdo has been verified.","wdoComplianceTitle":"Compliance context, not an explanation for the check","wdoComplianceBody":"Another documented fact concerns Tan Tieniu: the Chinese Academy of Sciences identifies him as a World Data Organization leader, while a 17 July 2026 US OFAC update lists him under Hong Kong Autonomy Act measures on the Non-SDN Menu-Based Sanctions List. That entry concerns an individual, not proof that World Data Organization is itself designated. No ICANN source connects it to the administrative check of .wdo; a causal claim would be speculation.","wdoUnknownTitle":"What remains unknown","wdoUnknownBody":"ICANN has not published the reason for the administrative check on WDO2627T-T45217. There is no verified objection by World Design Organization, refusal, established infringement, or decision on the merits of the application. Neither the new organization's age, another body's trademark nor a leader's sanctions record can be used to infer the cause.","wdoFollowTitle":"How this case will be followed","wdoFollowBody":"A revision will require new primary evidence: an APS status change, published comments or objections involving .wdo, official statements by either WDO, a decision affecting the name, or a relevant OFAC change. Each change will be dated and sourced. Research must not invent a dispute before one exists.","wdoRevisionInitial":"First publication: documented the two distinct WDO organizations, the Canadian trademark, ICANN procedures and OFAC's entry concerning a leader, without attributing any cause to the administrative check.","wdoArticleCta":"Read the standalone case","wdoArticleReturn":"All Field Notes","wdoMetaTitle":"Two WDOs and the .wdo domain | Field Notes","wdoMetaDescription":"Two organizations share WDO. ICANN, CAS and Canadian trademark evidence; possible rights, unproven objections and explicit research limits.","wdoSourcePreface":"Primary institutional sources and registers. The overlap in names is documented; any consequences for the application remain unestablished.","wdoSourceAPS":"ICANN APS · .wdo application","wdoSourceCAS":"Chinese Academy of Sciences · WDO launch","wdoSourceDesignAbout":"World Design Organization · About","wdoSourceTrademark":"Canada CIPO · WDO trademark","wdoSourceGuidebook":"ICANN · 2026 Applicant Guidebook","wdoSourceCalendar":"ICANN · 2026 objections calendar","wdoSourceOFAC":"US OFAC · 17 July 2026 update","release0768":"Expands the Reveal Day count case with regional reconciliation, independent CSV analysis and sourced interpretation; adds a second Field Note on the two WDO organizations, with primary evidence, explicit uncertainties and a standalone article in four languages. RR1 and DOI unchanged.","behindRegionTotal":"Total","wdoIdentityDataLabel":"Beijing · established 2026 · .wdo applicant","wdoIdentityDesignLabel":"Montréal · founded 1957 · wdo.org","behindSourceJPNIC":"JPNIC · 8 October official note","sanctionsContextCta":"Sanctions & the DNS: what ICANN can and cannot do","release0769":"Added an independently indexed, four-language explainer on OFAC and the DNS, with primary sources, case distinctions and cross-links from both 2026 field notes.","accessCrosslinkText":"Explore who controls Internet access","accessCrosslinkTitle":"Can a working domain still be blocked?","accessCrosslinkIntro":"DNS filtering, suspended domains, network shutdowns and ICANN's role explained through an interactive example.","release0770":"Adds a four-language Internet access explainer with seven interactive blocking scenarios, five historically sourced country/regional cases, and careful distinctions between DNS, TLDs, ISPs and ICANN.","release0771":"Refines the Iran case to reflect OONI's qualified measurements and documents the distinction between registry-level .cat redirection and local ISP filtering in Catalonia (2017).","field03Number":"Field Note 03 · Procedure","field03Title":"When the safeguards' own recruitment system failed","field03Lead":"After ICANN acknowledged technical failures in the selection process, three Independent Objectors were appointed on 23 October. The impact of those failures on individual respondents remains unquantified.","field03Status":"Three appointments confirmed · individual impact unquantified","field03Section1Title":"An inbox that could not receive","field03Section1Body":"On 30 June 2026 ICANN reported that the dedicated email inbox for Expressions of Interest from potential Independent Objectors had been configured to reject messages from external senders. The defect was discovered following a respondent's report. ICANN fixed the configuration but said the earlier messages could not be recovered.","field03Section2Title":"The portal closed 24 hours early","field03Section2Body":"ICANN reports that the EOI portal inadvertently closed on 30 May 2026, describing this as 24 hours before the deadline. The same notice identifies 1 June as the previously extended deadline. Without precise closing times and time zones, the calendar dates and stated 24-hour interval cannot be independently reconciled. Several parties were prevented from submitting; the technical cause remained unidentified in the 30 June report.","field03Section3Title":"Corrective actions and unanswered questions","field03Section3Body":"ICANN notified interested parties, offered an alternative submission channel and later kept the EOI open until all Independent Objector roles were filled. The public record does not establish how many emails were lost, whether anyone ultimately failed to participate or the cause of the early portal shutdown.","field03Section4Title":"Why this is relevant to the 2026 round","field03Section4Body":"Independent Objectors may raise Community or Limited Public Interest objections without representing a private applicant. Failures in their recruitment process therefore matter for procedural accessibility and trust. They do not prove a fault in TAMS, an altered Reveal Day application count, or misconduct in the evaluation of TLD applications.","field03SourceIncident":"ICANN · 30 June: reported EOI incidents","field03SourceExtension":"ICANN · 18 June: extended EOI deadline","field03SourceRole":"ICANN · role and independence of the Objectors","field03Revision":"Initial evidence review: ICANN incident notice, EOI extension and Objector mandate.","field04Number":"Field Note 04 · Counting","field04Title":"1,169 replacement strings: the extra rows behind Reveal Day","field04Lead":"A CSV can have more rows than applications. Alternative strings, early deactivation labels and variant-only records explain several numbers that look contradictory when read without their definitions.","field04Status":"Counted secondary records · individual deactivation reasons still require checking","field04MetricTotal":"Replacement-string rows recorded in the secondary index","field04MetricActive":"Replacement rows marked active in that index","field04MetricDeactivated":"Replacement rows marked deactivated in that index","field04ValueTotal":"1,169","field04ValueActive":"442","field04ValueDeactivated":"727","field04Section1Title":"2,783 rows do not mean 2,783 applications","field04Section1Body":"Stéphane Bortzmeyer's extraction counted 2,783 CSV records, but 1,614 application records after excluding replacement strings. The difference of 1,169 corresponds to the alternatives recorded by internet.Best. APS explicitly explains that a primary record and its replacement can appear separately while counting as one application in the official statistics. The official 1,615 figure remains a distinct source and is not silently replaced by 1,614.","field04Section2Title":"727 deactivated alternatives: what the label does not prove","field04Section2Body":"In its Reveal Day reconstruction, internet.Best marks 442 replacement rows active and 727 deactivated. The figures describe alternative records, not 727 rejected main applications. ICANN defines 'Deactivated' as a final application-record status but says that non-selected replacement strings are deactivated after String Confirmation Day. The historical secondary labels therefore need row-level and timestamp checks before we attribute reasons or dates to every record.","field04Section3Title":"980 primary strings and a secondary list of 986","field04Section3Body":"The project's frozen nTLDData string inventory contains 980 distinct primary strings associated with 1,608 applications. Six additional secondary-index applications are variant-only applications for existing TLDs, outside that primary-string inventory. internet.Best reports 986 distinct strings under its own extraction. The numerical match 980 + 6 = 986 is suggestive but not, by itself, a verified one-to-one reconciliation of string identities; that requires checking individual application IDs.","field04Section4Title":"The other gap: 47 submitted applications","field04Section4Body":"ICANN reported 1,663 submitted applications after the 12 August deadline and 1,616 paid applications proceeding on 22 September. Thus 47 submissions are outside the later paid-and-proceeding total. This is a change in eligibility and accounting stage, not evidence of 47 formal rejections. It also does not answer why the published figure shifted again from 1,616 to 1,615 on Reveal Day.","field04Section5Title":"An irreversible choice before 21 October","field04Section5Body":"The replacement window runs 8–21 October 2026, with string confirmation scheduled for 17 November. Eligible applicants may elect an alternative only once; they cannot reverse that choice. An alternative identical to another proposed primary or replacement string is ineligible, and the Guidebook restricts coordination among competing applicants. Final outcomes must not be inferred from Reveal Day status labels.","field04SourceAugust":"ICANN · application window closure","field04SourceSeptember":"ICANN · 1,616 paid applications","field04SourceReveal":"ICANN · official Reveal Day statistics","field04SourceAPS":"ICANN APS · how primary and replacement rows are counted","field04SourceTimeline":"ICANN · 8–21 October replacement period","field04SourceGuide":"Applicant Guidebook · replacement string rules","field04SourceIndex":"internet.Best · secondary application and replacement list","field04SourceBortzmeyer":"Stéphane Bortzmeyer · CSV row analysis","field04SourceSnapshot":"Project · frozen 2026 snapshot","field04Revision":"Initial source reconciliation of primary records, replacement rows, variants, deactivation labels and dates.","fieldSourceDisclaimer":"Source classification: ICANN primary documentation, attributed secondary indexes and a frozen project-derived snapshot. Reported totals use different units; explanations beyond the documents remain hypotheses.","fieldCaseCollectionIntro":"Field investigations · 2026","fieldCaseIndex":"Field note index","release0774":"Adds two documented 2026 Field Notes on Independent Objector recruitment failures and replacement-string counts, with sources and explicit uncertainties. Extends the 2026 economics, competing-string, namespace and community examples without changing archived RR1.","release0773":"Clarifies the Explorer’s 1,608 primary-string applications plus six variant-only records, specifies the .wdo public-list exclusion and uses localized number grouping in Field Notes across four languages.","release0772":"Repairs the .lugano HTML, hidden Explorer drawer keyboard focus, adaptive navigation menus, light/dark contrast and publication metadata. Keeps long Explorer coverage details accessible without hiding facts. Separates the registry precondition from the network visit in the access tutorial.","guideRelatedTitle":"Related explanations","guideRelatedBody":"These are general explanations, not new cases from the 2026 application round. They clarify what happens beyond the TLD and what decisions ICANN does or does not make.","guideAccessCta":"Internet filtering, DNS and shutdowns","guideSanctionsCta":"International sanctions and ICANN","field03MetaDescription":"ICANN appointed three Independent Objectors on 23 October after technical problems in the selection process. Their individual impact remains unclear.","field04MetaDescription":"How 1,169 replacement-string rows relate to 1,614 indexed applications, 980 primary strings and ICANN's 1,615 official Reveal Day total.","field04Disclosure":"Source transparency: internet.Best is itself a 2026 applicant for .socialmedia. Its figures are treated as secondary evidence, not as an ICANN-certified reconciliation.","fieldReadInvestigation":"Read the full investigation","economics2026DeepTitle":"The competition hidden inside 980 strings","economics2026DeepBody":"The 263 preliminary identical-string contention sets contain 891 applications. That is 55.2% of the secondary index's 1,614 applications, although the 263 contested strings are only 26.8% of the 980 primary strings in the frozen nTLDData inventory. In internet.Best's applicant ranking, the five largest portfolios account for 491 applications, 30.4% of its 1,614-record base. Contention is not an awarded TLD, and a filed application is not a revenue forecast.","socialMeow2026Title":".meow: crowdfunding the infrastructure","socialMeow2026Body":"The nonprofit dotMeow Foundation raised €121,896 in Kickstarter pledges from 1,838 backers for its proposed .meow registry, against a €80,000 goal. The sum is a crowdfunding campaign result, not an established operating budget, a delegated TLD or proof of ICANN approval. The project illustrates community financing of the naming layer without being automatically equivalent to ICANN's formal Community application category.","disputes2026CaseTitle":"Three 2026 examples: .anime, .agent and .official","disputes2026CaseBody":"The preliminary identical-string sets include .agent (13 applications), .official (8) and .anime (2). The two .anime applicants are Toei Animation, which filed as Community, and Oinkadot. That contrast raises questions about cultural representation and business operation, but neither an objection outcome nor a winner has been determined. A name such as .official also raises trust questions; the string itself grants no public authority and is not evidence of deceptive intent.","howBit2026Title":"One word, two naming systems: .bit","howBit2026Body":"The frozen 2026 secondary index shows 11 ICANN applications for .bit. Namecoin already uses .bit in a separate blockchain-based naming system. It is not a delegated TLD in the ICANN global DNS root and ordinary DNS resolvers do not automatically use Namecoin's records. This is a namespace collision risk to explain, not a claim that Namecoin is one of the 11 applicants.","researchPriorTitle":"Earlier research release · RR1","researchPriorBody":"RR1 · Reveal Day 2026 was published on 9 October 2026 and remains independently citable under 10.5281/zenodo.23262623. RR1 and RR2 belong to the same version series, with concept DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23262622. Cite the exact version used, not the latest one indiscriminately.","researchPriorLink":"View the original RR1 on Zenodo","researchConceptLink":"All archived versions · concept DOI","release0775":"Publishes the verified RR2 scientific DOI and its shared Zenodo concept series alongside RR1. Updates research citations and metadata in four languages, preserves both immutable scientific versions, and identifies String Confirmation as a future RR3 milestone.","cyclesTitle":"Economic cycles: what applied-for strings reveal (and what they do not)","cyclesLead":"The dot-com bubble, platform expansion, crypto boom-and-bust cycles and current AI investment frenzy frame successive moments of demand for names. They are different waves, not one inevitable path from a popular term to a profitable registry.","cyclesDotcomTitle":"The dot-com bubble: the web before the new rounds","cyclesDotcomBody":"The speculative surge and crash around 2000 occurred before the 2012 mass gTLD application round. Its legacy is a useful warning: a boom in companies, websites or domain prices does not prove that a new top-level registry will succeed.","cyclesCryptoTitle":"Crypto: more than one market winter","cyclesCryptoBody":"Between the 2012 and 2026 rounds, crypto experienced repeated booms and contractions. A 2026 string application may signal renewed interest, but does not measure blockchain adoption, registry demand or an applicant's financial resilience.","cyclesAiTitle":"AI: a different point in the cycle","cyclesAiBody":"Generative AI is still undergoing rapid commercialization. The editorial 2026 inventory includes AI-labelled applications, while the 2012 editorial comparison shows none in that category. Different taxonomies and the long gap require caution: names are not economic forecasts.","cyclesPlatformsTitle":"E-commerce, social networks and the physical economy","cyclesPlatformsBody":"Platforms can create demand for recognisable identities without needing a TLD of their own. Conversely, banking, manufacturing, logistics, retail and public institutions may seek branded or sector-restricted namespaces as infrastructure rather than as speculative real estate.","cyclesCaveat":"Method: this is a historical interpretation of sectors and digital-market cycles, not proof of causation. Do not equate an application with delegation, actual registry operation, second-level registrations or financial success.","cemeteryChapterNav":"The gTLD cemetery","cemeteryChapterCardDesc":"Ended delegations, registry changes and projects never entering the root, with their distinct outcomes kept clear.","cemeteryEconomicsTeaser":"The economic costs of running a registry also illuminate exits and failed projects. For evidence, chronology and distinct outcomes, continue to the dedicated chapter.","cemeteryOpenChapter":"Explore the gTLD cemetery →","cemeteryTitle":"The gTLD cemetery","cemeteryLead":"A signed registry agreement is not immortality. Some strings never reach delegation; others are delegated and subsequently revoked. Termination of an agreement may also lead to transfer to a successor instead of disappearance from the root.","cemeteryDoosan":"Doosan notified ICANN in September 2015 that it intended to terminate the .doosan registry agreement; ICANN issued a termination notice on 1 March 2016. A corporate TLD can be abandoned even after obtaining its own namespace.","cemeteryLupin":"ICANN's termination register records the operator-initiated .lupin termination and revocation of its delegation on 10 December 2020. This is a documented post-delegation exit, not merely an unsuccessful application.","cemeteryWed":"ICANN terminated the .wed registry agreement in October 2020; the subsequent determination was to transition its operation. Termination of an operator and death of a string must therefore not be conflated.","cemeteryTaxonomyTitle":"Four distinct outcomes","cemeteryTaxonomy":"Withdrawn or rejected application; signed contract without delegation; delegated TLD later revoked; registry operator replaced while the TLD persists. These categories must not be merged into a single mortality count.","cemeteryCaveat":"These are documented examples, not an exhaustive mortality census. A reproducible global graveyard requires reconciling ICANN registry agreement histories, termination determinations and dated IANA root records before publishing any total.","historicalPackNote":"Historical data pack v0.7.58 (9 October 2026), not the latest scientific release. For current RR2 evidence use its specific DOI.","release0776":"Independent audit repair: moved economic cycles and the gTLD cemetery inside Economics, corrected mobile layout in four languages, labelled historical ZIP v0.7.58 separately from current CSVs, reconciled RR2 citations and added Explorer/navigation regression tests.","currentDataPackNote":"Technical snapshot v{version} · SHA-256 available beside ZIP · separate from the archived Zenodo research releases","downloadApsOfficial":"ICANN 2026 · official public application list","downloadApsOfficialSub":"Official CSV · 2,783 rows / 1,614 base application IDs + 1,169 -R replacements; .wdo pending. Not individual PDFs.","downloadPackArchive":"Historical data pack v0.7.58","release0777":"Official 2026 ICANN APS Download CSV integrated: 2,783 published rows, 1,614 base application IDs, 1,169 -R records and the administrative-check .wdo gap. Versioned and SHA-256 checked v0.7.77 technical ZIP; preserved frozen v0.7.58 and scholarly RR1/RR2.","downloadPackChecksum":"Current ZIP SHA-256 checksum","downloadPackChecksumSub":"Text · independently verify the integrity of the complete site data pack","downloadApsProvenance":"Official APS data provenance","downloadApsProvenanceSub":"JSON · source URL, 34 columns, retrieval date, official disclaimer and both CSV SHA-256 hashes","apsAtlasTitle":"ICANN 2026 Application Atlas","apsAtlasIntro":"From TLD labels to individual applications: each published official application, its primary string, replacement option, variants, applicant and documented status observations.","apsAtlasApps":"Published applications (base IDs)","apsAtlasReplacements":"Replacement-string records","apsAtlasStrings":"Distinct new primary strings","apsAtlasOverlap":"Identical-primary-string groups","apsAtlasOverlapHint":"The 263 exact original-primary-string groups involve 891 applications. These are derived preliminary matches, NOT ICANN's final contention sets. Replacement choices, similar strings and variants are separate dimensions. An APS «In Contention = FALSE» label does not prove absence of contention before String Evaluation.","apsAtlasMethod":"Methods, coverage and limitations","apsAtlasWdo":"One of ICANN's 1,615 applications is absent from the public listing: .wdo is undergoing an administrative check.","apsAtlasSearch":"Search applications","apsAtlasSearchPlaceholder":"Application ID, string, applicant or country…","apsAtlasFilterType":"TLD type","apsAtlasFilterRole":"Relationship","apsAtlasFilterRegion":"ICANN region","apsAtlasAll":"All","apsAtlasBrand":"Brand","apsAtlasCommunity":"Community","apsAtlasGeo":"Geographic","apsAtlasIdn":"IDN","apsAtlasVariant":"With variants","apsAtlasHasReplacement":"Has replacement string","apsAtlasExactMatch":"Shared original primary string","apsAtlasExistingVariant":"Existing-TLD variant","apsAtlasReset":"Reset filters","apsAtlasResults":"Application results","apsAtlasMore":"Load more applications","apsAtlasDetails":"View details and evidence","apsAtlasApplicant":"Applicant","apsAtlasApplicationId":"Application ID","apsAtlasBaseStatus":"Primary-record status","apsAtlasProcessingStage":"Processing stage","apsAtlasReplacement":"Replacement","apsAtlasReplacementStatus":"Replacement-record status","apsAtlasVariants":"Declared variants","apsAtlasTypes":"Application type","apsAtlasPublicDetail":"Official APS summary","apsAtlasResponses":"Public responses","apsAtlasDocuments":"Documents and PDFs at ICANN","apsAtlasExplorer":"View in TLD Explorer","apsAtlasSourceDate":"Official CSV retrieved","apsAtlasSource":"Official ICANN APS public listing","apsAtlasDownloadGraph":"Download application graph","apsAtlasDownloadEdges":"Application–string relationships (CSV)","apsAtlasDownloadHistory":"Observed status history (CSV)","apsAtlasDownloadManifest":"Corpus manifest and provenance","apsAtlasBack":"Back to Explorer","apsAtlasResearch":"Research and citations","apsAtlasNoResults":"No applications match the selected filters.","apsAtlasNoJs":"JavaScript is required for interactive search. Original records remain downloadable:","apsAtlasError":"The application corpus is unavailable or failed validation.","apsAtlasScope":"2,783 public ICANN APS rows normalized to 1,614 distinct applications. The 1,169 -R rows are alternatives, not extra applications. Six applications concern variants of existing TLDs only. .wdo is withheld from the public list during administrative review. Official individual APS pages are linked, not fully downloaded or checked document by document.","apsAtlasWdoLink":"Read official notice","apsAtlasPreliminaryList":"ICANN's preliminary Reveal Day contention list","apsAtlasPermalink":"Link to this application","apsAtlasMatchesCount":"{count} applications for the same string","apsAtlasWithout":"No replacement","apsAtlasPagination":"Incremental results","apsAtlasRegionAll":"All regions","apsAtlasNone":"None","apsAtlasCountOf":"Showing {shown} of {total} applications","apsAtlasObservedStatusNote":"APS statuses and labels are reported as observed at retrieval; they do not establish future outcomes, final string choices or adjudicated contention.","apsAtlasFooter":"Public atlas based on the ICANN APS export. Derived editorial analysis is labelled separately.","apsAtlasOpen":"Explore applications","apsAtlasCtaTitle":"From a TLD to the underlying applications","apsAtlasCtaBody":"Explore all 1,614 published ICANN 2026 applications, IDs, -R replacement records, declared variants and exact-label matches, with links to official APS public files. Applications are not delegated TLDs.","release0778":"ICANN 2026 Application Atlas: a verifiable graph of 1,614 public applications, 1,169 -R options, 13 variant edges and 263 derived identical-original-string groups, with four-language application browsing, official APS links and a source-dated status observation log.","iaProjectQuestion":"What is this project?","iaProjectDefinition":"An atlas of what lies behind the dots on the Internet. From the history of TLDs to ICANN applications, from stories to primary sources, it connects accessible explanations with verifiable data.","iaPrimaryLabel":"The six paths through Connecting the Dots","iaNavUnderstand":"Understand","iaNavExplore":"Explore","iaNavStories":"Stories & issues","iaNavRound":"2026 round","iaNavResearch":"Research","iaLegacySummary":"All sections and direct shortcuts","iaLegacyNote":"All 21 original sections remain available, including chapters, tools and deep links.","iaHomeLaunchTitle":"From curiosity to evidence","iaHomeLaunchLead":"The six sections below connect explanations with cases, tools and sources.","iaHubUnderstandEyebrow":"FOUNDATIONS","iaHubUnderstandTitle":"Understand the Internet, DNS and TLDs","iaHubUnderstandLead":"Begin with simple concepts, then dive into how domains work, the rules and the people who can intervene.","iaHubExploreEyebrow":"TOOLS AND HISTORY","iaHubExploreTitle":"Explore names, applications and data","iaHubExploreLead":"Search for a TLD, a proposed string or a formal application: related objects, not synonyms. Then explore who can act on the DNS and on Internet access.","iaHubStoriesEyebrow":"PEOPLE AND POWER","iaHubStoriesTitle":"Stories and issues","iaHubStoriesLead":"Territories, markets, communities and curiosities: what TLDs mean in the real world.","iaHubRoundEyebrow":"THE ONGOING PROCESS","iaHubRoundTitle":"Follow the ICANN 2026 round","iaHubRoundLead":"Dated statistics, timeline, field observations and the .lugano case. Verified events are kept separate from open questions.","iaHubResearchEyebrow":"SOURCES AND METHOD","iaHubResearchTitle":"Verifiable, citable research","iaHubResearchLead":"Papers, datasets, methods and archived releases. The site's technical version is separate from scientific releases.","iaB2AboutRole":"An independent research and education project, not a domain sales service or an official position of ICANN or the City of Lugano.","iaB2MethodHint":"Summaries and analysis do not replace sources. Facts, interpretations and visions are distinguished.","iaB2ObjectNote":"A requested string, a formal application (Application ID) and a delegated TLD are distinct. A proposed string does not automatically exist in the DNS root.","iaB2RoundNote":"Reveal Day and the 11 October 2026 public APS listing snapshot: 1,615 applications in ICANN's aggregate, 1,614 published base Application IDs and 2,783 rows including -R alternatives. Interpret discrepancies using sources and observation dates.","iaB2RrLabel":"Version-specific DOI for scientific release RR2","iaB2RrNote":"RR2 is a frozen scientific release, separate from the regularly updated technical website.","iaCardOverviewDesc":"A guided walkthrough from the browser to the DNS root, suitable for beginners.","iaCardHowDesc":"Who decides, who operates and how registrations, applications and lifecycles work.","iaCardBeyondDesc":"The infrastructure, naming and access issues behind a simple extension.","iaCardAccessTitle":"Who controls Internet access?","iaCardAccessDesc":"DNS filtering, network blocks and domain suspension are different mechanisms.","iaCardSanctionsTitle":"Sanctions and DNS","iaCardSanctionsDesc":"How legal obligations interact with the governance of a global system.","iaCardGlossaryTitle":"Glossary and key terms","iaCardGlossaryDesc":"Working definitions to distinguish strings, TLDs, registries and applications.","iaCardExplorerDesc":"Search TLD records, events, categories, states and profiles in the interactive catalogue.","iaCardArchaeologyDesc":"Application history across the 2000, 2004, 2012 and 2026 rounds, distinct from the current round's timeline.","iaCardAtlasTitle":"2026 Application Atlas","iaCardAtlasDesc":"Official APS applications with Application IDs, applicants and replacement strings.","iaCardCitiesDesc":"Geographic extensions and public responsibility.","iaCardGeographyDesc":"Operators, territories and the concentration of power in the DNS.","iaCardEconomicsDesc":"Economic value, business models and the domain industry's development cycles.","iaCardSocialDesc":"Languages, communities, identity and the social meaning of extensions.","iaCardContentionDesc":"What happens when more than one applicant requests the same string.","iaCardOutcomesDesc":"Withdrawn or rejected applications and proposals never delegated. For what happens to TLDs later, see the Cemetery.","iaCardDisputesDesc":"Objections and governance disputes, distinct from contention mechanisms.","iaCardStrangeDesc":"Oddities, exceptions and surprising stories from the DNS.","iaCardRevealDesc":"Reveal Day figures with dates, units and limitations.","iaCardInsideDesc":"The 2026 round timeline: documented events and milestones, never forecasts presented as fact.","iaCardBehindDesc":"Field notes, open questions and ongoing verification.","iaCardLuganoDesc":"An application documented from within as one round case, without predicting an outcome.","iaCardResearchDesc":"Scientific identity, RR1/RR2 releases and citation guidance.","iaCardPaperDesc":"The working paper, research questions, methods and limitations.","iaCardResourcesDesc":"Datasets, packages and technical resources.","iaCardSourcesDesc":"Provenance, methods, revisions and fact/reading distinctions.","release0779":"v0.7.79: B2 navigation redesign with purpose-led Home, five reader journeys and integrated article shell; previous content, research evidence and DOI archives preserved.","helsinkiBridgeTitle":".helsinki: a city can also give up its TLD","helsinkiBridgeBody":"In 2026 Helsinki initiated the voluntary termination of its registry agreement. This is a concrete counterpoint for anyone considering a city TLD: securing a delegation and sustaining it over time are different decisions.","helsinkiBridgeCta":"Read the documented timeline and status","helsinkiCaseState":"Final ICANN non-transition decision · still delegated","helsinkiCaseHistory":"The City of Helsinki applied for .helsinki in the 2012 round. IANA records the TLD as registered on 10 December 2015 and documents delegation in a report dated 21 January 2016. The city's official registry website described the original goal of making municipal services easier to identify.","helsinkiCaseFact":"On 21 October 2026 ICANN published its final determination not to transition .helsinki to a successor registry operator. The City of Helsinki notified voluntary termination on 28 May 2026; the preliminary determination was issued on 19 August. This concerns the succession process, not completed removal from the DNS root.","helsinkiCaseStatus":"Verified 25 October 2026: ICANN has decided not to transfer operations to a successor. IANA still lists .helsinki as Active and delegated to the City of Helsinki. Revocation and termination are not yet confirmed.","helsinkiCaseReading":"The decision offers a documented case of a city gTLD entering the termination process, but the public primary sources do not establish the city's economic motives. A final non-transition decision is not the same as completed revocation.","helsinkiSourceIcann":"ICANN · termination proceedings","helsinkiSourceIana":"IANA · .helsinki delegation","helsinkiSourceCity":"City of Helsinki · original rationale","release0781":"v0.7.81: Explore groups DNS geography, Internet access controls and sanctions in one governance pathway, separate from data tools; chapter URLs, cards and RR2 remain unchanged.","release0780":"v0.7.80: adds the documented voluntary termination case of .helsinki, separating notice, preliminary determination and continued IANA delegation; content, URLs and RR2 remain intact.","econEditorialTitle2026":"Words are a market. Not yet an outcome.","econEditorialIntro2026":"In the 2026 round, competition for identical strings reveals applicants' economic expectations. Filing does not mean winning a TLD, and contention is not a direct measure of future demand.","econEditorialMarketTitle":"Scarcity forms around a word","econEditorialMarketBody":"Our analysis of the 7 October APS snapshot finds 263 exact-primary-string groups comprising 891 applications. These are analytical groups, not ICANN's definitive contention sets. Replacements and subsequent evaluations can change the picture.","econEditorialPortfolioTitle":"Intercap: applying as a portfolio strategy","econEditorialPortfolioBody":"Domain Name Wire (7 October) counts 71 Intercap applications: 58 strings in contention and 13 uncontested. Our comparison of original primary strings in the 11 October APS snapshot gives 54 with exact matches and 17 without: a derived, unofficial count. This is a portfolio strategy, not completed acquisitions.","econEditorialIdentityTitle":"Brands, products and AI agent identities: different logics","econEditorialIdentityBody":"ICANN reports 333 brand applications on Reveal Day. A corporate brand, a product name and a possible digital-agent identity are different strategies. Using TLDs as trust infrastructure for AI agents remains a hypothesis, not an established result.","econEditorialExploreContests":"Explore contention →","econEditorialDisclaimer":"Editorial analysis dated 14 October 2026. ICANN statistics, locally derived groups and third-party Intercap figures have different evidentiary status and must not be conflated.","iaStoryGroupSocietyTitle":"TLDs in society","iaStoryGroupSocietyLead":"How domains shape cities, markets and communities.","iaStoryGroupConflictsTitle":"Conflicts and disputes","iaStoryGroupConflictsLead":"When applicants compete for a string or rights and interests collide.","iaStoryGroupLifecyclesTitle":"Outcomes and lifecycles","iaStoryGroupLifecyclesLead":"From applications never delegated to TLDs that ended or changed operators.","iaStoryGroupOdditiesTitle":"The unusual Internet","iaStoryGroupOdditiesLead":"Anomalies, exceptions and paradoxes in Internet naming.","iaNavOutcomes":"Conflicts & outcomes","iaHubOutcomesEyebrow":"RULES IN PRACTICE","iaHubOutcomesTitle":"Conflicts & outcomes","iaHubOutcomesLead":"From competing applications to the fate of extensions: objections, decisions, withdrawals and lifecycles.","iaHubOutcomesContext":"Applications and TLDs have distinct stages: rejection before delegation is not the same as ending an active delegation.","iaExploreGovernanceEyebrow":"In depth · DNS geography and governance","iaExploreGovernanceTitle":"Who controls what on the Internet?","iaExploreGovernanceLead":"No single authority controls the Internet. DNS geography shows how infrastructure and influence are distributed; access controls explain how states and network operators can restrict connectivity; sanctions illustrate legal constraints that may affect ICANN and other DNS actors. Three related but distinct perspectives.","field04Section6Title":"A choice without a publicly stated reason","field04Section6Body":"ICANN's FAQs confirm that an eligible replacement can be chosen even when the original string is not in contention, without an explanation. After the TAMS attestation, both application records may still appear active: applicants can verify their selection in TAMS. An -R record, a CSV change or an Active/Deactivated label therefore cannot, alone, establish which choice was made, when, or why. Confirmed strings will be published on 17 November; undisclosed motives cannot be reconstructed.","field04SourceChoiceFAQ":"ICANN · FAQ: replacement without contention","field04SourceActiveFAQ":"ICANN · FAQ: both records may still appear active","field04Revision20261017":"17 October revision: discretion over replacements, no required rationale, and the limits of public APS status fields.","field05Number":"FIELD NOTE 05 · 17 OCTOBER 2026","field05Title":"Who may operate a TLD, and where will auction proceeds go?","field05Lead":"On 15 October ICANN named its applicant-screening provider. Two days earlier the Board chair previewed a discussion on potential auction proceeds. These are distinct questions, but they show that allocating a TLD involves more than assigning a name.","field05Status":"Announcements confirmed · no individual screening outcomes or auction decisions","field05MetaDescription":"ICANN selects PwC for applicant screening and previews a debate on auction proceeds: what is known and what remains open.","field05Section1Title":"15 October: the screening provider is selected","field05Section1Body":"ICANN announced an agreement with PricewaterhouseCoopers Advisory Services LLC (PwC) to carry out background screening for the 2026 round. A competitive procurement process began in November 2025. This is a procedural milestone, not an announcement that any individual application has been approved, rejected or screened.","field05Section2Title":"What is checked, and who is checked","field05Section2Body":"Background screening is mandatory during applicant evaluation. ICANN refers to organisations, individuals named in corporate account records, public information and Applicant Guidebook eligibility criteria: corporate standing, specified convictions or regulatory actions, and repeated cybersquatting. These criteria have defined thresholds. The existence of screening is not evidence of wrongdoing by an applicant.","field05Section3Title":"Different checks, no manufactured causal link","field05Section3Body":"Selecting a provider, conducting future background checks and performing an administrative check on a particular application are distinct actions. Sanctions regimes have separate legal bases. None of the official sources reviewed here links PwC's appointment to the administrative check on .wdo or to the gap between 1,616 paid applications and the 1,615 reported on Reveal Day. Coincidence is not an explanation.","field05Section4Title":"13 October: the question of potential auction value","field05Section4Body":"ICANN Board chair Tripti Sinha said the Board would begin discussing how to define uses of any potential 2026-round auction proceeds. No allocation or amount was approved in that statement. Separate from PwC's screening remit, this raises a public-interest question about a shared scarce resource: beyond who receives a contested string, what happens to any economic value produced by its allocation?","field05Section5Title":"Evidence to watch, rather than assume","field05Section5Body":"We will follow ICANN's published evaluation developments and actual Board decisions on auction proceeds. Appointing a service provider is not a screening outcome, and a planned discussion is not a resolution. These announcements establish no specific finding about .wdo, .lugano or any other applicant.","field05SourceAnnouncement":"ICANN · PwC announcement, 15 October","field05SourceCriteria":"ICANN · Screening eligibility criteria","field05SourcePurpose":"ICANN · Purpose of background screening","field05SourceWhen":"ICANN · When screening takes place","field05SourceBoard":"ICANN · Board chair's blog, 13 October","field05SourceWdo":"ICANN APS · .wdo administrative entry","field05Revision":"Note published on 17 October from official announcements and FAQs, with no applicant-specific outcome inferred.","insideBoardDate":"13 October 2026","insideBoardTitle":"Board previews discussion of auction proceeds","insideBoardBody":"Tripti Sinha says the Board will begin considering uses of potential 2026-round auction proceeds. A discussion is planned; no allocation is approved.","insideBoardSource":"ICANN · Board workshop preview","insideScreenDate":"15 October 2026","insideScreenTitle":"PwC chosen to screen applicants","insideScreenBody":"ICANN announces the provider for mandatory background checks. The appointment is not a finding on any individual applicant.","insideScreenSource":"ICANN · Official PwC announcement","insideChoiceSource":"ICANN · Replacement string rules","release0782":"v0.7.82: 13–15 October observations on potential auction proceeds and PwC screening; updated replacement-string methodology in Note 04 and sourced new Note 05. RR1/RR2 unchanged.","release0783":"v0.7.83: city-domain governance comparisons, use and sustainability indicators, and a stronger .lugano case. Sources, design proposals and external reception are distinguished; RR1/RR2 unchanged.","themeRoundOfficialApplicationsLabel":"applications in the official Reveal Day total","cemeterySub":"Terminations, transfers and withdrawals: four documented paths.","iaHomeOutcomesDesc":"CONTENTION, DISPUTES AND OUTCOMES","luganoSectionStatusTitle":"Status and sources","luganoSectionGovernanceTitle":"Governance decisions still to be made","luganoSectionUsesTitle":"Uses and criteria for results","luganoSectionRisksTitle":"Risks and next checks","contentionQuestionTitle":"Who gets a name when several applicants want it?","contentionQuestionLead":"When applications compete for the same string or for confusingly similar names, contention must be resolved before delegation. The cases and data show how rules and strategies change between rounds.","resourcesOfficialTitle":"Archived official data","resourcesOfficialIntro":"Copies of ICANN and IANA sources preserved by the project, with retrieval date and provenance. A row is not necessarily a separate application.","resourcesDerivedTitle":"Project analyses and datasets","resourcesDerivedIntro":"Tables, links and classifications built from the sources. Check their scope, denominator and method: project calculations are distinct from official ICANN aggregates.","resourcesArchivesTitle":"Releases and archives","resourcesArchivesIntro":"The current technical package, integrity checksums and historical versions. The RR1 and RR2 research releases have their own identities and DOIs.","apsAtlasDownloadHistorySub":"Derived CSV · 2,783 observations from the APS export retrieved on 11 October 2026; ID, status, stage and source checksum","periodPresent":"present","release0784":"Coherence audit: current downloads and editorial dates are aligned; the APS analysis uses official application IDs and separates string themes from applicant sectors; field-note reconciliations are documented; navigation and resources are clearer. The archived RR1/RR2 research releases remain unchanged.","explorerUniverseNote":"{total} names displayed: historical/technical catalogue and applications, deduplicated. This is not the number of delegated TLDs. Exports follow the selected filters.","explorerExport":"Export filtered results","explorerExportNote":"CSV · {count} names · all filtered results, not only the displayed rows","luganoContextDetails":"Reasons and precedents","luganoVisionDetails":"Scenarios and proposed path","resourcesFileRelease":"File from version {version}","resourcesRows":"{count} rows","cemeteryRootStatus":"Root status","cemeteryContractStatus":"Contract status","cemeteryVerified":"Verified on {date}","cemeteryRootAbsent":"Absent from the root","cemeteryRootActive":"Present in the root","cemeteryRootCaretaker":"In the root · continuity operator","cemeteryContractTerminated":"Registry agreement terminated","cemeteryContractNotice":"Termination notice · exit process under way","historicalEconomyTitle":"Exploratory comparison 2012–2026 · previous reconstruction","historicalEconomyNote":"This historical analysis mixes string themes and applicant sectors. It is preserved for reproducibility; the new APS layer above separates the two dimensions. Its percentages are not a uniform census of industries.","field18ReviewLabel":"Review of 18 October 2026","field18HistoricalObservation":"Observation recorded on 10 October 2026 — see the dated verification below.","field18ReconciliationTitle":"980 and 986: an exact comparison of the archived records","field18ReconciliationBody":"The official APS CSV archived on 11 October contains 2,783 public rows. Removing the 1,169 rows whose Application ID ends in -R leaves 1,614 distinct base application IDs. Six have Application Type “Existing gTLD with Variants”. Excluding those six leaves 1,608 applications and 980 distinct primary strings; including them gives 986 distinct primary strings. This is a project calculation from the public export, not a new aggregate published by ICANN.","field18EqualityBody":"The comparison uses the ASCII “Primary String” column after trimming whitespace and converting to lowercase. Its 980-name set is exactly equal to the “string” column of the frozen nTLDData CSV, using the same normalization. Neither set contains a name absent from the other: the result establishes identity of the names, beyond equality of the totals.","field18ExcludedCaption":"The six excluded existing-TLD variant applications","field18ApplicationID":"Application ID","field18PrimaryString":"Primary string (ASCII)","field18Applicant":"Applicant","field18SnapshotBoundary":"This dated verification advances the observation recorded on 10 October, which remains above. It reconciles the two local inventories; it does not establish that internet.Best used an identical extraction. It also does not explain the separate 1,616 → 1,615 change, for which no specific public cause has been verified.","field18CalendarTitle":"The public APS follows its own publication schedule","field18CalendarBody":"ICANN states on APS About that the public website is not updated between Reveal Day and String Confirmation Day. Later updates are regular, but are not made in real time; applicants must consult TAMS for the most up-to-date information about their own applications. The replacement-choice window closes on 21 October 2026 at 23:59 UTC. Publication of the confirmed strings and updated contention sets is scheduled for 17 November 2026.","field18CalendarLimit":"An unchanged public page during this interval is therefore not evidence that no internal processing has occurred. On 18 October the .wdo page still displays an administrative check. The public schedule neither establishes the outcome of that check nor promises that it will be resolved on 17 November.","field18ShortReconciliationTitle":"A separate count question now has a record-level answer","field18ShortReconciliationBody":"The archived official APS export contains 1,614 base applications, including six for variants of existing TLDs. Excluding those six yields 1,608 applications and 980 distinct primary strings, exactly the same set of names as in the frozen secondary inventory. Including them yields 986 primary strings. Field Note 04 lists the six IDs and the reproducible filter. This finding does not resolve the earlier 1,616 → 1,615 discrepancy.","field18FullReconciliationLink":"See the six IDs and the exact set comparison","field18SourceAbout":"ICANN APS — About and publication schedule","field18SourceExport":"ICANN APS — official public listing","field18SourceLocalExport":"Project archive — official APS listing, original fields preserved","field18SourceLocalStrings":"Project archive — frozen secondary string inventory","field18Revision":"Verified the exact 980/986 reconciliation against the archived official export and documented the APS publication schedule; original 10 October observations remain unchanged.","field18ReconciliationRevision":"Added the six application IDs, a reproducible filter and exact equality of the two 980-string sets; preserved the original observation and the separate unresolved 1,616 → 1,615 question.","insideReplacePublicScheduleBody":"Eligible applicants may choose a previously declared replacement string until 21 October 2026 at 23:59 UTC. ICANN schedules publication of the confirmed choices for 17 November. An unchanged public page in the meantime does not establish internal inactivity. No replacement action is presumed for .lugano.","insideConfirmPublicScheduleBody":"ICANN is scheduled to publish the confirmed strings and revised contention sets. APS is not updated between Reveal Day and this date; later updates will be regular, not in real time. Applicants should consult TAMS for their most current application information. This program milestone promises no individual outcome, including resolution of the .wdo administrative check.","sectorEducation":"Education & research","sectorEnergy":"Energy, industrial & materials","sectorTravel":"Travel & hospitality","sectorConsumer":"Consumer & lifestyle","sectorGaming":"Gaming, sports & entertainment","sectorProfessional":"Professional & business services","sectorFood":"Food & beverage","sectorAutomotive":"Automotive & mobility","sectorTelecom":"Telecom & connectivity","sectorRealEstate":"Real estate & construction","release0785":"Application fees by round, a cultural history of TLDs and a reproducible name census, with classification coverage and sources.","release0786":"Added gTLD counts by technical platform and two top-50 comparisons: website presence and delegated domains, with origins, dates, sources and distinct limitations.","researchPaperFormat":"HTML · v{paperVersion} · in English","release0787":"Audit revision: bibliographic dates, comparable regional application counts, qualified RSP and cultural inferences, mobile rankings and shareable filters; historical archives preserved.","periodPolicyFramework":"policy framework","release0788":"Expanded cultural research: explicit multilingual vocabulary, comparison of rules and new documented cases. Independent validation remains pending.","audienceScopeLabel":"Search in","audienceScopeAll":"Names, organisations and stories","audienceScopeNames":"Extension names only","audienceExact":"Exact match","audienceOther":"Other results","audienceSearchHelp":"An exact name match comes first. Extended search also includes organisations, countries and stories; choose “Names only” to narrow it.","audiencePlainStatus":"Documented status as of","audienceWhyInteresting":"Why this name matters","audienceBriefAi":"Anguilla’s country code. Its association with artificial intelligence illustrates how an existing name can acquire a new meaning.","audienceBriefCat":"A name for the Catalan linguistic and cultural community: a belonging that crosses state borders.","audienceBriefCom":"Created for commercial activities before the Web. It later became a symbol of online presence.","audienceBriefLugano":"An application by the City of Lugano in the 2026 round. It is not yet operational: approval, delegation and future uses remain to be established.","release0789":"Story-led entry, visual historical overview, compact rankings, name-only search and citation guide. Independent review materials prepared; human validation remains pending.","audienceRecordTools":"Languages, sharing and corrections","release0790":"A roughly three-minute introduction through four everyday situations with sources. Printable reader-study protocol and empty observation sheet in four languages; no human observations collected yet.","release0791":"v0.7.91: Economics reordered into six steps, from mechanisms to the 2026 analysis. Updated contents, transitions and heading hierarchy; existing cases, sources, data and deep links retained.","release0792":"Reading pathways across every chapter: narrative order, local contents, meaningful next links and direct research access. Content, sources, legacy links and scientific archives retained; no new research findings.","field03Section5Title":"23 October appointments: a confirmed outcome, not every answer","field03AppointmentDate":"23 October 2026 · official announcement","field03Section5Body":"ICANN announced that it had entered into agreements with Daniel Müller (chair), Abdelmohsen Sheha and Eran Sthoeger. They may file Limited Public Interest or Community objections on behalf of the public interest, without direction from ICANN or its Board. Their objection window begins on String Confirmation Day and lasts 111 calendar days, seven longer than the general public window. Verified fact: all three roles have been filled. Open question: the appointment announcement does not quantify how many people were affected by the earlier technical failures or establish the cause of the portal’s premature closure. The appointments do not, by themselves, demonstrate that the earlier procedural problems were fully remedied.","field03AppointmentRevision":"Recorded the three Independent Objectors, the chair and the 111-day filing window. Appointments are confirmed; individual effects of the June technical incidents are not established.","field03SourceAppointment":"ICANN · 23 October: three Independent Objectors appointed","release0793":"Independent Objector appointments: the sourced investigation now records the official outcome and outstanding procedural questions.","insideReplaceCompletedBody":"The replacement-string choice window closed on 21 October 2026 at 23:59 UTC. This does not reveal any applicant's final choice. ICANN schedules public string confirmation for 17 November. The 11 October APS -R records are alternatives, not additional applications.","insideQualityDate":"26 October 2026","insideQualityTitle":"Quality control of the 2026 evaluations: JAS appointed","insideQualityBody":"ICANN selected JAS Global Advisors to check that string, applicant, application and Community Priority evaluations follow the Applicant Guidebook consistently and impartially. This is program-wide procedural oversight, not a decision about any individual application, including .lugano.","insideQualitySource":"ICANN · 26 October provider announcement","nissayCaseState":"Preliminary non-transition decision · public consultation","nissayCaseFact":"Nippon Life Insurance Company notified ICANN on 8 September 2026 that it wished to terminate its .nissay registry agreement. On 26 October ICANN published a preliminary decision not to transition operation to a successor. Comments are invited from 26 October through 25 November 2026.","nissayCaseStatus":"As checked on 30 October, IANA still lists .nissay as delegated to Nippon Life Insurance Company. Neither a final determination nor completed DNS-root revocation has been documented.","nissayCaseReading":"This corporate gTLD provides a useful comparison with the municipal .helsinki case: a termination notice, a preliminary transition decision and removal from the root are different steps. These public sources do not establish economic motives.","nissaySourceIcann":"ICANN · registry termination procedure","nissaySourceIana":"IANA · .nissay delegation record","release0794":"Adds ICANN's 2026 evaluation quality-control provider, a source-dated .nissay termination case and the completed replacement-choice window. Editorial update only: 11 October APS and RR1/RR2 remain frozen.","release0795":"WikiLeaks (2008/2010): domain court order, withdrawn authoritative DNS, hosting and payment restrictions shown as separate control points.","release0796":"Seven Internet-access control case studies presented chronologically (2008–2026) across four languages; unchanged evidence and separate WikiLeaks episodes.","release0797":"Two 4 November 2026 stakeholder perspectives, BRS Media on community-TLD outcomes and INTA on private auctions: Field Notes and Contention, with no changes to APS data.","breadcrumbLabel":"Breadcrumb","apsAtlasStandalone":"Open the atlas on its own page","explorerUniverseNoteOne":"{total} name displayed: historical/technical catalogue and applications, deduplicated. This is not the number of delegated TLDs. Exports follow the selected filters.","explorerExportNoteOne":"CSV · {count} name · all filtered results, not only the displayed rows","release0798":"Technical audit: more reliable publication checks, current citation metadata, localized accessibility controls, lighter HTML and complete archive protection. Scientific evidence and archived releases are preserved.","release0799":"Accessible mobile results, earlier search, shareable Atlas filters and repaired official-source links. Scientific data and archives remain unchanged.","release07100":"Visible focus, accessible skip link and readable mobile metrics. Data unchanged.","release07101":"Home and Economics: clearer introductions and distinct headings. Data and sources preserved.","release07102":"Intercap corrected.","release07103":"New editorial design, clearer navigation and more comfortable reading on mobile. Data and sources preserved.","release07104":"Keep payment restrictions within the WikiLeaks case.","release07105":"Citable guides, TLD profiles and an AI-agent catalog.","release07106":"A new homepage explaining Internet names: a clear introduction, an illustrated domain and focused pathways.","release07107":"Economics becomes an introduction with four complete chapters: TLD adoption, economic cases, portfolios and contention, and the 2026 round. Text, sources, rankings, filters and established links are preserved in all four languages.","release07108":"Full-surface audit remediation: dated snapshots, consistent page types, shared presentation modules, stronger metadata, source-link monitoring and deployment hygiene, with archived research preserved.","release0747":"Cross-round analysis: historical cases and explicit 2000, 2004, 2012 and 2026 context.","release0748":"Consolidated publication metadata and the post-Reveal Day record; research corpora unchanged.","release0749":"Portfolio economics: applicant strategies, counting units and the documented Aruba case.","release0750":"Rebalanced portfolio economics and moved Aruba into a compact methodological note.","release0751":"Preserved the Reveal Day string inventory locally and removed visitor-time external requests.","release0752":"Expanded the .lugano case study, with application facts, public-interest rationale and conditional outcomes.","release0753":"Added sector analysis across the 2012 and 2026 rounds, with coverage and denominator disclosures.","release0754":"Clarified official ICANN aggregates and the separately frozen primary-string snapshot.","release0755":"Introduced Research Release RR1, a research and citation section and a working-paper draft.","release0756":"Centralized publication metadata and added a dedicated working-paper entry.","release0757":"Recorded the archived RR1 release and its persistent Zenodo DOI.","release0758":"Added the RR1 DOI to research entries and the footer; strengthened release checks.","resourcesRowsOne":"{count} row","strangeCaseLabel":"documented case","meowCrowdfundingLink":"Read the .meow crowdfunding story","meowSearchTerms":"crowdfunding crowd funding community cat cats","field03CalendarTitle":"Calendar of general public objections","field03CalendarBody":"ICANN scheduled the general public objection period for 17 November 2026 to 16 March 2027. This window already includes the pause from 19 December 2026 at 01:00 UTC to 4 January 2027 at 16:00 UTC.","field03CalendarLimit":"These dates describe the general public objection window; they do not specify the separate deadline for Independent Objectors.","field03CalendarSource":"ICANN · 2026 round milestones · 29 September 2026","release07109":"Added a dedicated legal and procedural layer inside Conflicts & outcomes, separating formal objections, government input, standing, providers, appeals and Registry Voluntary Commitments from the historical case stories.","release07110":"Closed the post-Legal audit: corrected the remaining light-theme contrast defect, aligned release metadata and chronology, made source-health versioning dynamic, sharpened 2026 legal timing and terminology, and removed duplicated procedure from Disputed Dots while preserving all frozen research evidence.","disputesLegalBridgeTitle":"The procedure now lives in one place","disputesLegalBridgeBody":"Formal objections, standing, providers, deadlines and appeals are explained in Rights, objections & remedies. Disputed Dots stays focused on what happened in individual cases.","disputesLegalBridgeCta":"Read Rights, objections & remedies","release07111":"Localized the Legal layer's editorial badges in all four languages, removed duplicated source chips and added a release-wide regression guard against generic English labels leaking into non-English pages.","release07112":"Closed the final pre-String-Confirmation audit: localized Research entry pages, separated the working-paper scholarly revision from live technical synchronization, clarified build provenance semantics."}};
